ecome dark):\n- index.html: \"+ Read Our Full Story\" (131)\n- programs.html: 7 \"Pay with Stripe\" buttons\n- evaluation.html: Back ×3 (138, 187, 202)\n\n**Already `btn-dark`**: the 5 CTA buttons + reference (index 406).\n\n**`btn-crimson`**: not used anywhere (0 matches). Leave definition.\n\n**`whatsapp-btn`**: 3 uses (evaluation, contact, index) — keep green (brand button).\n\nSo the total inventory is confirmed. The CSS approach covers ALL of these in one edit:\n- Redefine `.btn-primary` and `.btn-ghost` to the dark theme (black bg, white border, white text, sweep ::before, gold-border hover).\n- Update `.nav-links .btn-primary` overrides (they force gold bg on hover with !important).\n\nNo HTML changes needed at all. \n\nNow let me implement the CSS edits. I have the exact current CSS text from the first read. Let me make the replacements.\n\nReplacement 1 — the button blocks:\nOld:\n```\n.btn-primary {\n  background: var(--accent-gold);\n  color: var(--bg-deep);\n}\n\n.btn-primary:hover {\n  background: var(--accent-gold-light);\n  transform: translateY(-2px);\n  box-shadow: 0 0 40px rgba(201, 162, 39, 0.15);\n}\n\n.btn-ghost {\n  background: transparent;\n  color: var(--accent-gold);\n  border: 1px solid var(--accent-gold);\n}\n\n.btn-ghost:hover {\n  background: var(--accent-gold);\n  color: var(--bg-deep);\n}\n```\n\nNew:\n```\n/* ── Unified Dark Theme (primary & ghost follow the same style) ── */\n.btn-primary,\n.btn-ghost {\n  background: #000000;\n  color: #ffffff;\n  border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.15);\n  position: relative;\n  overflow: hidden;\n  z-index: 1;\n}\n\n.btn-primary::before,\n.btn-ghost::before {\n  content: '';\n  position: absolute;\n  top: 0;\n  left: -100%;\n  width: 100%;\n  height: 100%;\n  background: linear-gradient(90deg,\n      transparent 0%,\n      rgba(201, 162, 39, 0.25) 45%,\n      rgba(201, 162, 39, 0.5) 50%,\n      rgba(201, 162, 39, 0.25) 55%,\n      transparent 100%);\n  transition: left 0.6s ease;\n  z-index: -1;\n}\n\n.btn-primary:hover,\n.btn-ghost:hover {\n  color: #ffffff;\n  border-color: var(--accent-gold);\n  transform: translateY(-2px);\n  box-shadow: 0 0 30px rgba(201, 162, 39, 0.2);\n}\n\n.btn-primary:hover::before,\n.btn-ghost:hover::before {\n  left: 100%;\n}\n```\n\nReplacement 2 — nav overrides:\nOld:\n```\n.nav-links .btn-primary {\n  color: var(--bg-deep) !important;\n}\n\n.nav-links .btn-primary:hover,\n.nav-links .btn-primary.active {\n  color: var(--bg-deep) !important;\n  background: var(--accent-gold-light);\n}\n```\nNew:\n```\n.nav-links .btn-primary {\n  color: #ffffff !important;\n}\n\n.nav-links .btn-primary:hover,\n.nav-links .btn-primary.active {\n  color: #ffffff !important;\n  background: #000000;\n  border-color: var(--accent-gold);\n}\n```\n\nWait — careful with replacement 2's old string: it must match exactly including whitespace. From the read:\n\n```\n.nav-links .btn-primary {\n  color: var(--bg-deep) !important;\n}\n\n.nav-links .btn-primary:hover,\n.nav-links .btn-primary.active {\n  color: var(--bg-deep) !important;\n  background: var(--accent-gold-light);\n}\n```\n\nYes. But note `.nav-links a` sets color via `color: var(--text-body)` — not !important, so `.btn-primary` (0,1,0)... wait: `.nav-links .btn-primary` is (0,2,0) with !important — wins. And `.nav-links a` (0,1,1). OK.\n\nAlso, `.nav-links a:hover` sets `color: var(--accent-gold)` — with `.nav-links .btn-primary:hover` (0,2,1? no — `.nav-links .btn-primary:hover` = 2 classes + 1 pseudo = (0,3,0)? Classes: .nav-links, .btn-primary = 2 classes; :hover = 1 pseudo-class. Total (0, 2+1, 0)? Specificity counts: classes AND pseudo-classes together = (0,3,0). vs `.nav-links a:hover` = (0,2,1). The nav rule wins + !important. Good.\n\nNow — one more check: `btn-ghost` had `border: 1px solid var(--accent-gold)` before; new one gives white border. Good.\n\nAlso the `.btn` base: `border: none;` — but `.btn-primary`/`.btn-ghost` set border after? Order in file: `.btn` is defined earlier 