utton on the site now follows the same dark theme you approved.\n\n## What I changed (1 file: `css/style.css`)\n\nI redefined `.btn-primary` and `.btn-ghost` to share the exact same dark style as `.btn-dark` — black background, white text, thin white border, and the gold sweep animation on hover. Since **every** button on the site uses one of these three classes, this converted all of them at once with zero HTML edits:\n\n- ✅ **Nav \"Free Evaluation\" button** — all 7 pages (previously gold)\n- ✅ **6 service card buttons** — services page (View Signal Plans, Apply for Account Management, etc.)\n- ✅ **7 \"Enrol Now — PayPal\" + 7 \"Pay with Stripe\"** — programs page\n- ✅ **\"Explore Our Programs\" + \"+ Read Our Full Story\"** — homepage\n- ✅ **Form buttons** — evaluation page (Next Step, Back, Review & Submit, Request My Assessment) and contact page (Send Message)\n- ✅ The 6 already-dark CTA buttons stay the same\n\nI also updated the nav-specific hover rule so the Free Evaluation button keeps white text instead of reverting to dark-on-gold.\n\n**I left these as-is** (deliberately): the green **Chat on WhatsApp** buttons (brand-recognizable — say the word if you want those dark too), and the small functional controls like testimonial arrows and FAQ accordion toggles, which aren't really \"buttons\" in the CTA sense.\n\n**Verified:** in the live preview, all 8 buttons on the services page render black with white text and the sweep animation is active on each — confirmed via computed styles, and no JS depends on the old colors."},{"kind":"tool","id":"0j42ygfhzd4","toolName":"suggest_prompts","input":{"prompts":[{"prompt":"The dark buttons look great — but the green WhatsApp buttons stand out now. Should I restyle those to match the dark theme too?","label":"Darken the WhatsApp buttons too"},{"prompt":"Let me check the programs page (Enrol Now / Pay with Stripe buttons) and the evaluation form buttons in the preview to make sure they all look right.","label":"Check programs & form buttons"},{"prompt":"The buttons are all dark now — want me to make the gold sweep animation a bit more visible so the hover effect really pops?","label":"Boost the sweep animation"}]}},{"kind":"changes","id":"p16-711b6b65","files":[{"path":"REALITY-FOREX-TRADING-\\css\\style.css","status":"modified","lockfile":false,"binary":false,"adds":38,"dels":17,"patch":"Index: REALITY-FOREX-TRADING-\\css\\style.css\n===================================================================\n--- REALITY-FOREX-TRADING-\\css\\style.css\tbefore\n+++ REALITY-FOREX-TRADING-\\css\\style.css\tafter\n@@ -139,26 +139,46 @@\n   overflow: hidden;\r\n }\r\n \r\n-.btn-primary {\r\n-  background: var(--accent-gold);\r\n-  color: var(--bg-deep);\r\n+/* ── Unified Dark Button — primary & ghost share the same theme ── */\r\n+.btn-primary,\r\n+.btn-ghost {\r\n+  background: #000000;\r\n+  color: #ffffff;\r\n+  border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.15);\r\n+  position: relative;\r\n+  overflow: hidden;\r\n+  z-index: 1;\r\n }\r\n \r\n-.btn-primary:hover {\r\n-  background: var(--accent-gold-light);\r\n-  transform: translateY(-2px);\r\n-  box-shadow: 0 0 40px rgba(201, 162, 39, 0.15);\r\n+.btn-primary::before,\r\n+.btn-ghost::before {\r\n+  content: '';\r\n+  position: absolute;\r\n+  top: 0;\r\n+  left: -100%;\r\n+  width: 100%;\r\n+  height: 100%;\r\n+  background: linear-gradient(90deg,\r\n+      transparent 0%,\r\n+      rgba(201, 162, 39, 0.25) 45%,\r\n+      rgba(201, 162, 39, 0.5) 50%,\r\n+      rgba(201, 162, 39, 0.25) 55%,\r\n+      transparent 100%);\r\n+  transition: left 0.6s ease;\r\n+  z-index: -1;\r\n }\r\n \r\n-.btn-ghost {\r\n-  background: transparent;\r\n-  color: var(--accent-gold);\r\n-  border: 1px solid var(--accent-gold);\r\n+.btn-primary:hover,\r\n+.btn-ghost:hover {\r\n+  color: #ffffff;\r\n+  border-color: var(--accent-gold);\r\n+  transform: translateY(-2px);\r\n+  box-shadow: 0 0 30px rgba(201, 162, 39, 0.2);\r\n }\r\n \r\n-.btn-ghost:hover {\r\n-  background: var(--accent-gold);\r\n-  color: var(--bg-deep);\r\n+.btn-primary:hover::before,\r\n+.btn-ghost:hover::before {\r\n+  left: 100%;\r\n }\r\n \r\n .btn-crimson {\r\n@@ -289,13 +309,14 @@\n }\r\n \r\n .nav-links .btn-primary {\r\n-  color: var(--bg-deep) !important;\r\n+  color: #ffffff !important;\r\n }\r\n \r\n .nav-links .btn-primary:hover,\r\n .nav-links .btn-primary.active {\r\n-  color: var(--bg-deep) !important;\r\n-  background: var(--accent-gold-light);\r\n+  color: #ffffff !important;\r\n+  background: #000000;\r\n+  border-color: var(--accent-gold);\r\n }\r\n \r\n .nav-links .btn-primary::after {\r\n","tooLarge":false}]}],"ts":1785938725352},{"role":"user","parts":[{"kind":"text","text":"🎯 Purpose of the Careers Page\n\nThe page has four goals:\n\nRecruit talented people.\nShow visitors that Reality FX is a growing company.\nBuild credibility with potential clients.\nCollect candidate information for future hiring.\n\nEven if you aren't hiring every month, you can keep a talent pool ready.\n\nOverall Structure\nNavbar\n\nHero\n\nWhy Join Reality FX\n\nOur Culture\n\nOpen Positions\n\nHiring Process\n\nBenefits\n\nFrequently Asked Questions\n\nApply CTA\n\nFooter\n1. Hero Section\n\nThis is the first thing candidates see.\n\nHeading\n\nBuild Your Career With Reality FX\n\nSubtitle\n\nJoin a team passionate about financial markets, innovation, education, and helping traders reach their full potential.\n\nButtons\n\nPrimary\n\nView Open Positions\n\nSecondary\n\nSubmit General Application\n\nBackground image:\n\nImagine a premium trading office with multiple monitors displaying market charts, a modern workspace, or a sleek dark-themed trading environment.\n\n2. Why Join Reality FX\n\nThis section explains why someone should work here.\n\nInstead of paragraphs, use elegant cards.\n\nCard 1\n\n📈\n\nCareer Growth\n\nCard 2\n\n🌍\n\nRemote Opportunities\n\nCard 3\n\n🎓\n\nContinuous Learning\n\nCard 4\n\n💰\n\nPerformance-Based Rewards\n\nCard 5\n\n🤝\n\nSupportive Team\n\nCard 6\n\n🚀\n\nWork on Real Financial Projects\n\n3. Our Culture\n\nThis is where you explain who Reality FX is.\n\nFor example:\n\nAt Reality FX, we believe in discipline, continuous improvement, integrity, and data-driven decision making. We value people who enjoy solving problems, learning new skills, and contributing to a collaborative environment.\n\nAlongside this, display four core values:\n\nIntegrity\nExcellence\nInnovation\nDiscipline\n4. Open Positions (Main Feature)\n\nThis is the heart of the page.\n\nInstead of a boring list...\n\nCreate beautiful job cards.\n\nEach card contains:\n\nJob Title\n\nDepartment\n\nEmployment Type\n\nLocation\n\nExperience\n\nShort Description\n\nSalary\n\n(if applicable)\n\nSkills Required\n\nApply Button\n\nExample\n\nTechnical Analyst\n\nDepartment\n\nTrading\n\nLocation\n\nRemote\n\nEmployment\n\nFull-Time\n\nExperience\n\n2+ Years\n\nRequirements\n\nTechnical Analysis\nMarket Structure\nPrice Action\nMetaTrader\nTradingView\n\nButton\n\nApply Now\n\nRepeat this layout for every position.\n\nSuggested Positions\nTrading Department\n\nTechnical Analyst\n\nFundamental Analyst\n\nRisk Manager\n\nJunior Trader\n\nSenior Trader\n\nProp Trader\n\nTrading Mentor\n\nMarket Research Analyst\n\nPortfolio Analyst\n\nTrade Support Specialist\n\nEducation Department\n\nForex Instructor\n\nCourse Coordinator\n\nStudent Success Officer\n\nCurriculum Developer\n\nTrading Coach\n\nMarketing\n\nDigital Marketing Specialist\n\nSEO Specialist\n\nSocial Media Manager\n\nGraphic Designer\n\nVideo Editor\n\nContent Creator\n\nCopywriter\n\nSales\n\nSales Consultant\n\nClient Success Manager\n\nBusiness Development Representative\n\nPartnership Manager\n\nTechnology\n\nFrontend Developer\n\nBackend Developer\n\nUI/UX Designer\n\nQA Tester\n\nWeb Developer\n\nIT Support\n\nCybersecurity Analyst\n\nData Analyst\n\nAdministration\n\nHR Assistant\n\nRecruitment Officer\n\nFinance Officer\n\nOperations Manager\n\nVirtual Assistant\n\nOffice Administrator\n\n5. Hiring Process\n\nTurn it into a timeline.\n\nApplication\n\n↓\n\nInitial Review\n\n↓\n\nSkills Assessment\n\n↓\n\nInterview\n\n↓\n\nFinal Decision\n\n↓\n\nWelcome to Reality FX\n\nSmall explanation under every step.\n\n6. Employee Benefits\n\nUse icons.\n\nRemote Work\n\nProfessional Development\n\nFlexible Hours\n\nPerformance Bonuses\n\nMentorship\n\nCertification Opportunities\n\nNetworking\n\nCareer Advancement\n\n7. FAQ\n\nExamples\n\nCan I work remotely?\n\nYes.\n\nCan beginners apply?\n\nDepends on the role.\n\nIs trading experience required?\n\nOnly for trading positions.\n\nHow long does hiring take?\n\nNormally 1–3 weeks.\n\nCan international applicants apply?\n\nYes.\n\n8. General Application\n\nSometimes people won't fit any current role.\n\nHave a section saying:\n\nDon't see the perfect role?\n\nSubmit your CV anyway.\n\nButton\n\nJoin Our Talent Pool\n\nThis goes to the general application form.\n\n9. Footer CTA\n\nEnd with a premium call to action.\n\nReady to build your future with Reality FX?\n\nLarge button\n\nApply Today\nForms You'll Need\n\nRather than creating a separate form for every role, I'd keep it streamlined with two forms:\n\n1. General Application\n\nCollects:\n\nFull name\nEmail\nPhone number\nCountry\nCV upload (or resume link if using a service without file uploads)\nLinkedIn (optional)\nCover letter\nAreas of interest\nYears of experience\n2. Job Application\n\nIncludes everything above, plus:\n\nPosition applying for (pre-filled from the job card)\nExpected salary (optional)\nEarliest start date\nPortfolio or GitHub (where relevant)\nFuture Enhancements\n\nOnce you migrate to WordPress and add a member portal, candidates could:\n\nCreate an account.\nSave favourite job listings.\nTrack application status (e.g. Submitted → Under Review → Interview → Offered).\nReceive notifications when new positions are posted.\nUpdate their CV without submitting a new application.\nView previous applications and interview history.\nMy Recommendation\n\nDon't think of this as just a \"Careers\" page—think of it as a Recruitment Hub. A well-designed recruitment hub not only helps you hire talent, it also signals to clients and students that Reality FX is an established, growing organization. Even before you have dozens of employees, presenting a structured hiring process can significantly strengthen your brand's credibility."}],"ts":1785939158531},{"role":"assistant","parts":[{"kind":"reasoning","id":"p1-42b36ae1","text":"The user has shared a detailed spec for a Careers page. They want me to build it. The message is a comprehensive spec — they clearly want me to create this page. The spec is detailed:\n\n1. Hero: \"Build Your Career With Reality FX\" + subtitle + 2 buttons (View Open Positions, Submit General Application), background image of premium trading office\n2. Why Join Reality FX — 6 cards\n3. Our Culture — text + 4 core values\n4. Open Positions — job cards (Technical Analyst example) with many suggested positions across departments\n5. Hiring Process — timeline (6 steps)\n6. Employee Benefits — 8 benefits with icons\n7. FAQ — 5 questions\n8. General Application — \"Join Our Talent Pool\"\n9. Footer CTA — \"Apply Today\"\n10. Two forms: General Application + Job Application\n\nI notice the existing site already has some careers-related CSS (`careers-hero`, `job-listing`, `job-meta`, `job-requirements`, `careers-form` in style.css) — so there was probably a careers.html at some point, or it was planned. There's no careers.html in the file tree though. I'll create a new careers.html following the site's existing conventions (nav, footer, main.js, style.css).\n\nAlso, the existing CSS has `.careers-hero`, `.job-listing`, `.job-meta`, `.job-requirements`, `.careers-form` styles already defined. Great — I can reuse those and add new styles for sections like values, timeline, benefits, FAQ.\n\nGiven the scope, this is a big task. Let me plan:\n\n1. Look at an existing page (e.g., our-services.html or why-choose-us.html) to copy the exact nav/footer structure and conventions.\n2. Look at main.js to see what JS features exist (mobile menu, reveal, accordion, etc.).\n3. Create careers.html with all sections.\n4. Add CSS for new components (values cards, timeline, benefits grid, job cards polish, FAQ).\n5. Add the page to the nav of all pages? Hmm — the user didn't ask to add a nav link. Should I? The nav currently has: Home, Why Us, Services, Industries, Programs, Contact. Adding \"Careers\" to nav on all 7 pages would be consistent... but the user didn't explicitly ask. The spec says \"Navbar\" as part of the page structure — meaning the page should have the navbar, not necessarily add Careers to other pages' navbars. I'll create the page with the existing nav (consistent), and NOT modify other pages' navs unless asked. Actually — hmm, should I add a Careers link? The user's spec includes navbar as part of the page. I'll keep the standard nav but maybe ask/suggest adding the link. Better to not touch other pages without being asked. I could mention it as a follow-up suggestion.\n\nLet me first read the existing files to understand conventions:\n- Read why-choose-us.html fully (or our-services.html) to get nav + footer + main.js usage.\n- Read main.js.\n- Read the careers CSS portion (already have from style.css read: `.careers-hero`, `.job-listing`, `.job-meta`, `.job-requirements`, `.careers-form`).\n\nI already have the full style.css. The careers CSS exists:\n```css\n.careers-hero { min-height: 50vh; padding-top: 120px; display: flex; align-items: center; text-align: center; }\n.careers-hero h1 { margin-bottom: 1rem; }\n.job-listing { background: linear-gradient(145deg, #111, #0a0a0a); border: 1px solid var(--glass-border); border-radius: 16px; padding: 2.5rem; margin-bottom: 1.5rem; transition: var(--transition); }\n.job-listing:hover { border-color: var(--border-subtle); transform: translateY(-3px); }\n.job-listing h3 { color: var(--accent-gold); margin-bottom: 0.5rem; font-size: 1.3rem; }\n.job-meta { display: flex; gap: 1.5rem; margin-bottom: 1.5rem; flex-wrap: wrap; }\n.job-meta span { font-size: 0.8rem; color: var(--text-muted); text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.1em; }\n.job-listing p { margin-bottom: 1.5rem; font-size: 0.9rem; }\n.job-requirements { list-style: none; margin: 1rem 0 1.5rem; }\n.job-requirements li { padding: 0.3rem 0; font-size: 0.9rem; color: var(--text-muted); display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.6rem; }\n.job-requirements li::before { content: '→'; color: var(--accent-gold); }\n.careers-form { background: var(--glass-bg); border: 1px solid var(--glass-border); border-radius: 16px; padding: 3rem; }\n.careers-form h2 { margin-bottom: 2rem; }\n```\n\nThese classes will be reused. I'll need to add CSS for:\n- `.values-grid` / value cards\n- `.timeline` (hiring process)\n- `.benefit-grid` / benefit items\n- FAQ (can reuse `.accordion-*` classes already in style.css!)\n- `.why-card` (why join cards) — can reuse `.card` or `.industry-card` grid.\n\nReuse existing: `.card-grid`, `.card`, `.card-icon`, `.accordion-item/.accordion-header/.accordion-icon/.accordion-body`, `.stats-grid`?, `.section-header`, `.eyebrow`, `.btn`, `.cta-section`, `.whatsapp-btn`, `.form-group`, `.form-row`, `.btn-dark`.\n\nNow — how many job positions should I include? The spec lists ~40 suggested positions across 6 departments. That's a LOT of job cards. Including all 40 would make the page enormous. The spec says \"Repeat this layout for every position\" with \"Suggested Positions\". I think a reasonable approach: include a representative set covering each department (maybe 2-4 per department, ~12-16 jobs total) with a note, or include all? Hmm. The user pasted the spec as instructions to build the page. \"Suggested Positions\" — they suggest including them. But 40 cards is excessive and mostly repetitive. \n\nI'll make a judgment call: include a solid representative set — all departments represented (Trading, Education, Marketing, Sales, Technology, Administration), about 12-15 job cards with realistic details. That demonstrates the layout while keeping the page usable. I can mention that I can add more if they want. Actually — let me include a good number: maybe 3 from Trading (Technical Analyst, Fundamental Analyst, Risk Manager), 2 Education (Forex Instructor, Trading Coach), 2 Marketing (Digital Marketing Specialist, Content Creator), 2 Sales (Sales Consultant, Client Success Manager), 2 Technology (Frontend Developer, UI/UX Designer), 1-2 Administration (Operations Manager, Virtual Assistant). That's ~12. Good balance.\n\nForms: The spec wants two forms — General Application and Job Application (with position pre-filled). I'll build one careers page with:\n- \"Don't see the perfect role? Join Our Talent Pool\" section → general application form\n- Job application: each job card's \"Apply Now\" button can link to the general form with a hidden \"position\" field pre-filled? Since static HTML, I can make Apply Now buttons link to `#apply` with the position name via query param, and JS pre-fills the position select. That's a nice touch: `<a href=\"careers.html#apply?position=Technical Analyst\">`. Actually query params before hash don't work that way. Standard: `careers.html#apply` then read `?position=` — actually URL format is `careers.html?position=Technical%20Analyst#apply`. JS can read query param and prefill. Let me do that — simple JS in the page or in main.js. Keep it in the page's own script to avoid touching main.js? Better: add a small script block in careers.html. Or add to main.js since that's the shared JS. I'll add a small inline script in careers.html for the query-param prefill and form interactions (simple). Actually the site has main.js for shared behaviors (nav scroll, reveal, accordion). Let me check main.js first.\n\nLet me read why-choose-us.html (full), contact.html (for form structure), evaluation.html (for multi-step form), and main.js. These give me the conventions to replicate: nav markup, footer markup, contact-strip, what'sapp, and how forms are built (contact form + evaluation form), and the toast notification usage (contact form seems to trigger a toast via JS in main.js).\n\nI need to be careful about file size limits when reading. index.html was huge due to base64. Let me read why-choose-us.html and our-services.html fully — they seemed moderate. And main.js.\n\nPlan:\n1. Read why-choose-us.html, our-services.html (structure), main.js, contact.html (form pattern).\n2. Write careers.html (big file) following conventions.\n3. Append CSS for new components to style.css.\n4. Register preview, inject CSS, screenshot, verify.\n5. Code review.\n\nLet me start by reading the needed files. Read why-choose-us.html fully (it's ~260+ lines, moderate), our-services.html (~275 lines), main.js, contact.html (~140 lines).\n\nActually, to be efficient: read our-services.html (has full nav + footer + cards), contact.html (form), main.js (JS behaviors). That should be enough. Let me also glance at why-choose-us.html for the hero CTA pattern — actually I have enough from code searches.\n\nLet me read those files.","open":false,"collapse":"preview"},{"kind":"ad","id":"p2-42b36ae1","ad":{"adText":"Ensure your website's new theme and buttons are inclusive with Accessibly's user-activated accessibility features.","title":"Meet Accessibility Standards—No Coding Required","cta":"Improve Accessibility","url":"https://dash.accessiblyapp.com/register?ref=zach46","favicon":"https://icons.duckduckgo.com/ip3/dash.accessiblyapp.com.ico","clickUrl":"https://api.trygravity.ai/track/click?p=gAAAAABqc0T9aDl7vHJFzYxX4BO_HPIoJOzkiKvW-cUnz_2Yy2hoLixZFGmjwDoE-wJIMSenPOBoc2u18WmpnZCmgS3r8Z3gSrYNFXtiCW13tLMWunAk2RMpzm7rmxQ8dIeVL1rP5lkG3wL1CiBL6wxh1XQQh6EJPha--VRF2mc_3BOzC9hOtjbDJSWCApzOK3lXGu5kyjqBbvNSGRSh_PnydVREHncleTF53FJLrzfQwTdH-3T2gvi0v_x1zsB14nTwYtFqXTxJQ6Hk7dPWAronQwO38nhbQHyPlgDUh5yjI8DY8pIe2ZgxAFVd7AahMnz7NL