nsparent 92%)` — the radial mask fades edges all around, but the bottom-center might still show the dust since radial fades only at the extreme edge (92%).\n- I can use a more targeted mask: `radial-gradient(ellipse 70% 60% at 50% 45%, black 60%, transparent 85%)` or add a linear-gradient mask at the bottom: `linear-gradient(to bottom, black 70%, transparent 100%)` combined with the radial. CSS mask supports multiple layers!\n\nActually — a good approach: combine two masks:\n```css\nmask-image:\n  radial-gradient(ellipse 75% 70% at 50% 42%, black 55%, transparent 92%),\n  linear-gradient(to top, transparent 0%, black 12%);\n```\nWait, multiple mask layers composite (they're OR'd by default — actually mask layers combine by alpha addition? By default mask-composite is add). Hmm, mask compositing: multiple layers are composited with 'add' by default, meaning the most transparent wins? No — actually multiple mask layers: the final mask alpha is the... Let me think. CSS mask layers are like background layers; each layer's alpha contributes. With default `mask-composite: add`, the mask values are added (clamped), meaning a pixel is visible if ANY layer is opaque. That's not what I want — I want AND behavior (visible only where BOTH opaque). For that I'd need `mask-composite: intersect` (supported in modern browsers with -webkit prefix as -webkit-mask-composite: intersect; older syntax: -webkit-mask-composite: source-in).\n\nThis is getting complex and browser-support-risky. Simpler approach: just adjust the single radial mask to fade the bottom more. A single elliptical mask centered slightly above center:\n```css\nmask-image: radial-gradient(ellipse 72% 62% at 50% 44%, black 55%, transparent 90%);\n```\nThe ellipse is narrower vertically (62%), centered at 44% — so the bottom fades out before reaching the bull's hooves, blending the dust into the background. The top gets some fade too (bull's head/horns should stay mostly visible — 72% horizontal, centered, with 55% solid → the top of the bull's head at ~10-15% height would be in the fade zone... hmm.\n\nActually let me think about the bull image composition. It's a bull illustration (charging bull, like the Wall Street bull). The dust/mud is at the bottom around the hooves. The bull's body occupies the middle. The mask needs to:\n- Keep the bull's body/head visible (center + upper)\n- Fade the bottom (hooves/dust) into the background\n\nA radial ellipse centered a bit high with stronger bottom fade: `radial-gradient(ellipse 80% 65% at 50% 40%, black 55%, transparent 92%)` — at the bottom (y=100%), distance from center is large → fully transparent. At the top (y=0%), the fade would also kick in (since 65% vertical radius) — the bull's horns might fade slightly. That could look OK (fading into the darkness above) or cut the horns.\n\nAlternative: use two-layer mask with the `-webkit-mask-composite: source-in` trick:\n```css\n-webkit-mask-image:\n  radial-gradient(ellipse at center, black 45%, transparent 92%),\n  linear-gradient(to top, transparent 0%, black 18%);\n-webkit-mask-composite: source-in;\nmask-image:\n  radial-gradient(ellipse at center, black 45%, transparent 92%),\n  linear-gradient(to top, transparent 0%, black 18%);\nmask-composite: intersect;\n```\nsource-in = keep only where BOTH layers are opaque → the bottom 18% fades fully, the rest uses the radial fade. This is the clean solution and supported in modern browsers (Chrome/Safari with -webkit-, Firefox with standard mask-composite: intersect — actually Firefox supports mask-composite: intersect? mask-composite is supported in Firefox 53+... values: add, subtract, intersect, exclude. Chrome supports the standard `mask-composite` since 120ish? Chrome historically only supported -webkit-mask-composite with weird values. To be safe, provide both properties. If unsupported, the linear-gradient layer alone would apply (fading bottom 18% to transparent — which is still a decent fallback since the dust is at the very bottom).\n\nHmm wait — with fallback behavior if mask-composite isn't supported: the multiple mask layers would 'add' — meaning visible where either is opaque — the linear layer fades bottom to transparent, so bottom still shows via radial layer? With 'add', the composite alpha = sum clamped... actually 'add' means the layers are combined by addition (alphas add). The radial layer at the very bottom is already ~transparent (92%→transparent), so combined ≈ still transparent-ish. The 18% fade would darken the bottom. Honestly the safest simple approach:\n\n**Simplest robust**: single radial mask, but shift the ellipse's center up and reduce the vertical radius so the bottom fades more aggressively than the top:\n\n```css\nmask-image: radial-gradient(ellipse 85% 68% at 50% 42%, black 58%, transparent 92%);\n```\n\nLet me reason: center at (50%, 42%). Bottom at (50%, 100%): relative distance = 58/68 = 0.85 of vertical radius → with stops black 58%, transparent 92%, at 85% the alpha is between... gradient from 58% solid to 92% transparent; 85% is 85% through the transition → alpha ≈ 1 - (85-58)/(92-58) = 1 - 0.79 = 0.21 → mostly transparent. Good — the bottom 8%+ (where dust sits) fades out.\n\nTop at (50%, 0%): relative distance = 42/68 = 0.62 → alpha ≈ 1 - (62-58)/34 = 1 - 0.12 = 0.88 → still quite visible. Good, horns keep.\n\nHmm, but wait — do I know where the dust is exactly in the image? The user says \"on the bulls feet/legs, the mud/dust like makes it look like a cutout\". So the bottom of the image around the hooves has dust. A bottom fade will fix it.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: maybe the simplest and safest is a linear bottom fade PLUS keep the radial:\nUse the two-layer + source-in approach but ALSO make the fallback decent. Modern browsers (the user is likely on Chrome/Edge/Safari): -webkit-mask-composite: source-in is well supported (Safari/Chrome for ages). Firefox: mask-composite: intersect supported since FF 53. So the combo works broadly. And worst case fallback: 'add' compositing → bottom slightly faded but radial still handles edges. Acceptable risk, and I can note it.\n\nActually, let me simplify even further and make it robust: put the bottom fade INSIDE the image itself using... no, can't edit the base64 image easily.\n\nOK decision: two-layer mask with source-in/intersect + keep radial. Plus — I could also darken the very bottom with a subtle overlay gradient (a ::after already used for shadow). Hmm, the ::after is the ground shadow ellipse. I could also add a bottom gradient overlay div... but keep it CSS-only with the mask.\n\nActually — wait. There's an even simpler and very effective approach: a bottom fade via an overlay gradient that blends the bull's lower edge into the background color. Add a pseudo or div with `background: linear-gradient(to top, var(--bg-primary), transparent)` positioned at the bottom of hero-visual — it covers the dust with the page's background color, smoothly. But it would also cover the ground shadow and lower bull... positioned at bottom ~15% height, it fades from page-bg (opaque) to transparent — creating a smooth blend. The dust gets covered by the opaque bg at the very bottom, blending it out. This is robust (no mask-composite needed) and simple!\n\nBut a gradient overlay at the bottom would also darken the bull's legs gradually — which is fine (fade into the darkness = the desired blend).\n\nHmm, but the mask approach preserves the bull's legs better. Let me think about what \"mud/dust\" looks like: brownish/grey smudges at the hooves area, possibly a dirt mound the bull stands on. The user wants it to not look like a cutout — meaning the bottom edge with dust should blend into the dark background. \n\nI think the cleanest: adjust the mask to fade the bottom + slightly reduce opacity of the whole image and strengthen the bottom fade. Combined approach:\n1. Two-layer mask (bottom fade + radial), with source-in composite.\n2. Keep the ground shadow (already helps ground it).\n\nLet me also reconsider the hover effect request. \"is it possible to add hover effects on it as well\" — on the bull itself (hero-visual). Classy hover:\n- `.hero-visual:hover img { opacity: 0.8; }` — brightens\n- `.hero-visual:hover::before { opacity: 1; transform: scale(1.15); }` — glow intensifies (no animation on hover, just a transition)\n- Maybe a subtle scale of the whole visual? They said no float; a tiny scale on hover might be OK but risky. Keep it to brightness + glow + maybe the sweep speeds up:\n- `.hero-visual:hover .sweep, .hero-visual:hover .sweep-2 { animation-duration: 2.5s; }` — sweeps move faster when hovered → feels interactive/alive\n- Maybe sparks brighten: `.hero-visual:hover .spark { opacity: 1; }` — hmm sparks animate opacity; overriding could conflict. Skip sparks.\n\nAlso add `transition` where needed. The img has no transition currently — add `transition: opacity 0.5s ease;` (careful: adding transition to img is fine since no animation on it anymore). The glow ::before has an animation (bull-breathe) — adding a hover rule with transform/opacity would conflict with the animation (animation overrides). Hmm — the ::before has `animation: bull-breathe 7s infinite` which continuously sets transform/opacity. A hover transition on opacity/transform won't apply while animation is running (animations take precedence over normal declarations for animated properties). \n\nTo make hover work on the glow, I could pause the animation on hover and apply stronger styles:\n```css\n.hero-visual:hover::before {\n  animation-play-state: paused;\n  opacity: 1;\n  transform: scale(1.18);\n}\n```\nWith animation paused, the non-animated declarations apply (paused animation keeps its current keyframe values though! animation-play-state: paused freezes at current frame — the keyframe values still override normal declarations). Hmm — actually when animation is paused, the animated properties still come from the animation. So setting opacity: 1 won't take effect. \n\nAlternative: instead of pausing, add a stronger glow as a separate element? Overkill. \n\nSimplest hover effects that definitely work:\n- `.hero-visual:hover img { opacity: 0.8; filter: drop-shadow(0 0 60px rgba(201,162,39,0.3)); }` — img has no animation → transition works. Add `transition: opacity 0.5s ease, filter 0.5s ease;` to the img rule.\n- `.hero-visual:hover .sweep, .hero-visual:hover .sweep-2 { animation-duration: 2.2s; }` — animation-duration is not an animated property issue; changing duration mid-animation restarts timing? Changing animation-duration while running causes the animation to jump (it re-times). It works fine visually — the sweep will complete faster. Add `transition: animation-duration`? Can't transition animation-duration reliably. It's fine — it just changes speed. Actually changing animation-duration on an element restarts the animation at the same progress point... it can cause a jump. Risky. Alternative: keep it simple, skip duration change.\n\nLet me do a clean, reliable hover set:\n1. `.hero-visual:hover img` → opacity 0.8, stronger gold drop-shadow, slight brightness via filter: `brightness(1.08)`. \n2. `.hero-visual:hover::after` (the shadow) → slightly wider/softer (no animation on it now, so transition works): `transform: translateX(-50%) scaleX(1.1); opacity: 0.8;`\n3. Maybe `.hero-visual:hover .spark` → `animation-duration: 4.5s;` (sparks rise faster on hover — nice \"excited\" feel; same duration-jump caveat but sparks are tiny, jump is imperceptible).\n\nAdd transitions to img and ::after.\n\nThe glow breathing continues regardless (it's the ambient life) — hover just brightens the bull itself and shadow. That's classy and safe.\n\nHmm — also, maybe add a subtle ring or border glow on hover around the visual? Like a gold ring fading in: use a new pseudo... ::before is glow, ::after is shadow — no free pseudos on hero-visual. Could add a `.hero-visual::marker`? No. Skip — brightness + shadow is enough. Or use the img's filter drop-shadow intensify (covers it).\n\nNow the bottom dust blending. Let me implement:\n\n```css\n.hero-visual img {\n  ...\n  mask-image:\n    radial-gradient(ellipse at center, black 45%, transparent 92%),\n    linear-gradient(to top, transparent 0%, black 16%);\n  -webkit-mask-image:\n    radial-gradient(ellipse at center, black 45%, transparent 92%),\n    linear-gradient(to top, transparent 0%, black 16%);\n  mask-composite: intersect;\n  -webkit-mask-composite: source-in;\n  ...\n}\n```\n\nWait — I need to double check the semantics: \n- mask layers listed first is on top. `-webkit-mask-composite: source-in`: keeps the result of source (top layer) intersected with destination (bottom). Top layer = radial; bottom = linear. Result = radial ∩ linear → visible where both opaque → bottom 16% faded. Good.\n- Standard `mask-composite: intersect`: same semantics (intersect = keep where both are opaque). Good.\n\nFallback if unsupported: browsers ignoring composite would 'add' the two layers → alpha = min(1, radialAlpha + linearAlpha)? 'add' composites by adding alpha values (clamped). At the very bottom, radial alpha ≈ 0 (from the 92% fade) + linear alpha ≈ 0 → transparent. At bottom 8%, radial maybe 0.2 + linear ~0.5 → 0.7 → partially visible (dust might peek). Hmm, 'add' fallback isn't great but modern browsers all support intersect/source-in. Acceptable.\n\nActually, let me reconsider — maybe simpler and fully robust: make the mask a SINGLE gradient that fades the bottom more: \n\n`radial-gradient(ellipse 80% 62% at 50% 46%, black 60%, transparent 90%)`\n\nThis is one layer, universally supported, no composite needed. Let me evaluate the fade at the bottom: center (50%, 46%), vertical radius 62%. Bottom y=100% → distance 54/62 = 0.87 → alpha ≈ 1 - (87-60)/(90-60) = 1-0.9 = 0.1 → nearly transparent. The dust at the bottom gets faded ~90%. Top y=0% → distance 46/62 = 0.74 → alpha = 1 - (74-60)/30 = 0.53 → half faded at very top (horns might get half-faded at the extreme top — could be OK, fade to darkness above).\n\nHmm, 0.53 at the very top might fade the bull's horns too much. The bull's horns are important. Let me compare with the two-layer approach which fades ONLY the bottom 16% and keeps the radial fade symmetric.\n\nActually the current radial (45% → 92% at center) already fades the top edge moderately. The issue is only the bottom. The two-layer approach is precise. I'll go with two-layer + composite, since Chrome/Safari/Edge all support -webkit-mask-composite: source-in, and Firefox supports mask-composite: intersect. This covers ~100% of users. \n\nLet me also increase the bottom fade from 16% to ~20% to make sure the dust is covered. Actually the dust — I don't know exactly how tall the dust area is. Let me use 18% and also strengthen with the ground shadow already present. \n\nHmm — actually, let me reconsider the whole thing once more. The user says \"on the bulls feet/legs, the mud/dust like makes it look like a cutout, is there a way to make look more smoother or blended into the background so more\". \n\nThe dust/mud is INSIDE the image (artwork). A bottom mask fade (transparent at the very bottom) will hide the dust entirely — the bull's feet will fade into the page background. That directly solves \"looks like a cutout\" → now it looks like it emerges from the darkness. \n\nLet me go with the two-layer mask, bottom fade 20%.\n\nAlso — should the mask fade be smooth (\"smoother\")? Use `transparent 0%` → `black 20%` for the linear layer: fully transparent at bottom, fully opaque by 20%. Linear = smooth. Good.\n\nWait, one more consideration: `mask-image` currently on img. The img also has `filter: drop-shadow(...)`. Filter + mask both apply — fine.\n\nLet me now write the CSS edits:\n\nEdit 1 — img rule: add hover transition, update mask to two layers:\n\nold:\n```css\n/* The bull itself — bigger, still, smoothly blended */\n.hero-visual img {\n  position: relative;\n  z-index: 1;\n  max-width: 176%;\n  max-height: 95vh;\n  height: auto;\n  opacity: 0.65;\n  filter: drop-shadow(0 0 45px rgba(201, 162, 39, 0.18));\n  mask-image: radial-gradient(ellipse at center, black 45%, transparent 92%);\n  -webkit-mask-image: radial-gradient(ellipse at center, black 45%, transparent 92%);\n}\n```\nnew:\n```css\n/* The bull itself — bigger, still, smoothly blended */\n.hero-visual img {\n  position: relative;\n  z-index: 1;\n  max-width: 176%;\n  max-height: 95vh;\n  height: auto;\n  opacity: 0.65;\n  filter: drop-shadow(0 0 45px rgba(201, 162, 39, 0.18));\n  transition: opacity 0.5s ease, filter 0.5s ease;\n  /* Blend the bottom (hooves / dust) into the page, keep edges soft */\n  mask-image:\n    radial-gradient(ellipse at center, black 45%, transparent 92%),\n    linear-gradient(to top, transparent 0%, black 20%);\n  -webkit-mask-image:\n    radial-gradient(ellipse at center, black 45%, transparent 92%),\n    linear-gradient(to top, transparent 0%, black 20%);\n  mask-composite: intersect;\n  -webkit-mask-composite: source-in;\n}\n\n/* Hover — the bull brightens and its aura warms up */\n.hero-visual:hover img {\n  opacity: 0.85;\n  filter: drop-shadow(0 0 60px rgba(201, 162, 39, 0.3));\n}\n\n.hero-visual:hover::after {\n  transform: translateX(-50%) scaleX(1.08);\n  opacity: 0.85;\n}\n```\n\nEdit 2 — ::after: add transition for smooth hover:\nold:\n```css\n.hero-visual::after {\n  content: '';\n  position: absolute;\n  bottom: 4%;\n  left: 50%;\n  width: 58%;\n  height: 7%;\n  transform: translateX(-50%);\n  background: radial-gradient(ellipse at center, rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.55), transparent 70%);\n  border-radius: 50%;\n  pointer-events: none;\n  z-index: 0;\n}\n```\nnew: add `transition: transform 0.5s ease, opacity 0.5s ease;`\n\nAlso sparks faster on hover:\n```css\n.hero-visual:hover .spark {\n  animation-duration: 4.5s;\n}\n```\nAdd this (harmless, adds excitement).\n\nLet me also make sure `.hero-visual:hover img` doesn't conflict with anything — no.\n\nNow, one concern: with `mask-composite: intersect` — if some browser doesn't support it AND doesn't support -webkit-mask-composite, the two mask layers 'add' → the linear layer (bottom transparent→black at 20%) combined with radial: at bottom region, radial ~0-0.3, linear 0-0.6... the dust would be partially visible. But again, all modern browsers support one of the two properties. Fine.\n\nLet me apply the edits. Also verify in preview afterward: img mask has 2 layers, hover rules exist.\n\nLet me write the str_replace.","open":false,"collapse":"preview"},{"kind":"ad","id":"p2-86dae4e5","ad":{"adText":"Build fast, relevant search and discovery experiences with Algolia’s AI-powered hosted platform.","title":"Streamline Site Search With Algolia's Hosted Platform","cta":"Learn More","url":"https://algolia.com","favicon":"https://icons.duckduckgo.com/ip3/algolia.com.ico","clickUrl":"https://api.trygravity.ai/track/click?p=gAAAAABqc1DavGCm7QE0NhAP0uYXqIPrXTlv_GnRcLhEjpvRFz0KxmU5pZs7rpBaTjSMar01rnXfa7LsZ0Zm0yZw9SH_bgyXAQV506wo8znZZ5OepHJa3e5mPYGdwhnpQotKwR_JRBXvni8PLD9cO9Hb7HbFEVhK66lEMlK9g0TN81LG4KERG8ccY7PdIf_UlE7D8iCDTZfTPyyzA2CcJuVx2p5TyzmEAz5CdbsaXLdp3pg1uoNYfgUHYLbKlmOCurzftPHYVHNRgY1nJu6K8x49AczY7XnczQQLd5C7sMLyQLGCsr8mJNWjuGfAEQZt3QQ-F5IRu4Hg12eLh3pBc8i46keXvrIX4NbO95D7LAa8qAG6uJJVPOxFIPUg8lxtUax1y5kQxHUcTKgvoM8GPiSbP0q4smCVluzyiOsTy-NtYnbHRM8Rt_Vwwv0H7uhFYybxz_CBjiPWgARNntdx21kt_G8PeLr_kRl1jjeDwvdGPwVvnOgLLSL-N9YlaY88UEeJoE4qC5dJsp-V98rsuS_8-Rx_LHG7kbgIKrg3uO-zFwrlAzAmzaAe6QjMmYgifJdeHmhQVpOwC1SQoV_KcJhgCnbUWG7udPjqutc3aMhxmuxzUXKX82wnv1qlzZM7Fu_GWSVgTiVFay1S0eEtHwIkDcEM6orhSzPHP8f29JjcsuRUCvdvzT8IV0pDIJOjrVcR-F8zIHyiwCvMtoylGkl1AdoKfTQXkMj42Aeb2dxIsT0LeUdjrOu2hEVaP0YiFcfra3JGxxzP9SCAkiw2HluKBG0beBwiD4myyQByOLYtsD-LJ9qErFzKr9EAmwkhKfP8__bFivVLcLgO_vuBbCYMTpuQt74Vu-WE4lOTDsw3_b3vo6WGV5dRH2Tg0XrZ_z-tFLrbpAFzayHnzwV-86FgMCAFzsSFl2mUgARhsBAKmhu-vBmkPUyQYniTtBakmRXKxm7pFaQyF38qcF4ys0WVNCn_AHTMmqV5ORe_i0BlpOS9fBVrjGThOUb8lcEKgYtg2yayXnRO4Tx3IDYj-s5imBJVIYQVRl30odQA-Hka5f-HIKGWrr-aS3s