reate a luxury cinematic hero image where a powerful bull becomes a symbol of market strength rather than just an animal. Position the bull on the right side of the screen with empty space on the left for website content. The bull should have a realistic detailed head, sharp horns, and a powerful front body, but gradually transform into flowing dust, smoke, and golden particles toward the back of its body. The bull should appear as if it is emerging from a storm of energy, with swirling dust wrapping around it and particles flowing backward to create a sense of speed and movement. Use a dark premium background with black, charcoal, bronze, and gold tones, dramatic lighting behind the bull, metallic highlights, and a powerful cinematic atmosphere. The final result should feel like a world-class financial brand identity — not just a bull image, but a visual representation of ambition, dominance, opportunity, and market power."}],"ts":1785943436617},{"role":"assistant","parts":[{"kind":"reasoning","id":"p1-37c49ef6","text":"Now the user has described both concepts:\n\n**SCENARIO A** — Realistic charging bull, right side, dust explosion at legs (hiding lower body), dark luxury bg with warm golden lighting, subtle highlights on horns/shoulders.\n\n**SCENARIO B** — Cinematic bull emerging from dust — realistic head/horns/front body that transforms into flowing dust, smoke, and golden particles toward the back. Bull emerging from a storm of energy. Black, charcoal, bronze, gold tones.\n\nI need to pick the one I can style better with CSS/SVG. The user said \"do whichever you feel like you will be able to style better with css or the other tools\".\n\nBoth are realistic bull concepts — I can't create a photorealistic bull with CSS. But I can create a stylized SVG bull. Which is more feasible?\n\n**Scenario B** is actually MUCH more feasible for CSS/SVG: the bull transforms into dust/particles toward the back. This works perfectly with a stylized approach:\n- A strong SVG bull head/front body (stylized, geometric or line-art with gold gradient fills)\n- The back half dissolves into floating gold particles (the sparks effect we already have!)\n- Swirling dust/smoke = CSS gradient blobs with blur\n- Golden particles flowing backward = animated sparks (already have the spark system!)\n- This perfectly matches what we've built: sparks, breathing glow, gold theme\n\nScenario A requires realistic fur and a dust explosion at the ground — harder to stylize convincingly; a \"realistic\" bull can't be done in SVG/CSS without looking cheap.\n\nSo **Scenario B** is the right choice — \"bull emerging from dust/energy\" translates beautifully into a stylized SVG bull with the back dissolving into particles, which I can build and animate with pure SVG/CSS. It also reuses and enhances the existing effects (sparks, glow, sweeps) making it cohesive.\n\nPlan:\n1. Create a stylized SVG bull (head + front shoulders, charging pose) with gold gradients — SVG is the tool. I need to draw a bull. This is the hard part: creating a good-looking bull SVG path by hand.\n\nLet me think about how to draw a credible stylized bull in SVG:\n- Charging bull head with lowered head, horns, front chest\n- Use layered paths: dark body silhouette + gold gradient highlights + horns + eye\n- The back/bottom dissolves into particles (achieved via the SVG fading out with a gradient mask, plus CSS particles)\n\nDesign approach for the SVG bull (viewBox 0 0 500 500 or similar):\n- A side-profile charging bull: head at left-front (lowered, facing left since content is on left... wait, bull positioned right side, facing... content on left. Typically bull charges toward the left toward the content, or faces left). Let me make the bull face LEFT (charging toward the text) — dynamic and common in trading brand imagery. Actually — the head lowered, charging forward. Facing left makes sense with content on left? Hmm, the text is on the left, bull on right. If the bull faces left (toward the text), it's charging \"at\" the content — could be aggressive but common (bull market = bull charging left/up). Alternatively face right (away). Most bull market graphics have the bull facing left (moving in the direction of the read). Let me face the bull LEFT.\n\n- Body: shoulders + chest on the right side of the SVG, neck rising, head lowered to the left, horns, muzzle. The back end (right side of SVG) dissolves into particles.\n\nLet me construct the SVG with these elements:\n1. **Back glow** — radial gold gradient behind (CSS already has ::before glow, keep).\n2. **Bull silhouette** — a dark charcoal body with subtle gold rim light. I'll build the body from overlapping shapes:\n   - Chest/shoulder: big curved shape\n   - Neck: rising curve\n   - Head: lowered, elongated muzzle\n   - Horns: two curved horns (one near, one far)\n   - Ear\n   - Eye: small gold/green glow\n3. **Gold rim lighting** — strokes along the top of the neck/head/horns in gold gradient\n4. **Dissolve effect** — apply a linear gradient mask on the right side of the SVG so the back fades out; plus CSS sparks flowing backward (to the right/up).\n\nActually, for the \"dissolving into particles\" — I can use SVG with a gradient stroke/fill fading to transparent toward the back, and layered CSS particle spans behind/around.\n\nLet me be pragmatic: build the bull as a cohesive set of SVG paths. Getting a *good-looking* bull from hand-coded paths is genuinely hard. Let me design carefully with simple but elegant geometry — a stylized, semi-geometric bull (like premium brand marks) rather than trying for realism. Stylized geometric bulls can look VERY premium (think trading platform logos).\n\nDesign (stylized geometric charging bull, side profile facing left):\n- Body/neck: bold curved silhouette filled with a dark-to-charcoal gradient with a gold edge stroke\n- Head lowered: a strong angular muzzle shape\n- Horns: sweeping curves in gold gradient\n- Chest: defined with a lighter charcoal/body curve\n- The whole thing fades to the right into particles\n\nLet me draft SVG paths (viewBox=\"0 0 520 440\"):\n\nBull facing left, head at left.\n\nElements:\n1. Far horn (behind head): gold gradient stroke, curve up-left from top of head\n2. Near horn: bigger gold curve\n3. Head + muzzle: path — lowered head. \n4. Neck: from head down-right into shoulders.\n5. Chest/shoulders: big mass on right.\n6. Eye + nostril details: small shapes.\n7. Gold rim-light strokes along top of neck and shoulders.\n\nRather than perfect realism, I'll do a layered approach:\n- A large dark silhouette path (body+neck+head as ONE path) filled with linear gradient (charcoal #1a1a1a → #050505) with subtle gold top highlight stroke\n- Horns on top\n- A \"dissolve\" mask fading the right side\n\nLet me carefully write the main silhouette path. Side profile bull, head lowered to the left:\n\nHead/muzzle at left ~x=40-120, y=200-280 (lowered below shoulders)\nNeck from head rising right to shoulders x=180-320, y=120-260\nShoulders/chest: big rounded mass x=180-480, y=140-420\n\nPath idea (clockwise from muzzle):\n```\nM 60 240          (muzzle tip, facing left, low)\nC 70 265, 100 275, 130 268   (jaw/chin curve down)\nC 150 285, 175 290, 195 285  (lower neck/chest front)\nC 210 320, 240 345, 280 360  (chest front dropping)\nC 330 380, 390 385, 430 370  (chest bottom)\nC 465 355, 480 330, 478 300  (back bottom right)\n... (back of body dissolving — I'll let the mask cut this off)\nC 460 250, 420 230, 380 235  (upper back)\nC 340 240, 300 210, 270 190  (back of neck up)\nC 250 172, 235 158, 215 150  (crest/top of neck rising)\nC 180 138, 150 150, 130 170  (back of head)\nC 110 190, 95 205, 85 215    (top of head to forehead)\nC 75 225, 65 233, 60 240 Z   (forehead to muzzle)\n```\n\nThis is hand-waving; precise beauty needs iteration. I can't preview the SVG rendering visually (I can't see screenshots!). That's a real constraint — I'll build it carefully and verify structurally via DOM, but aesthetic quality can't be visually confirmed by me. Hmm.\n\nWait — can I verify visually? I CAN use preview_snapshot (a11y tree — not visual) and computed styles. Screenshots I can't view. So I'll be building blind aesthetically. That's risky for an artwork-heavy change. \n\nGiven that, the SAFEST high-quality approach: \n1. Keep the existing bull IMAGE (the base64 webp the site already has — it's presumably a decent bull graphic) as the core, and ENHANCE it to match Scenario B: add the \"dissolve into particles\" via the mask + animated gold particles flowing backward, dust/smoke blobs with blur, stronger gold cinematic lighting. This way I'm not drawing a bull from scratch blind — I reuse the existing bull artwork and transform its presentation toward Scenario B's vibe.\n\nActually the user liked the existing bull (\"I currently like the bull\"). The concepts are about the DIRECTION/atmosphere, not necessarily replacing the bull asset. Scenario B is about: bull emerging from a storm of dust/energy, back transforming into particles, cinematic dark premium with gold.\n\nSo the plan: transform the existing hero bull toward **Scenario B** using pure CSS:\n1. **Bull dissolves backward into particles**: \n   - Add a horizontal fade mask so the bull's back side fades out (like it's transforming into particles) — combine with existing mask.\n   - Add more gold particles flowing backward (left→right or rising) from the bull — the spark system, enhanced: sparks behind the bull trailing right.\n2. **Dust/smoke storm**: CSS radial-gradient blobs with `filter: blur()` animating slowly (swirling), in charcoal/bronze/gold tones around the bull's base and trailing behind.\n3. **Cinematic lighting**: the breathing gold glow (have it) + a warm rim-light effect on the bull (drop-shadow gold, maybe a subtle brightness gradient overlay).\n4. **Golden particles flowing backward**: dedicated particle layer with directional drift (translate right + up + fade), more particles than current sparks, gold.\n\nThis achieves Scenario B's essence without redrawing the bull: \"head/front body strong, back dissolving into dust & particles, storm of energy, gold metallic highlights.\"\n\nI think this is the right call: pick **Scenario B**, implement via enhancing the existing bull with:\n- Directional dissolve mask (back half fades out)\n- Swirling dust/smoke blobs (CSS blurred gradients, slowly rotating/drifting)\n- A trailing particle field (gold particles streaming backward — replace/boost the spark system)\n- Keep breathing glow + gold sweeps + hover\n\nWhere does \"back\" point? Bull faces left (toward content) — wait, which way does the existing bull face? I don't know! The base64 image — from the earlier screenshots I never saw it clearly (vision descriptions of the homepage didn't detail the bull orientation). Hmm. The mask fade I'll apply on the RIGHT side (back = right) assuming bull faces left. If the bull actually faces right, the dissolve would hit its head. Risk!\n\nSince I can't see the bull image... this is risky. Let me think. The bull image in the hero — typical \"bull market\" graphics: the bull charges left (toward where text is) OR right. The original CSS had `mask-image: radial-gradient(ellipse at center, black 30%, transparent 70%)` — symmetric, no hints. The sweep elements sweep left→right (left:-60% → 160%) — no orientation hint.\n\nHmm. Actually — wait, I have a clue! The very first vision description in this conversation (Image 1, contact page) had no bull. But in the turn where I was shown the hero (Image 2 - index hero \"Ready to Stop Guessing\"? no...). Let me recall: I never got a vision description of the homepage hero bull. The preview screenshots I took were omitted to me.\n\nOK here's a safer plan: make the dissolve direction adaptive is impossible; instead, dissolve BOTH sides mildly? No — Scenario B needs the back to dissolve.\n\nAlternative: use the preview_snapshot? No visual. \n\nHmm — what about asking the user? They can