parent bg?)\n- Where the bull body is (opaque dark pixels)\n- Column-wise density → left heavy or right heavy → head direction?\n\nThis is doable with preview_evaluate! The image is same-origin data URI → canvas not tainted. Let me:\n1. Create an offscreen canvas, draw the img, get ImageData.\n2. For each of ~40 columns, compute average alpha and darkness.\n3. Report the profile → I can see where the bull mass is horizontally.\n\nThen I'll know: if the bull mass is concentrated on the LEFT with a tail on the right → head on left (faces left) → dissolve right side. Etc.\n\nThat's a clever, safe way to \"see\" the image. Let me do that.\n\nAlso I can get overall color tones (gold highlights?) to match lighting.\n\nOK let me execute:\n\nStep 1: Analyze the bull image via canvas pixel sampling in the preview.\nStep 2: Based on result, implement Scenario B:\n   - Right/back side dissolve mask (or appropriate side)\n   - Dust/smoke swirl blobs (CSS)\n   - Gold particle stream trailing backward\n   - Keep glow/sweep/hover\nStep 3: Verify.\n\nAlso the user said \"do whichever you feel like you will be able to style better\" — I'll explain my choice of Scenario B and why (it's the one that translates to pure CSS/SVG best — the dissolve-to-particles concept is what sparks/masks do naturally, whereas realistic fur can't be done in CSS).\n\nLet me start with the canvas analysis.\n\nThe img element: `document.querySelector('.hero-visual img')`. src is a data URI. Draw to canvas and sample.\n\nAnalysis plan in JS:\n```js\nconst img = document.querySelector('.hero-visual img');\nconst c = document.createElement('canvas');\nc.width = 120; c.height = 80;\nconst ctx = c.getContext('2d');\nctx.drawImage(img, 0, 0, 120, 80);\nconst data = ctx.getImageData(0, 0, 120, 80).data;\n// per column: avg alpha and avg luminance (weighted by alpha)\nconst cols = [];\nfor (let x = 0; x < 120; x++) {\n  let sumA = 0, sumL = 0, n = 0;\n  for (let y = 0; y < 80; y++) {\n    const i = (y * 120 + x) *