oward the right, drifting leftward/upward (\"particles flowing backward\").\n5. **Position**: lower the bull slightly (bottom offset), maybe shift right.\n6. Keep hover effects.\n\nLet me also consider: the user said \"do whichever you feel like you will be able to style better\". I picked B. But actually, let me reconsider Scenario A vs B once more. Scenario B is \"bull emerging from dust\" — the rear dissolves into flowing dust and golden particles. This is very achievable with CSS masks + blurred gradients + particles. Scenario A is \"cleaner crop\" — trivially achievable (just crop). But B is more impressive and matches the \"make it feel alive\" direction the user has been pushing. And it builds on the existing gold sparks. Go with B.\n\nOne important consideration: I cannot see the bull image, so I must be careful with the mask direction. Let me verify the image orientation programmatically. I could sample pixels: the head has horns (bright gold), and typically the head is smaller/denser than the body. Actually, a better approach: analyze the image's horizontal luminance profile. The bull is \"polished gold and dark metallic plates against black background\". The head is at one end.\n\nHmm, but pixel analysis of a compressed base64 webp requires decoding. I have node? Let me check what tools are available. Actually simpler: I can use Python PIL if available, or just trust the mockup description (\"facing left\" per Scenario A). The mockup and current site use the same artwork — the mockup is literally this page redesigned. So the bull faces left.\n\nActually wait — is the current site's bull the same as the mockup? The mockup shows the exact same layout/text as the current hero, so the user made the mockup from the current site's bull image. \"facing left\" — the description says \"The bull is viewed from the side/three-quarter angle, facing left.\" So yes: head on the left, rear on the right.\n\nSo: dissolve the RIGHT side of the image.\n\nBut hmm, one more subtlety — the current hero-visual is on the right side of the page. The bull faces left (toward the text). The rear is on the far right edge, partially cut off by the screen edge. Dissolving the right side into dust there is perfect — it already probably extends past the viewport edge (the image is 176% width of the container).\n\nLet me also verify by pixel analysis if possible — quick check whether node/python exists. Actually, let me not over-engineer. I'll sample the actual bull image file (images/RFX GOLDEN BULLS X.png) with a quick node script if node is available, to determine orientation. This is a good verification step since the whole effect depends on dissolving the right side.\n\nHmm wait, is images/RFX GOLDEN BULLS X.png even the same image as the hero base64? Maybe. Let me compare sizes. Actually, let me first read the hero markup fully (after the img tag, the .sweep/.spark elements) — the read truncated. Let me get lines 86-130 of index.html via code_search with context, or read with a targeted approach. Actually the read gave me up to the base64. The hero-visual structure: \n\n```html\n<div class=\"hero-visual\">\n  <img src=\"data:image/webp;base64,...\">\n  <div class=\"sweep\"></div>\n  <div class=\"sweep-2\"></div>\n  ...sparks...\n</div>\n```\n\nI need to see what comes after. Let me search index.html for \"spark\" and \"sweep\" lines.\n\nPlan of tool calls:\n1. Read style.css lines 400-500 and 1540-1910 (two targeted reads). read_files reads whole files; the CSS is big but probably under limits. Let me just read the whole style.css? It's ~2000 lines, maybe 60-80KB. The read_files limit is 100k chars total. Risky but the earlier read of index.html truncated at 19k tokens because of the base64. style.css should be fine... but it might be ~100KB+. Let me instead use code_search with context flags to pull specific blocks:\n   - Search pattern \"\\.hero-visual\" with -A 30 context around lines 473 and 1742.\n   - Search for \"@media\" near 1655.\n   \n   Actually, a cleaner approach: read the file with sed via basher to extract line ranges. `sed -n '400,500p' style.css` etc. That's terminal output — fine.\n\n2. Check spark markup in index.html: code_search \"spark\" -g index.html.\n\n3. Check node availability for pixel analysis: `node -e ...` or check `which node`. Also check `python`. Let me do a quick check of the image file's dimensions and orientation via a tiny script. Actually — since the base64 webp is in the HTML, the file images/RFX GOLDEN BULLS X.png may or may not be identical. Let me check its file size and maybe decode.\n\nLet me be pragmatic: run `sed` extractions in parallel with a node/python capability check, and code_search the index.html sparks.\n\nThen implement:\n\n**CSS changes** (in the hero-visual override block ~line 1742+):\n- Update `.hero-visual img` mask: add a horizontal fade on the right (back) — `linear-gradient(to right, black 45%, transparent 92%)` intersected with the existing radial edge fade and bottom fade. Wait — mask-composite with 3 layers: order matters. The existing mask has 2 layers (radial + bottom linear). Adding a third. With `mask-composite: intersect` (standard) / `-webkit-mask-composite: source-in` (webkit), all layers intersect. Actually `-webkit-mask-composite: source-in` means \"intersect with the layer below\"... For multiple layers in webkit, you need multiple `-webkit-mask-composite` values (one per layer). Hmm, this gets complicated. \n\n  Actually, let me reconsider. The cleanest approach: keep the existing radial+bottom mask on `.hero-visual img`, and add the directional back-dissolve into the same mask stack. mask-composite per layer:\n  - For the standard `mask-composite` property, values apply per layer in order. `intersect` for layers 2 and 3.\n  - For `-webkit-mask-composite`, values also apply per layer; `source-in` means the layer combines with the one below via intersection. So two `-webkit-mask-composite` values: `source-in, source-in`.\n  \n  Current CSS (from search results line 1795-1800):\n  ```\n  mask:\n    radial-gradient(...),\n    linear-gradient(to top, transparent 0%, black 20%);\n  mask-composite: intersect;\n  -webkit-mask-composite: source-in;\n  ```\n  \n  Hmm wait, with 2 layers, one composite value applies to layer 2 (intersect with layer 1). Adding a 3rd layer means I need `mask-composite: intersect, intersect;` and `-webkit-mask-composite: source-in, source-in;`.\n\n  Actually, maybe simpler: rather than intersecting a third mask, I can put the directional fade INSIDE the existing radial-gradient via a combined gradient? No — a single gradient can't do both radial and directional. \n\n  Alternative simpler approach: instead of complicating the mask, position the image so its right edge is already off-screen (the bull's rear extends past the viewport), and the radial fade already softens edges. But the user explicitly wants \"flowing dust... particles flowing backward... swirling dust wrapping around it\". The visual dissolve effect needs the image to actually fade on the right side.\n\n  Hmm, but wait — there's a subtlety with dissolving \"the back of its body\" — the rear of the bull is on the right. But if the image extends off-screen on the right, a fade in the mask will be subtle. The bull is at 176% width — the rear may be partially cropped off-screen. A fade from ~55% to 95% across the image will visibly dissolve the visible rear portion.\n\n  Let me go with the 3-layer mask with proper composite values. That's well-supported:\n  ```css\n  mask:\n    radial-gradient(ellipse ...),\n    linear-gradient(to top, transparent 0%, black 20%),\n    linear-gradient(to right, black 50%, transparent 96%);\n  mask-composite: intersect, intersect;\n  -webkit-mask-composite: source-in, source-in;\n  ```\n  \n  Wait, but I need to check what the current mask values are exactly. Let me read the block.\n\n- **Dust clouds**: add 2-3 `::before`-like elements? `.hero-visual` already uses `::before`