eft.\" The bull on this page is the same artwork (mockup is a redesign of this page). The current hero-visual is in the RIGHT column of the hero grid; the content is on the LEFT. A bull facing left looks toward the content — natural. And the existing sparks cluster more on the left-ish (18%, 30%) and right (80%, 68%)...\n\nHmm, but wait — actually, let me think about whether the bull in the CURRENT site faces left or right. The mockup is what the user wants to achieve (\"I found the visual concept for you\" — meaning they want the hero to look like these mockups). The mockups show the bull facing left. If the current site's bull faces right, then the mockup was made with a mirrored/flipped version... unlikely — more likely the current site already has the bull facing left (the mockup just reuses the same image).\n\nActually, I realize I can verify orientation from the base64 image data! The bull image is embedded in index.html as base64 WebP. I could decode it... but no node, and python is a stub. Hmm. What about extracting a thumbnail? I have `preview_evaluate` — I can use the browser itself to analyze the image! The browser is running the page. I can:\n1. Load the image via the DOM (`document.querySelector('.hero-visual img')`).\n2. Draw it to a canvas and sample pixel columns to detect where bright gold pixels are.\n\nThat's a great approach — use preview_evaluate to sample the image and determine orientation by finding the brightest region (the head/horns are usually the brightest/highlighted part... though the whole bull is gold).\n\nBetter: determine which side has the \"head\" by analyzing column-wise brightness asymmetry... it's ambiguous. Hmm.\n\nAlternative: crop-sample the left third vs right third. The head + horns typically have high contrast bright highlights. The rear is a big mass. Honestly, brightness analysis is unreliable.\n\nBUT — there's a much better signal: the mockup description. Scenario A: \"The bull is viewed from the side/three-quarter angle, facing left.\" This is authoritative for the artwork. I'll go with facing left → rear on right.\n\nHmm, wait, actually, let me reconsider once more. There's still ambiguity about whether the current site bull = mockup bull. The mockup shows the nav with \"CAREERS\" link (we just added Careers to nav!). The current site has Careers in nav (we added it). And the hero content text matches exactly (\"Game Changers\", \"Premium Trading Education\", \"Since 2020...\"). So YES — the mockup IS this exact page. Same bull image. Facing left. \n\nSo Scenario B implementation: bull's back (right side) dissolves into dust.\n\nAlso — scenario B mockup: \"Position the bull on the right side of the screen with empty space on the left for website content\" — that matches current layout (content left, bull right). \"bull... positioned lower and centrally charging forward\" — I'll lower the bull a bit.\n\nLet me now also verify via the browser canvas sampling as a sanity check — quick and cheap. Actually, let me do it properly: sample the image's left half vs right half brightness AND detect horns (pointy bright shapes). Too complex. A simpler proxy: find the column x where the bull's \"mass\" ends on each side — the head is at one end, rear at the other; the head region typically has a narrower profile (neck + head) while the rear is bulky. \n\nHonestly, I'll trust the mockup. It's described clearly. Facing left.\n\nNow let me design the CSS for Scenario B:\n\n**1. Bull mask — dissolve the rear (right side) into dust:**\nAdd a third mask layer to `.hero-visual img`:\n```css\nlinear-gradient(to right, black 45%, transparent 96%)\n```\nwith composites: `mask-composite: intersect, intersect;` and `-webkit-mask-composite: source-in, source-in;`\n\nWait, careful: currently there are 2 layers and `mask-composite: intersect` (single value applies to the second layer, intersecting it with the first). With 3 layers, I need two values: layer 2 intersects layer 1, layer 3 intersects the re