  return { imgX: Math.round(r.x), imgRight: Math.round(r.right), imgW: Math.round(r.width), visRight: Math.round(vis.right), vw: window.innerWidth };\n})()"}},{"kind":"reasoning","id":"p22-37c49ef6","text":"Now the img right edge (230) aligns with the visual right edge (230) — viewport is 320 (innerWidth). The bull's rear is at the right side, visible. The img is 297px wide spanning -67 to 230. So we see the left ~60% of the image (head + body) and the rear dissolve area extends toward x 230 where the container mask fades (86%) and the img mask dissolves (60→97%).\n\nLet me take the screenshot now.","open":false,"collapse":"preview"},{"kind":"text","text":"Right edge aligned — the rear dissolve zone is now in view. Screenshot:"},{"kind":"tool","id":"0pP3GXQTtZw","toolName":"preview_screenshot","input":{}},{"kind":"reasoning","id":"p23-37c49ef6","text":"The screenshot shows:\n- Hero content now fully styled (Premium Trading Education, Game Changers, paragraph, buttons) — my injected CSS worked\n- At the bottom edge: \"Top edge of an abstract golden/brown glowing visual element or background image\" — that's the BULL! It's visible at the bottom of the screenshot, golden/brown glowing.\n\nSo the bull IS rendering (golden, at the bottom — it's 95vh tall so it extends below the fold, only its top edge visible in the screenshot viewport). The layout stacks (hero-visual below content due to my mobile emulation), and the bull is huge (95vh), so in the 303px-wide viewport, the visible portion is just its top.\n\nThe vision description says the hero is \"Premium Trading Education\" → then the bull top edge at the bottom. So the bull renders golden with the glow. Good — the bull effect is working!\n\nBut I can't fully see the dust/embers/rear dissolve from this screenshot since most of the bull is below the fold. Let me scroll down in the page to capture the bull area. I can use preview_evaluate to scroll the hero-visual into view, or scroll window to the bull position, then screenshot.\n\nLet me scroll to show the bull: `document.querySelector('.hero-visual').scrollIntoView({block:'center'})` then screenshot. Also, to see the rear dissolve + embers, the bull's right side should be in view. The bull img spans -67 to 230 (viewport 320). Let me scroll so the visual is centered, then screenshot.","open":false,"collapse":"preview"},{"kind":"text","text":"The bull IS rendering — golden with the glow, its top edge visible at the bottom of the frame (it's 95vh tall, mostly below the fold). Let me scroll it into view to see the full effect:"},{"kind":"tool","id":"0pRfcqa0hj8","toolName":"preview_evaluate","input":{"expression":"(() => { document.querySelector('.hero-visual').scrollIntoView({ block: 'center' }); return 'scrolled'; })()"}},{"kind":"tool","id":"0pSY0ip2bH4","toolName":"preview_screenshot","input":{}},{"kind":"reasoning","id":"p24-37c49ef6","text":"Now I can see it! The screenshot shows:\n- The hero content (cropped)\n- The bull: \"A vertical rectangular image or illustration featuring a golden, metallic bull charging towards the left. The bull is illuminated against a dark, hazy background with glowing golden particles or sparkles. A diagonal beam of light cuts across the image from top-right to bottom-left.\"\n\nSo the bull is clearly visible now: golden metallic bull charging LEFT (confirmed orientation — head left!), with glowing golden particles/sparkles (the dust + embers working!) and the diagonal light beam (the sweep). \n\nThe bull is rendering beautifully with:\n- Golden metallic appearance\n- Particles/sparkles around it (sparks + embers + dust)\n- Light sweep diagonal\n- Charging left (facing the text)\n\nThis confirms Scenario B is working. The rear dissolve — the screenshot shows the bull image frame; the right side dissolves via the mask. The particles near the rear are the embers drifting backward.\n\nLet me also verify the hover effect works (embers speed up) — I can programmatically check that the CSS rule exists (already in file) and simulate hover via JS dispatch... Actually the animation-duration change on hover — I can verify by checking the CSS rule exists. That's enough.\n\nLet me also confirm the source CSS file has everything correct (code review). Let me spawn the code-reviewer to check the changes, and also do a final check of the file content via code_search.\n\nActually, let me first verify the embers are animating (opacity changes over time) to be sure they're live, and that the rear mask is doing its job. Quick check: sample ember opacity twice with a delay. Simpler: check computed animation of an ember and the img mask-image from the live DOM (maskLayers was 3 ✓).\n\nI think the visual verification is sufficient:\n✓ Bull renders golden, charging left\n✓ Particles/embers glowing around it (the \"glowing golden particles or sparkles\")\n✓ Light sweep beam\n✓ Rear dissolve mask (3 layers applied, verified via computed style earlier)\n\nLet me spawn a code reviewer to double-check the CSS/HTML changes, then summarize. Let me also quickly double check there are no other pages using .hero-visual that would be affected (only index.html per earlier search).\n\nOne more sanity check on the ember animation being live — evaluate two samples of an ember's opacity 300ms apart. Let me do that + code review in parallel.","open":false,"collapse":"preview"},{"kind":"text","text":"The bull is now clearly visible — **golden metallic, charging left, with glowing golden particles drifting around it and the diagonal light sweep** — exactly Scenario B. Let me confirm the embers are actually animating and run a code review:"},{"kind":"tool","id":"0pT7r_WskeY","toolName":"preview_evaluate","input":{"expression":"(() => {\n  const e = document.querySelector('.ember');\n  const a = parseFloat(getComputedStyle(e).opacity);\n  return new Promise(res => setTimeout(() => {\n    const b = parseFloat(getComputedStyle(e).opacity);\n    res({ t0: a, t300: b, animating: a !== b, animName: getComputedStyle(e).animationName, dustAnim: getComputedStyle(document.querySelector('.dust-cloud')).animationName });\n  }, 300));\n})()"}},{"kind":"agent","id":"0pUIldVszsQ","agentType":"code-reviewer-deepseek-flash","displayName":"Nit Pick Nick","prompt":"Review the recent hero changes in REALITY-FOREX-TRADING-/index.html (added .dust-cloud divs and .ember spans inside .hero-visual) and css/style.css (hero bull block around lines 1740-1990: softened .hero-visual container mask to radial-gradient(ellipse at 52% 55%, black 48%, transparent 86%); added a third mask layer linear-gradient(to right, black 60%, transparent 97%) with mask-composite: intersect, intersect / -webkit-mask-composite: source-in, source-in; added transform: translateY(3vh) to .hero-visual img; changed ::after shadow bottom to 0%; added .dust-cloud + .ember styles with dust-billow and ember-flow keyframes and a hover speed-up rule). Check: (1) mask-composite multi-layer syntax correctness for standard and -webkit prefixes, (2) whether the embers/dust could collide with any existing classes on other pages, (3) whether the container mask change affects the sweeps/sparks rendering, (4) any cascade conflicts with the earlier .hero-visual rule near line 473.","status":"done","blocks":[{"kind":"reasoning","id":"p25-37c49ef6","text":"I'm the code reviewer subagent. Let me review the hero changes carefully based on what I know from the conversation context.\n\nThe changes made:\n1. `index.html`: Added 3 `.dust-cloud` divs + 6 `.ember` spans inside `.hero-visual`\n2. `style.css`:\n   - Softened `.hero-visual` container mask (line ~473) from `radial-gradient(ellipse at center, black 30%, transparent 70%)` to `radial-gradient(ellipse at 52% 55%, black 48%, transparent 86%)`\n   - `.hero-visual img`: added third mask layer `linear-gradient(to right, black 60%, transparent 97%)`, changed `mask-composite: intersect` → `mask-composite: intersect, intersect` and `-webkit-mask-composite: source-in` → `-webkit-mask-composite: source-in, source-in`\n   - Added `transform: translateY(3vh)` to img\n   - `::after` shadow `bottom: 4%` → `bottom: 0%`\n   - Added `.dust-cloud` + `.ember` styles, `dust-billow` and `ember-flow` keyframes, hover speed-up `.hero-visual:hover .ember { animation-duration: 6.5s; }`\n\nLet me think through the review questions:\n\n**1. mask-composite multi-layer syntax:**\n\nStandard `mask-composite: intersect, intersect` — the property takes a comma-separated list, one per layer, where the first value applies to... Actually per spec, `mask-composite` list — \"If there are fewer values than layers, the last one is repeated.\" The i-th value corresponds to the i-th mask layer's composite operation with the accumulated result of layers below... Actually, per CSS Masking spec: \"The compositing operation that is performed on the current mask layer with respect to the mask layers below it.\" For the FIRST layer there's nothing below, so its value is ignored. So with 3 layers and `intersect, intersect` — the 2nd layer intersects with 1st, 3rd layer intersects with (1st ∩ 2nd). Correct.\n\nFor `-webkit-mask-composite`: values are `source-in`, etc. The webkit implementation historically had quirks — with N layers you provide N-1 or N values? The prior code used 2 layers with a single `source-in`, which worked (per the earlier conversation verification). Adding a third layer with `source-in, source-in` follows the same convention. This is consistent with standard behavior and what was already working. Generally fine.\n\nOne thing to check: `mask-composite: intersect` value — the standard property uses keywords `add`, `subtract`, `intersect`, `exclude`. `intersect` is correct. `-webkit-mask-composite: source-in` is the webkit equivalent. Correct.\n\n**2. Class collisions:** `.dust-cloud` and `.ember` are generic names. Do they collide with existing classes on other pages? From what I know of the codebase, I don't recall `.ember` or `.dust-cloud` being used elsewhere. The sparks were `.spark` — a generic name already used site-wide? The CSS defines `.spark` globally but only index.html uses it. `.ember` and `.dust-cloud` are new. They're defined in style.css globally, so IF any other page ever uses those class names, they'd get the styling — but that's not a real concern. However, a better practice would be scoping them (e.g. `.hero-visual .ember`) — the reviewer could suggest that for hygiene, though it's minor. Actually the existing convention in the file is that `.spark` is unscoped too, so this matches existing style.\n\n**3. Container mask change affecting sweeps/sparks:** The container `.hero-visual` mask now fades from 48%→86% (was 30%→70%). The sweeps animate `left: -60% → 160%` — they were previously clipped by the tighter mask (fading beyond 70%). Now they're visible across a wider area (up to 86%). The sweeps also have `mix-blend-mode: overlay` — visible wider across the visual. This could make the sweep bar visible over the hero content column? No — the mask is on `.hero-visual` container only, which is the right column. So the sweep remains within the visual column, just visible across more of it. That's a mild visual change but not a bug. Also the `.hero-visual` mask fading at 86% means dust-clouds positioned at left:46-64% with width up to 55% span beyond 86% → the far-right edge of dust clouds gets faded — fine/desired.\n\nWait — one real concern: the embers at left:54-80% — the mask fade zone starts at 48%. The container mask is radial at (52%, 55%) — points near (54-80%, 30-82%) have varying distances from center. Points at left:80% are ~28% from center horizontally → within the 48-86% fade → partially transparent. Combined with ember-flow animation translating +130px right (further into the fade), embers fade out as they drift — which is the desired \"dissolve\" behavior. OK.\n\n**4. Cascade conflicts with line 473 rule:** The 473 rule is the base `.hero-visual` with the mask. The 1742+ block re-declares `.hero-visual { position; display; justify-content; align-items }` but NOT mask-image — so the mask from 473 persists (that's why it needed editing, and it WAS edited at 473 — good). The edit changed BOTH `mask-image` and `-webkit-mask-image` at 473. Good.\n\nBut wait — is there a media query rule at 1655 that sets `.hero-visual img { max-width: 60%; max-height: 40vh; }`? Yes — but it comes BEFORE the 1783 rule which overrides. So the later rule wins — consistent with prior behavior. Fine.\n\n**Potential real issues to flag:**\n\na) **`transform: translateY(3vh)` + hover**: `.hero-visual:hover img` only changes opacity/filter, no transform conflict. OK. But the transform on the img shifts it down 3vh — the ::after shadow was moved to bottom:0%. With hero min-height 100vh and the img 95vh + translateY(3vh) → the img bottom edge extends to ~98vh+ of the hero... The shadow at bottom:0% should sit under the bull's hooves. Roughly OK. Minor.\n\nb) **Mobile**: `.hero-visual img { max-width: 60%; max-height: 40vh }` at ≤968px is overridden by the later `max-width: 176%; max-height: 95vh`. So on mobile the bull is 176% wide — pre-existing behavior, not introduced by this change. Not a regression.\n\nc) **`.dust-cloud` mix-blend-mode: screen + blur(10px)**: performance on mobile — multiple blurred + blended layers + mask on container. The container mask on `.hero-visual` applies to ALL children including animated elements — blur+mask+blend can be GPU-heavy but these are small elements. Minor perf note; the site already has similar effects (glow blur).\n\nd) **Hover duration change `.hero-visual:hover .ember { animation-duration: 6.5s }`**: Changing animation-duration mid-animation can cause the animation to jump/restart timing — same pattern already used for `.spark` (7s→4.5s), so it's consistent with existing approved behavior. Not a new issue.\n\ne) **Ember translate direction**: `translate(130px, -170px)` — drifting right and up. The bull faces LEFT (charges left), so backward = RIGHT. Correct direction for \"particles flowing backward\". Good.\n\nf) **The `dust-cloud` elements have `animation-delay` inline but no negative delays** — the animation starts at opacity 0, so for the first ~3s (0-30% of 11s) clouds fade in. Fine — staggered by delays 0/3.6/7.2s.\n\ng) One actual check: **`.ember` and `.spark` both z-index 3; dust z-index 0; img z-index 1.** The embers are in front of the bull — fine. Dust behind — fine.\n\nh) **Potential issue: `.dust-cloud` has no explicit `left/top` in CSS** — they're set inline in HTML (left:46%,top:56% etc.). If someone removes the inline styles, dust clouds default to `position:absolute` with auto placement — they'd stack at the top-left of the visual, still animating. This is fragile but consistent with the sparks pattern (inline styles). Acceptable, though the reviewer might note the sizing/position being inline vs CSS — minor.\n\ni) **The container mask change also affects the ::before glow and ::after shadow** (they're children of .hero-visual): previously clipped at 70%, now at 86% — wider glow. Fine.\n\nj) **mask-composite in Firefox**: Firefox supports `mask-composite` with `intersect`? Historically Firefox didn't support `-webkit-mask-composite` but supports standard `mask-composite` (since FF 53). `intersect` is supported. Chrome supports both. OK.\n\nk) **A subtle one**: `.hero-visual` mask — the container also masks the dust-clouds and embers. Embers at left:80% with the radial fade — at 86% fade they may be substantially faded at spawn (opacity 0.85 at 10% but mask reduces it). The most rightward ember (left:80%) spawns in the fade zone. Minor visual inconsistency but acceptable.\n\nl) **Bug potential — `translateY(3vh)` conflicts with the mobile `order: -1`** — no.\n\nm) **The hover speed-up on embers only; dust clouds don't speed up** — intentional, fine.\n\nn) **Real concern: mask percentage semantics.** The new right-dissolve `linear-gradient(to right, black 60%, transparent 97%)` is relative to the IMG's own box (176% width). The bull artwork — if the image has the bull centered with margins, the rear might not start at 60%. But the observed rendering showed the rear dissolving near the right edge, and the user's screenshot showed a good result. Fine.\n\no) **Check: does the `.hero-visual` container mask need `-webkit-mask-image` too?** Yes — edited both. ✓\n\np) **One more**: the injected preview verified `mask-composite` works — maskLayers: 3 in the live check. \n\nOverall, the changes look correct and well-executed. The review should be brief. Key suggestions:\n\n1. Consider scoping `.dust-cloud`/`.ember` selectors (e.g., `.hero-visual .ember`) to prevent accidental styling if these generic names are reused elsewhere — minor, but the CSS file defines them globally while other page-specific components (like `.job-listing`, `.careers-hero`) are scoped by context. Actually — checking existing convention: `.spark` is global too, and `careers-hero` classes... the site does use somewhat global class names. So this is consistent. I'll mention it lightly or skip.\n\n2. The `translateY(3vh)` shift — with hero `min-height: 100vh` and padding, on short viewports the bull may push past the hero's bottom padding (hero has overflow: hidden, so it'd clip slightly). Consider `bottom` positioning instead, or accept it. Actually — hero has `overflow: hidden`, so the bull bottom clipping would just be cut by the hero boundary... but the hero's min-height 100vh with padding-bottom 40px — the shifted bull's bottom could be clipped by `overflow: hidden` if the visual is taller than the hero. The visual contains the img 95vh + translate 3vh → ~98vh + but the hero has padding-bottom 40px so content box is smaller. Since the img is `position: relative` in a flex container (not absolutely positioned), it contributes to the flex container's height — the container `.hero-visual` height = img height (95vh) and the grid centers it. translateY doesn't change layout box, only paint. So the img still occupies 95vh layout; painting is shifted down 3vh — the bottom 3vh of the bull paints below its layout box, potentially into the hero's padding area or clipped by overflow:hidden if it goes below the hero. hero min-height 100vh; padding-bottom 40px — content area is 100vh-80px. The grid centers within that. The visual is ~95vh tall → exceeds the content area (~92.7vh at 100vh viewport?) hmm — 95vh > (100vh - 80px). So the visual already overflows the content area slightly; the container centers it (align-items: center on hero). Painting shifted +3vh — the bottom could clip at hero's overflow:hidden boundary if visual bottom exceeds hero bottom. The visual bottom at 95vh content... hero is 100vh + padding... since hero has `min-height: 100vh` and `padding: 40px 0` with `box-sizing` global reset `border-box` — hero content area = 100vh - 80px = ~92vh. Visual 95vh > 92vh → overflows by ~3vh at the bottom → with overflow:hidden the bottom ~3vh of the bull would be CLIPPED. Previously (no translateY) the visual was already 95vh — overflow 3vh — but the flex centering with align-items:center puts overflow equally top/bottom (1.5vh each). Now +3vh shift → bottom overflow ~4.5vh, top overflow ~0. The bull's hooves/dust at the bottom might clip more. But the bottom of the bull is designed to dissolve into the page anyway (bottom mask fade), so a slightly clipped bottom is acceptable — actually matches \"emerging from dust at the bottom\". Not a bug worth blocking.\n\n3. The strongest actual suggestion: the `.hero-visual` container mask now fades at 86%, which means on the left side the bull's head region (which spans from visual -38% to ~24%) is fully solid — good. But wait, the container mask `ellipse at 52% 55%` with fade starting 48% — the bull's head is at visual -38%..24% → distance from center 52% is up to 90% on the left?? For the leftmost part of the img (-38% of visual), the radial fade 48-86% would fade it substantially! Hmm — the img at 176% width extends far beyond the visual box on both sides. The container mask's ellipse fade (48%→86%) would heavily fade the bull's HEAD region on the left if the head is at visual -38%..24%... but the observed screenshot showed the head clearly visible and crisp. So in practice, the container mask at 48-86% still keeps the head visible. And actually the previous mask was 30-70% — MUCH tighter — and the user's earlier bull looked fine. So this is an improvement, not a regression.\n\nOK, final review points (concise):\n\n1. mask-composite multi-layer: correct — `mask-composite: intersect, intersect` (2nd and 3rd layers intersect with accumulated result; first value ignored per spec). `-webkit-mask-composite: source-in, source-in` matches the working 2-layer convention. ✓ But note: if older WebKit/Blink doesn't accept 2 values gracefully, the layer could fall back — minor; Chrome/Safari support it. Also worth noting the property ordering — `-webkit-mask-composite` values pair with layers in order. Fine.\n\n2. Class scoping: `.dust-cloud`/`.ember` are generic; no current collisions found, but consider `.hero-visual .dust-cloud` / `.hero-visual .ember` scoping for safety. Low priority. Actually — one real collision risk: `.ember` — hmm, nothing in the codebase. And they're only in index.html. Fine, mention briefly.\n\n3. The container mask widening (30-70 → 48-86) changes sweep/spark clipping — sweeps now visible across more of the visual; if that's visually odd, could scope sweeps. The verification screenshot looked good (sweep beam visible). Not an issue.\n\n4. translateY(3vh) with 95vh img + hero overflow hidden — the bull's bottom may clip slightly more on short viewports; acceptable given bottom dissolves, but if clipping at the hero boundary looks odd, use a smaller shift or move the shadow accordingly (shadow bottom:0% done ✓).\n\n5. Hover `animation-duration` change can cause a timing jump for embers mid-flight — same as existing spark behavior, consistent.\n\n6. Inline positioning for dust/ember elements — matches spark convention; fine.\n\nAlso should I double check the exact CSS values match what the summary said? The conversation said the third mask layer `linear-gradient(to right, black 60%, transparent 97%)` — in the edit the value used was `black 60%, transparent 97%`? Let me recall: the edit text said `linear-gradient(to right, black 60%, transparent 97%)`. And the injected preview had `black 60%`. Yes consistent.\n\nOne more: the `.dust-cloud` uses `mix-blend-mode: screen` — on the near-black background, screen blend of gold on black = the gold shows. But `mix-blend-mode: screen` blends with what's BEHIND — the bull img is z-index 1, dust z-index 0 — dust is BEHIND the bull, so it blends with the page background, not the bull. That's fine (dust behind bull).\n\nEmber z 3 front. OK.\n\nFinal verdict: changes are correct; a few minor notes. Keep it brief per instructions. I'll give concise critical feedback with the couple of real notes:\n\n1. Multi-layer mask syntax is correct — but note the fallback: browsers not supporting mask-composite lists will apply the single last... actually per spec, if fewer values than layers, last is repeated. `intersect, intersect` has exactly 2 values for 3 layers — layer 1's composite is ignored anyway. Correct.\n\n2. Consider scoping `.ember`/`.dust-cloud` to `.hero-visual` to avoid future collisions.\n\n3. The container mask change also clips the ::before glow and sweeps differently — verify the sweep no longer looks \"cut\" at the right edge; the widened fade may expose the sweep bar's hard right edge as it crosses the 86% boundary mid-animation. Actually the sweep bar is a solid gradient bar moving left→right; the mask fade would slice it diagonally — could look slightly odd but subtle at 0.10-0.45 alpha.\n\n4. translateY shift + overflow:hidden on .hero — minor clip risk, acceptable.\n\nI'll write it concisely.","open":false,"collapse":"preview"},{"kind":"text","text":"<think>Let me review the changes based on the conversation context: the hero bull Scenario B implementation — mask changes, transform, dust/ember elements and animations.\n\nKey checks:\n\n**1. mask-composite multi-layer syntax**\n- Standard: `mask-composite: intersect, intersect` with 3 layers — per spec the first layer's composite value is ignored (nothing below it), values apply from layer 2 onward; fewer values than layers means the last repeats. So 2 values for 3 layers = correct.\n- `-webkit-mask-composite: source-in, source-in` — the prior 2-layer version used a single `source-in` and worked; extending with one value per subsequent layer follows the same convention. Correct and consistent.\n- Both prefixes updated on the img AND the container mask edits updated both `mask-image` and `-webkit-mask-image` at line ~473. Good.\n\n**2. Class collisions**\n- `.dust-cloud` and `.ember` are global selectors. No existing usages elsewhere in the codebase (searches earlier only showed `.spark` used on index.html). Low risk, but scoping to `.hero-visual .ember` would be safer hygiene given how generic these names are.\n\n**3. Container mask change effects on sweeps/sparks**\n- The container mask widened from 30%→70% to 48%→86%. The sweeps animate left:-60%→160% — they were previously clipped at 70%, now visible through 86%. The sweep bar is a hard-edged gradient bar moving across; the new fade boundary will slice it mid-travel. At 0.10–0.45 alpha with overlay blend this is subtle, but the sweep's trailing edge could look slightly \"cut\" where it crosses the fade. The verification screenshot showed the beam looking good, so this is minor.\n- Sparks/embers near the right (left:80%) spawn inside the 48→86 fade zone → partially masked at spawn. Works in favor of the dissolve effect, but worth knowing the farthest ember is dimmer than the others.\n\n**4. Cascade conflicts**\n- The 1742 `.hero-visual` rule doesn't redeclare mask-image, so the 473 rule (now edited) still applies — correct approach, no conflict.\n- The 1655 media query `.hero-visual img { max-width:60%; max-height:40vh }` is overridden by the later 1783 rule — pre-existing behavior, unchanged.\n- `transform: translateY(3vh)` doesn't conflict with the hover rule (opacity/filter only). Good.\n\n**Real issues to flag:**\n- translateY(3vh) on a 95vh image inside `.hero { overflow:hidden }` (min-height:100vh, 40px padding, border-box): the visual is ~95vh tall in a ~92vh content area, so the bottom already overflows; +3vh paint shift clips a bit more at the hero boundary. Acceptable since the bottom dissolves, but on short viewports the hooves/dust may clip harder. A smaller shift or bottom-anchoring would be safer.\n- Hover `animation-duration` change (10s→6.5s) can cause a visible timing jump for embers mid-flight — same pattern as the existing `.spark` hover rule, so consistent with approved behavior, just noting it.\n- Inline positioning/sizing for dust/ember elements (left/top/delay in HTML) matches the existing `.spark` convention — fine, just fragile if someone removes the inline styles.\n- `.dust-cloud` uses `mix-blend-mode: screen` at z-index:0 behind the bull — blends with the page background, not the bull. Intentional-looking; fine.\n\nOverall the changes are correct and verified. Concise feedback below.</think>\n\nThe changes look good and the multi-layer mask syntax is correct. A few minor notes:\n\n1. **Scoping**: `.dust-cloud` and `.ember` are global selectors; no current collisions, but scoping them as `.hero-visual .ember` / `.hero-visual .dust-cloud` would be safer hygiene for such generic names.\n\n2. **translateY(3vh) clipping**: the img is 95vh in a hero with `overflow: hidden` and a ~92vh content area (100vh − 80px padding, border-box) — the bull's bottom already overflows; the 3vh paint shift clips a little more at the hero boundary on short viewports. Acceptable since the bottom dissolves anyway, but a smaller shift would be safer.\n\n3. **Sweep clipping**: widening the container mask (30→70 to 48→86) means the sweeping gold bar is now sliced by the fade boundary mid-travel instead of being clipped at the old 70% edge — subtle at its low alpha, and the screenshot looked fine, but worth a glance on desktop.\n\n4. **Rightmost ember**: embers at `left:80%` spawn inside the mask fade zone, so they render dimmer than the others — works in favor of the dissolve, just noting the asymmetry.\n\nMask-composite `intersect, intersect` / `source-in, source-in` is spec-correct (first layer's value is ignored; rest intersect with the accumulated result), both prefixes are updated on all edited rules, and the cascade order (473 base mask edited in place, 1742 block doesn't redeclare it) is right."}]},{"kind":"reasoning","id":"p26-37c49ef6","text":"The reviewer confirms everything is correct with minor notes:\n1. Scoping `.dust-cloud`/`.ember` to `.hero-visual` — good hygiene. Worth doing since these are generic names. Cheap fix.\n2. translateY(3vh) clip risk — acceptable, bottom dissolves anyway.\n3. Sweep slicing — minor, screenshot looked fine.\n4. Rightmost ember dimmer — works in favor of the dissolve.\n\nThe ember animation is confirmed live (animating: true, ember-flow running, opacity changing 0.16→0.13 over 300ms).\n\nLet me apply the scoping fix (cheap, avoids future collisions) — change `.dust-cloud` → `.hero-visual .dust-cloud` and `.ember` → `.hero-visual .ember`. Wait, but the hover rule is already `.hero-visual:hover .ember` — scoped. The base rules `.dust-cloud` and `.ember` → scope them. And keyframes stay global (fine). Let me do that edit.\n\nActually — the CSS defines `.spark` globally too (existing convention). But scoping the new ones is strictly better and costs nothing. Let me apply.\n\nThen I'm done — summarize. Let me make the scoping edit and mark todos complete.","open":false,"collapse":"preview"},{"kind":"text","text":"Reviewer confirms everything is spec-correct and the ember animation is confirmed live. Let me apply the one worthwhile suggestion — scoping the new generic class names to `.hero-visual` for collision safety:"},{"kind":"tool","id":"0paeai2nd0E","toolName":"str_replace","input":{"path":"REALITY-FOREX-TRADING-/css/style.css","replacements":[{"oldString":"/* ── Golden dust storm — the bull emerges from a cloud of energy ── */\n.dust-cloud {\n  position: absolute;","newString":"/* ── Golden dust storm — the bull emerges from a cloud of energy ── */\n.hero-visual .dust-cloud {\n  position: absolute;","allowMultiple":false},{"oldString":"/* Dust particles flowing backward off the bull's rear */\n.ember {\n  position: absolute;","newString":"/* Dust particles flowing backward off the bull's rear */\n.hero-visual .ember {\n  position: absolute;","allowMultiple":false}]}},{"kind":"text","text":"\n"},{"kind":"reasoning","id":"p27-37c49ef6","text":"Done. Let me update the todos and write the final summary.","open":false,"collapse":"preview"},{"kind":"tool","id":"0pcuerDUI10","toolName":"write_todos","input":{"todos":[{"task":"Add dust clouds + backward-flowing ember particles to index.html hero","completed":true},{"task":"CSS: dissolve bull's rear (right side) via mask, soften container mask, lower bull slightly","completed":true},{"task":"CSS: dust-cloud billow + ember-flow animations with hover speed-up","completed":true},{"task":"Verify in preview + code review","completed":true}]}},{"kind":"reasoning","id":"p28-37c49ef6","text":"All done. Let me write the final summary — concise. Then suggest follow-ups.\n\nThe summary should cover:\n- Chose Scenario B (bull emerging from dust) — explained why\n- What was implemented:\n  1. Rear (right side) dissolves into golden dust via a third mask layer\n  2. 3 blurred golden dust clouds billowing behind the bull\n  3. 6 ember particles streaming backward (drifting right/up, fading as they dissolve)\n  4. Bull lowered slightly (3vh) + shadow grounded to bottom\n  5. Container mask softened so the dissolve is visible\n  6. Hover: embers stream faster\n- Verified: bull renders golden, charging left, particles glowing, animations running; code reviewed, scoped classes\n- Preview note: preview shows mobile viewport so bull appears below content — on desktop it's the right-side hero visual.\n\nFollow-ups: suggest things like applying the same dust treatment to other pages, or the watch-our-story button behavior, or making the bull concept reusable, etc. Per instructions, end with suggest_prompts with varied follow-ups.","open":false,"collapse":"preview"},{"kind":"text","text":"**Scenario B is live!** 🐂✨ The bull now emerges from a golden dust storm — verified rendering in the preview: golden metallic bull charging left, glowing particles drifting around it, light sweep crossing. Here's what went in:\n\n## 1. The rear dissolves into dust (the core of Scenario B)\nAdded a **third mask layer** to the bull image: `linear-gradient(to right, black 60%, transparent 97%)` — the head and charging chest stay solid, while the back/body *gradually transforms into flowing dust* toward the right, exactly like the concept. I also softened the container's edge mask (30→70% → 48→86%) so the dissolve is actually visible instead of being clipped early.\n\n## 2. The dust storm itself\n- **3 blurred golden dust clouds** billowing behind/around the bull's rear (slow 11s scale-and-drift loops, `screen` blend so they glow)\n- **6 ember particles** streaming *backward* — they rise and drift to the right (behind the charging bull), brightening then dissolving to nothing as they cross into the mask fade, which sells the \"transformed into particles\" look\n\n## 3. Grounding touches\n- The bull sits **3vh lower** (the \"positioned lower\" feel from the mockup) and its grounding shadow now anchors at the page bottom\n- **Hover**: the whole storm warms up — the embers stream faster (10s → 6.5s), matching the spark speed-up you already approved\n\n**Verified:** ember-flow and dust-billow animations confirmed running live, the 3-layer mask applies correctly (both standard and `-webkit` prefixes), and the code review passed — I scoped the new classes to `.hero-visual` as a safety measure. One note: the preview window is phone-sized so the bull stacks below the text there — on desktop it's the right-side hero visual with the dust trailing off the right edge.\n\nThe whole effect builds on what you loved — gold sweeps, breathing glow, sparks — and now tells the \"emerging from the storm\" story from your concept."},{"kind":"tool","id":"0peCdZf44c0","toolName":"suggest_prompts","input":{"prompts":[{"prompt":"The preview showed the hero in a narrow phone viewport — can you check the bull and dust storm look right on mobile widths and adjust the ember/dust positions so the effect survives the responsive layout?","label":"Dust storm on mobile too"},{"prompt":"The golden dust storm looks amazing in the hero — could we carry a subtle version of the particle + dust language into the operation meter section or the ticker strip for a cohesive feel?","label":"Add dust particles to the homepage ticker/meter"},{"prompt":"I'd also like to see Scenario A (cleaner tight crop, no dust) as an alternative — could you make it a simple toggle in the CSS so I can preview both looks and pick?","label":"Scenario A variant"}]}},{"kind":"changes","id":"p29-37c49ef6","files":[{"path":"REALITY-FOREX-TRADING-\\css\\style.css","status":"modified","lockfile":false,"binary":false,"adds":85,"dels":8,"patch":"Index: REALITY-FOREX-TRADING-\\css\\style.css\n===================================================================\n--- REALITY-FOREX-TRADING-\\css\\style.css\tbefore\n+++ REALITY-FOREX-TRADING-\\css\\style.css\tafter\n@@ -475,8 +475,9 @@\n   justify-content: center;\r\n   align-items: center;\r\n   position: relative;\r\n-  mask-image: radial-gradient(ellipse at center, black 30%, transparent 70%);\r\n-  -webkit-mask-image: radial-gradient(ellipse at center, black 30%, transparent 70%);\r\n+  /* Generous edge fade — leaves room for the rear to dissolve into dust */\r\n+  mask-image: radial-gradient(ellipse at 52% 55%, black 48%, transparent 86%);\r\n+  -webkit-mask-image: radial-gradient(ellipse at 52% 55%, black 48%, transparent 86%);\r\n }\r\n \r\n .hero-visual img {\r\n@@ -1767,7 +1768,7 @@\n .hero-visual::after {\r\n   content: '';\r\n   position: absolute;\r\n-  bottom: 4%;\r\n+  bottom: 0%;\r\n   left: 50%;\r\n   width: 58%;\r\n   height: 7%;\r\n@@ -1788,16 +1789,20 @@\n   height: auto;\r\n   opacity: 0.65;\r\n   filter: drop-shadow(0 0 45px rgba(201, 162, 39, 0.18));\r\n+  transform: translateY(3vh);\r\n   transition: opacity 0.5s ease, filter 0.5s ease;\r\n-  /* Blend the bottom (hooves / dust) into the page while keeping edges soft */\r\n+  /* Bottom (hooves) melts into the page; the REAR (right side) dissolves\r\n+     into golden dust as the bull charges forward */\r\n   mask-image:\r\n     radial-gradient(ellipse at center, black 45%, transparent 92%),\r\n-    linear-gradient(to top, transparent 0%, black 20%);\r\n+    linear-gradient(to top, transparent 0%, black 20%),\r\n+    linear-gradient(to right, black 60%, transparent 97%);\r\n   -webkit-mask-image:\r\n     radial-gradient(ellipse at center, black 45%, transparent 92%),\r\n-    linear-gradient(to top, transparent 0%, black 20%);\r\n-  mask-composite: intersect;\r\n-  -webkit-mask-composite: source-in;\r\n+    linear-gradient(to top, transparent 0%, black 20%),\r\n+    linear-gradient(to right, black 60%, transparent 97%);\r\n+  mask-composite: intersect, intersect;\r\n+  -webkit-mask-composite: source-in, source-in;\r\n }\r\n \r\n /* ── Hover — the bull brightens and its energy warms up ── */\r\n@@ -1908,6 +1913,78 @@\n   }\r\n }\r\n \r\n+/* ── Golden dust storm — the bull emerges from a cloud of energy ── */\r\n+.hero-visual .dust-cloud {\r\n+  position: absolute;\r\n+  border-radius: 50%;\r\n+  background: radial-gradient(circle,\r\n+      rgba(201, 162, 39, 0.16),\r\n+      rgba(201, 162, 39, 0.05) 55%,\r\