old blobs — these are part of the dust storm, not really \"glow behind the bull\". But they do glow. Hmm. The user said \"remove the glow on the hero all of it\". But the dust clouds were the NEW feature they wanted (Scenario B). The screenshot shows \"glowing gold-and-black illustration... kicking up dust/sparks... dark, smoky background with vertical light rays\". The \"vertical light rays\" might be the sweeps (skewed) or the dust. I think the dust clouds should STAY — they're the storm, and the user liked them. The \"glow\" refers to the ambient halo/aura behind the bull (::before + drop-shadow) that makes the bull look like it's sitting in a golden fog.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. \"Remove the glow on the hero all of it\" — the cleanest interpretation: the ::before breathing glow and the img drop-shadow halo. Those are the \"glow\". The sparks/embers/dust stay (they're particles, not glow). On hover, the glow returns (::before reappears + stronger drop-shadow).\n\nImplementation:\n- `.hero-visual::before`: set opacity: 0 by default; on `.hero-visual:hover::before` set opacity back (e.g. 0.75 or 1) — but wait, the ::before has `animation: bull-breathe 7s` which animates opacity 0.75→1. If I set `opacity: 0` on the element, the animation overrides it (animations override non-!important styles during the animation). So I should remove the animation from default state, or use the animation only on hover.\n\nApproach A: Remove the `animation` and `background` from `::before` by default; on hover, add them back. But ::before always has content:'' — with no background it's invisible anyway. So:\n```css\n.hero-visual::before {\n  content: '';\n  position: absolute;\n  width: 85%;\n  height: 85%;\n  border-radius: 50%;\n  background: radial-gradient(circle at center, rgba(201,162,39,0.18), rgba(201,162,39,0.05) 45%, transparent 70%);\n  filter: blur(12px);\n  opacity: 0;            /* hidden by default — the sweep must stand out */\n  animation: none;\n  transition: