h. Let me measure with the actual styles. In the preview, remove my injected styles temporarily? That's fiddly. Instead, let me compute what the gap is on DESKTOP by measuring the real CSS values (min-height, padding) and the content height in the preview (content heights are roughly viewport-independent... the bull is 95vh which scales with viewport height).\n\nDesktop (1440×900, typical):\n- nav height ≈ 1.2rem×2 + 26px ≈ 65px (fixed, overlay)\n- ticker: margin-top 60px → occupies y 60 → height = 8px×2 padding + widget height. The TradingView ticker widget renders ~40-46px tall. So ticker ≈ y 60-110.\n- hero starts y=110. min-height 100vh (900px) → hero box 110-1010 (clipped at page bottom... whatever). padding 40/40, border-box → content box height 900−80=820... wait, hero min-height 100vh = 900px INCLUDING padding (border-box). content box = 900−80 = 820.\n- hero-grid height: text col ~470px; bull col: img max-height 95vh=855 → the visual box = 855px tall (the img defines it). Grid = max(470, 855) = 855px. This EXCEEDS the content box (820) — flexbox: the hero is display:flex; the container hero-grid is the flex item. Flex item min-height auto... The container has width 100%. Its height = grid content height 855 (it's a grid with fixed 855 row). The hero flex container align-items: center — the flex item (hero-grid) is 855 tall, centered in content box 820 → overflows 17.5px top and bottom (hero overflow hidden clips the overflow). Grid top = 110 + 40 − 17.5 ≈ 132.5.\n- Text top ≈ 132.5 + small offset. Ticker bottom ≈ 110. Gap ≈ 22px.\n\nThat's NOT far at all (~22px). So my model must be wrong somewhere.\n\nAlternative model: the ticker widget renders TALLER or the margin-top creates the gap differently. OR the hero-grid height is much less than 95vh: maybe max-height 95vh is relative to the hero-visual's height, and the visual is sized by the grid... circular. In the preview, img rendered 251px tall at vw=320 → 95vh of 264px viewport = 251. Yes 95vh. On desktop