measure the real desktop gap.\n\nLet me step back and reason from the CSS + the user's screenshot:\n\nIn the user's screenshot (desktop), between the nav and the hero content, the ticker IS present (they say the gap is between the tradingview widget and the hero section). \n\nThe structure:\n```\n<nav> fixed, ~64px tall, transparent\n<div class=\"ticker-strip\" style=\"margin-top:60px\">  ← in normal flow\n<section class=\"hero\"> padding: 40px 0; min-height: 100vh; flex centered\n```\n\nGap between ticker bottom and hero content = hero padding-top (40px) + flex centering offset.\n\nThe flex centering: hero is min-height 100vh, content box = 100vh − 80px. The grid is as tall as the bull column (img max-height 95vh = 0.95 × viewportHeight). If viewport is 900px: bull = 855px, content box = 820px. Grid (855) > content box (820) → overflow 35px total → 17.5 top/bottom. Grid top = nav_bottom(≈64) + ticker(≈60+52=112... let me compute: ticker top = 60 (margin) → but nav is fixed transparent, so ticker at y=60, height ≈ 8+widget(~40)+8 ≈ 56 → ticker bottom ≈ 116) + hero top (116) + padding-top 40 − 17.5 ≈ 138.5. Text top ≈ 138.5. Gap = 138.5 − 116 = 22.5px.\n\nHmm, that's small. UNLESS the grid is smaller than I think. If the bull column's img height isn't 95vh but smaller... Say the visual is ~70vh tall (630px). Then content box 820, grid 630 → centered: grid top = 116 + 40 + (820−630)/2 = 116 + 40 + 95 = 251. Gap = 251 − 116 = 135px. THAT matches the screenshot's big gap! So the grid must be smaller than the content box → the centering creates ~100-150px of empty space.\n\nWhy would the visual be smaller than 95vh? `max-height: 95vh` is a MAX — the actual img height is determined by... the img's width! `max-width: 176%` — width relative to the visual box width. If the visual box is ~600px wide (half of 1280 container minus gap ≈ 600), img width = 176% of 600 = 1056px, height = 1056/1.5 (1536:1024 ratio) = 704px. 704 < 95vh (855) → so the img is 704px tall, NOT 855! The img height is width-driven (704px), not max-height driven. So the visual/grid ≈ 704px (plus text column 470 < 704).\n\nSo: content box 820, grid 704 → centering offset = (820−704)/2 = 58. Grid top = 116 + 40 + 58 = 214. Gap = 214 − 116 = 98px. Plus the ticker's own height... the gap between ticker bottom and text top ≈ 98px. \"A bit far\" ✓.\n\nAlso the bull column height (704px) with the img centered in it. The text column (470px) is centered too. The visible \"gap\" = 98px of empty space between the ticker's bottom border and the text/bull top.\n\nTo close the gap, I need to reduce the vertical empty space above the content:\n1. Reduce hero min-height: 100vh → e.g. calc(100vh − 60px). Content box = 900−60−80 = 760... hmm wait: min-height: calc(100vh - 60px) = 840 → content box = 840−80 = 760 → offset = (760−704)/2 = 28 → grid top = 116+40+28 = 184 → gap = 68px. Better but still some.\n2. Also reduce padding-top: 40px → 16px. Content box = 840−16−40 = 784 → offset = (784−704)/2 = 40 → grid top = 116+16+40 = 172 → gap = 56px.\n3. Reduce padding-bottom too: padding: 16px 0 40px keeps some bottom room.\n\nAlternatively — set the hero to not center but let the bull extend: since the bull (704px) nearly fills the content box anyway, the cleanest is to reduce min-height so the hero hugs the content + a little breathing room, and reduce padding-top.\n\nLet me pick: \n- `.hero { min-height: 100vh; padding: 40px 0 }` → `min-height: calc(100vh - 40px); padding: 16px 0 48px;`\n\nHmm, wait — but actually, there's a subtlety: the ticker's margin-top 60px is INLINE. The gap I want to reduce is BELOW the ticker. The hero top padding is the lever. And min-height.\n\nActually — hold on. Let me reconsider whether the bull/img is really width-driven at 704px. The screenshot: the bull \"occupies roughly the right half of the viewport below the header\" and the SCROLL indicator at the very bottom. If the grid were only 704px centered in 820, the bull would end at ~214+704=918 (below viewport 900 — clipped). SCROLL at bottom 40px (absolute in hero) = y 860. The bull occupies from ~214 down past 900. So the right half below the header is filled with the bull from y~214 down. The text (470px) centered: text top = 214 + (704−470)/2 ≈ 214+117 = 331. Hmm, that puts \"Premium Trading Education\" at y~331 — but the screenshot shows the text starting quite high, just below the nav...\n\nThe screenshot description: \"Small uppercase tagline at the top: PREMIUM TRADING EDUCATION\" — it's near the top of the hero section. If text starts at 331 out of 900, that's 37% down — \"near the top-ish\". Eh. And the vision model said the headline/paragraph follow. The bull starts around the same height.\n\nOK whatever — the exact numbers don't matter that much. The user wants the gap CLOSED. Plan:\n\n**Gap fix:**\n1. `.hero`: `padding: 40px 0` → `padding: 12px 0 48px` and `min-height: 100vh` → `min-height: calc(100vh - 40px)`. This reduces the empty space above the content by ~40-70px on desktop. \n\nHmm, wait — but will reducing min-height make the hero too short / move the SCROLL indicator? The scroll indicator is absolute bottom:40px in .hero — hero is ~860px tall, indicator at 860−40=820 (visible). Fine.\n\nBut hold on — I want to double check the min-height change doesn't hurt OTHER pages: other pages override `.hero` min-height inline (50-70vh) — no effect. Only home page. ✓\n\nAlso — should I touch the ticker's inline margin-top: 60px? That's the gap between NAV and TICKER. The user said \"gap between the trading view widget and the hero section\" — the widget and the hero. So the relevant gap is below the ticker. BUT — hmm, wait, re-read once more: \"the gap between the trading view widget and the hero section is a bit far\". \n\nHmm — could they mean: between the widget and the hero — the widget (ticker) sits right below the nav, and the HERO section starts further down? The hero section = the section containing the text and bull. The gap = hero padding-top +