"hidden\" name=\"_subject\" value=\"New Portfolio Assessment Request — Reality FX\">\r\n+          <input type=\"hidden\" name=\"_gotcha\">\r\n \r\n           <!-- Progress Bar -->\r\n           <div class=\"progress-bar\">\r\n","tooLarge":false}]}],"ts":1785949886401},{"role":"user","parts":[{"kind":"text","text":"Oh i thought that users will be able to take forms and be able to stay on the reality fx site, isn't that possible for free?\n\nIf not then i guess you next best suggestion."}],"ts":1785950041517},{"role":"assistant","parts":[{"kind":"reasoning","id":"p1-a9a5c4b6","text":"The user asks: can users stay on the Reality FX site after submitting a Formspree form, for free?\n\nThe answer: YES — Formspree supports AJAX submission. You can intercept the form submit with JavaScript (fetch POST), prevent the default page navigation, and show a success message on the page — all without leaving the site. This works on the FREE tier. This is the standard \"AJAX Formspree\" approach:\n\n```js\nform.addEventListener('submit', async (e) => {\n  e.preventDefault();\n  const form = e.currentTarget;\n  const data = new FormData(form);\n  const res = await fetch(form.action, {\n    method: 'POST',\n    body: data,\n    headers: { 'Accept': 'application/json' }\n  });\n  if (res.ok) {\n    // show success message, reset form\n  } else {\n    // show error\n  }\n});\n```\n\nFormspree's AJAX API: POST to the same form action URL with `Accept: application/json` header — returns JSON `{ ok: true }` or error. This is free and officially supported by Formspree.\n\nSo yes — it's possible for free. I should implement it: add a small JS handler in main.js that intercepts all 4 forms' submissions, sends them via fetch to Formspree, and on success shows an inline success message (staying on the page) + resets the form. On error, shows an error message.\n\nDesign decisions:\n1. **Where**: main.js — a generic handler for all forms with `action` containing `formspree.io`. This keeps it DRY and applies to all 4 forms at once. No HTML changes needed (the forms already have the action URLs).\n\n2. **Success UI**: Show an inline success message. Options:\n   - A success message div inserted/revealed above the submit button or replacing the form content.\n   - Per-form success message text.\n   \n   Clean approach: after a successful submit, hide the form and show a styled success message (\"Thank you! Your submission has been received...\"). Or simpler: show a success banner at the top of the form and reset fields.\n\n   I'll create a reusable success panel: a div with class `form-success` that's inserted after the form, hidden by default; on success, hide the form (or just the success shows) and scroll it into view. Text varies per form:\n   - careers job: \"Application submitted! Thanks for applying...\"\n   - careers general: \"Thanks for joining our talent pool...\"\n   - contact: \"Message sent! We'll get back to you...\"\n   - evaluation: \"Assessment request received! Our team will contact you within 24 hours...\"\n\n   To keep it generic, I can build the message from the form's name attribute or a data attribute. Simplest: add `data-success` attribute? That requires HTML edits. OR generate a generic message based on form name. Let me use a per-form message map in JS keyed by form name — no HTML changes needed:\n   ```js\n   const successMessages = {\n     'job-application': 'Application received — thank you for applying...',\n     'general-application': 'Thanks for joining our talent pool...',\n     'contact': 'Message sent — we will get back to you shortly.',\n     'evaluation': 'Assessment request received — our team will contact you within 24 hours.'\n   };\n   ```\n\n3. **Behavior**:\n   - On submit: prevent default, fetch POST FormData to form.action with Accept: application/json.\n   - Disable the submit button while sending (prevent double-submit).\n   - On ok: hide the form, show success panel (with a nice classy animation matching the site theme), scroll into view.\n   - On error: show an error message (or alert), re-enable the button.\n\n4. **Styling**: Add `.form-success` CSS — a premium card: gold-bordered, check icon, centered. Match the site's dark theme (the forms are on dark sections). Some forms sit on cream? No — all forms are on dark sections (careers, contact, evaluation all dark). Style: glass card with gold border, gold check icon, heading + text.\n\n5. **The evaluation multi-step form**: On success, hide the whole form (including progress bar). The success panel replaces it. ✓ The submit button is in step 4 — fine.\n\n6. **Validation**: keep the browser's native validation — the submit event only fires if validation passes (required fields). ✓\n\n7. **File uploads via AJAX**: FormData with a file input — fetch POST with FormData handles file upload automatically (multipart). Formspree AJAX supports file uploads. ✓ (The Accept: application/json header doesn't interfere with multipart.)\n\nLet me implement:\n\n**main.js** — add after the scroll indicator section (or near the end before the closing). A generic handler:\n\n```js\n// ── Formspree Forms — submit in the background, stay on the site ──\nconst successMessages = {\n  'job-application': 'Application submitted — thank you for applying to Reality FX. Our team will be in touch soon.',\n  'general-application': 'Thanks for joining our talent pool — we will reach out when the right role opens.',\n  'contact': 'Message sent — thank you for getting in touch. We will respond shortly.',\n  'evaluation': 'Assessment request received — our team will review your profile and contact you within 24 hours.'\n};\n\ndocument.querySelectorAll('form[action*=\"formspree.io\"]').forEach(form => {\n  form.addEventListener('submit', async function (e) {\n    e.preventDefault();\n    const submitBtn = form.querySelector('button[type=\"submit\"]');\n    const originalText = submitBtn ? submitBtn.textContent : '';\n    if (submitBtn) {\n      submitBtn.disabled = true;\n      submitBtn.textContent = 'Sending…';\n    }\n\n    try {\n      const response = await fetch(form.action, {\n        method: 'POST',\n        body: new FormData(form),\n        headers: { 'Accept': 'application/json' }\n      });\n\n      if (response.ok) {\n        const panel = document.createElement('div');\n        panel.className = 'form-success';\n        panel.innerHTML = `\n          <svg width=\"52\" height=\"52\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#C9A227\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\n            <circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\" />\n            <polyline points=\"8 12.5 11 15.5 16 9.5\" />\n          </svg>\n          <h3>Thank You</h3>\n          <p>${successMessages[form.name] || 'Your submission has been received. We will be in touch soon.'}</p>\n        `;\n        form.style.display = 'none';\n        form.insertAdjacentElement('afterend', panel);\n        panel.scrollIntoView({ behavior: 'smooth', block: 'center' });\n      } else {\n        throw new Error('Formspree error');\n      }\n    } catch (err) {\n      if (submitBtn) {\n        submitBtn.disabled = false;\n        submitBtn.textContent = originalText;\n      }\n      const errorBox = form.querySelector('.form-error') || document.createElement('div');\n      errorBox.className = 'form-error';\n      errorBox.textContent = 'Something went wrong. Please try again, or reach us directly on WhatsApp.';\n      form.prepend(errorBox);\n    }\n  });\n});\n```\n\nWait — one issue: `panel.innerHTML` with the message from a map — the messages are static strings I control, no injection risk. But `form.name` — I use successMessages[form.name] — safe.\n\nAlso — the `.form-error` prepend on each retry would stack if user retries twice. Guard: `form.querySelector('.form-error') || document.createElement('div')` — if exists, reuse. OK.\n\n**CSS** — add `.form-success` and `.form-error`:\n\n```css\n/* ── Form success / error states ── */\n.form-success {\n  text-align: center;\n  padding: 3rem 2rem;\n  background: linear-gradient(145deg, #111, #0a0a0a);\n  border: 1px solid var(--border-subtle);\n  border-radius: 20px;\n  animation: fadeIn 0.5s ease;\n}\n.form-success svg { margin-bottom: 1rem; }\n.form-success h3 {\n  color: var(--accent-gold);\n  font-size: 1.5rem;\n  margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n}\n.form-success p { margin: 0 auto; max-width: 480px; }\n.form-error {\n  background: rgba(201, 39, 39, 0.12);\n  border: 1px solid rgba(201, 39, 39, 0.35);\n  color: #e58a8a;\n  padding: 0.9rem 1.2rem;\n  border-radius: 10px;\n  margin-bottom: 1.5rem;\n  font-size: 0.9rem;\n}\n```\n\n`fadeIn` keyframes — is it defined? Line 815 mentioned `animation: fadeIn 0.6s ease;` — so fadeIn exists in the CSS already. ✓ \n\nThe gold color #C9A227 hardcoded in the SVG stroke — could use currentColor + color var. Cleaner: use `stroke=\"currentColor\"` and set `color: var(--accent-gold)` on the svg. Let me do that.\n\nNow — the user asked \"isn't that possible for free?\" — I should answer: YES, absolutely free — Formspree's AJAX API. Explain briefly, then implement it. That's the best next step (better than the `_next` redirect suggestion — staying on-site is even nicer).\n\nAlso mention: this works on the free plan, no upgrade needed.\n\nLet me also double check the submit button text reset on success — on success the form is hidden so no reset needed. On error, restore.\n\nOne more consideration — the evaluation form's submit button is in step 4 and has class `btn btn-primary btn-large`. Disabling + text change works.\n\nAlso — should the success panel replace the form visually (display:none) — yes, clean.\n\nEdge: `form.name` — the forms have name attributes (job-application, general-application, contact, evaluation). ✓\n\nLet me also think about whether to prevent double-submit on the file-upload forms (CV) — the disabled button handles it.\n\nNow implement:\n1. main.js — add the handler block (after the scroll indicator block, before the Active Nav Link section).\n2. style.css — add .form-success / .form-error styles (append near the forms section or at the end of the careers form styles — I'll append after the careers-form block; actually simplest: add at the end of the file or near other form styles. Let me add after the `.careers-form` media query block or somewhere logical. I'll append near the end of the file to be safe — but the file has media queries at the end... Let me add it right after the multi-step/progress form CSS... I don't know exactly where that is. Simplest safe: append at the very end of style.css. But wait — the file's last block is the coming-soon styles at ~2195+. Appending at the end is fine (global styles).\n\nActually — hmm, adding at the end of style.css means after all media queries — fine for these new classes (no media overrides needed).\n\nLet me verify where main.js should get the block — after the scroll indicator block (which ends before \"Active Nav Link\"). The anchor for the str_replace in main.js: the end of the scroll indicator block:\n\n```js\n    scrollIndicator.addEventListener('keydown', (e) => {\n      if (e.key === 'Enter' || e.key === ' ') {\n        e.preventDefault();\n        scrollDown();\n      }\n    });\n  }\n```\n\nThen next is:\n```js\n  // ── Active Nav Link ──\n```\n\nI'll insert between them.\n\nFor style.css, I need the exact end of file or a good anchor. Let me read the last ~30 lines of style.css to find a safe append point. Actually — I can anchor on the coming-soon block end or just read the tail. Let me read the tail of style.css.\n\nActually, let me do it efficiently: read the tail, then apply both edits.","open":false,"collapse":"preview"},{"kind":"ad","id":"p2-a9a5c4b6","ad":{"adText":"Ensure your luxury finance site is inclusive and compliant with Accessibly’s easy-to-install accessibility tools.","title":"Reduce Legal Risk & Support All Visitors","cta":"Boost Accessibility","url":"https://dash.accessiblyapp.com/register?ref=zach46","favicon":"https: