 Compliance Statement\") to use the same font as the summary — i.e., the body font (Inter), not the serif Playfair.\n\nThe simplest fix: change `.disclaimer h4` font-family from `var(--font-heading)` to `var(--font-body)`. That applies to all h4s in all disclaimers (Terms & Conditions + Refund Policy) — consistent across all 8 pages via the shared CSS.\n\nShould I keep the gold color? The user said they love the overall look. They only want the font changed. The summary is `--text-muted` color (gray). The h4s are gold. If I change only the font, the h4s remain gold serif→Inter. Hmm — \"font should change to the one that you used in Risk Disclaimer\" — font specifically. So keep gold color, just change family to Inter.\n\nBut wait — should the headings also become uppercase and smaller like the summary? The user said \"font\" — the font family. Let me just change the font-family to match. I could also align weight/size slightly, but minimal change: change `font-family` from `var(--font-heading)` to `var(--font-body)`. Maybe also uppercase to look like the summary style? The user said \"font should change to the one that you used\" — I'll interpret it as the font family (Inter). To be safe and make it feel cohesive, I'll change font-family to var(--font-body) and maybe uppercase + smaller size to truly match the summary treatment. Hmm, but they said \"i love what you did overall\" — so don't overdo. Let me make the h4s use the body font but keep the gold color and maybe slightly adjust to look like the summary font style (uppercase, letter-spacing). Actually, the summary style is: Inter, 0.8rem, weight 600, letter-spacing 0.1em, uppercase.\n\nThe user explicitly compared to the Risk Disclaimer — which is the summary. So the safest interpretation: make the h4s look like the summary font (Inter, uppercase, letter-spaced) but keep them gold so they still stand out. That seems like what they want — a consistent font language. But \"font should change to the one that you used\" — most literally the font family. If I make them uppercase, they'd look like the summary which might be what they want for cohesion.\n\nLet me do a middle path: font-family Inter, keep gold color, uppercase with letter-spacing to mirror the summary, size ~0.8rem, weight 600. That matches the summary treatment closely while staying gold. Actually, let me reconsider — making them uppercase might be too aggressive a change from \"just the font\". But the user said \"the one that you used in 'Risk Disclaimer'\" — the visual identity of the Risk Disclaimer text IS uppercase Inter with letterspacing. I'll go with the full treatment but keep gold.\n\nHmm, let me keep it simple and safe: change font-family to var(--font-body) AND apply uppercase + letter-spacing so it reads like the summary font. If the user only wanted family, uppercase small text still looks classy and gold. I think that's the right call — the summary's distinctive look is the uppercase letter-spaced styling.\n\nWait — actually, re-reading: \"Business Transparency & Compliance Statement font should change to the one that you used in 'Risk Disclaimer'\". The Risk Disclaimer text (the summary label) is Inter uppercase. So making the section headers match means Inter, uppercase, letter-spaced, 0.8rem, weight 600. Gold color retained (they love the gold look). Let me do that.\n\nEdit the `.disclaimer h4` rule:\n\n```css\n.disclaimer h4 {\n  font-family: var(--font-heading);\n  font-size: 0.9rem;\n  font-weight: 600;\n  letter-spacing: 0.04em;\n  color: var(--accent-gold-light);\n  margin-top: 1.2rem;\n  margin-bottom: 0.35rem;\n}\n```\n\nbecomes:\n\n```css\n.disclaimer h4 {\n  font-family: var(--font-body);\n  font-size: 0.8rem;\n  font-weight: 600;\n  letter-spacing: 0.1em;\n  text-transform: uppercase;\n  color: var(--accent-gold-light);\n  margin-top: 1.2rem;\n  margin-bottom: 0.35rem;\n}\n```\n\nThis is a single tiny CSS change affecting all pages. No HTML changes needed. Quick verify with a grep and maybe preview. Then a short reply. No code review needed for a one-line-ish CSS tweak (extremely straightforward). I'll do a quick verification via grep and preview evaluate.","open":false,"collapse":"preview"},{"kind":"text","text":"One tiny CSS change — the h4 headers will match the Risk Disclaimer's font treatment (Inter, uppercase, letter-spaced) while staying gold:"},{"kind":"tool","id":"0z_hlbNGM0I","toolName":"str_replace","input":{"path":"REALITY-FOREX-TRADING-/css/style.css","replacements":[{"oldString":".disclaimer h4 {\n  font-family: var(--font-heading);\n  font-size: 0.9rem;\n  font-weight: 600;\n  letter-spacing: 0.04em;\n  color: var(--accent-gold-light);\n  margin-top: 1.2rem;\n  margin-bottom: 0.35rem;\n}","newString":".disclaimer h4 {\n  font-family: var(--font-body);\n  font-size: 0.8rem;\n  font-weight: 600;\n  letter-spacing: 0.1em;\n  text-transform: uppercase;\n  color: var(--accent-gold-light);\n  margin-top: 1.2rem;\n  margin-bottom: 0.35rem;\n}","allowMultiple":false}]}},{"kind":"text","text":"\n"},{"kind":"agent","id":"00Ax06xQZhM","agentType":"basher","displayName":"Basher","status":"done","blocks":[{"kind":"tool","id":"50e342b9-aa4f-4336-a1a5-937ce80929c7","toolName":"run_terminal_command","input":{"command":"sed -n '/.disclaimer h4 {/,/^}/p' REALITY-FOREX-TRADING-/css/style.css","process_type":"SYNC","timeout_seconds":30}}]},{"kind":"reasoning","id":"p2-11000d72","text":"The change is in. Since it's a single shared CSS rule, it applies to all section headings in Terms & Conditions and Refund Policy on all 8 pages — including \"Business Transparency & Compliance Statement\". The headings now use the exact same font treatment as the Risk Disclaimer summary (Inter, 0.8rem, weight 600, 0.1em letter-spacing, uppercase) — but remain gold so they still stand out as section headers.\n\nThat's the complete change. Quick, targeted, no review needed. Done — reply.","open":false,"collapse":"preview"},{"kind":"text","text":"Done! The section headi