otential issues:\n\n**1. SVG transform-box:fill-box rotation origin:**\n- `transform-box: fill-box; transform-origin: center;` — this makes the rotation origin the center of each circle's bounding box. For a circle, fill-box center = the circle's center. So rotation should spin the dashed arcs around their own center. That's correct — each dashed circle rotates around its own center, creating the orbital arc effect. Good.\n- One subtlety: `transform-box: fill-box` on SVG child elements is widely supported in modern browsers (Chrome 64+, Firefox 55+, Safari 11+). Fine.\n- Note: CSS transforms on SVG elements use the SVG user unit coordinate system with fill-box. Should work.\n\n**2. Z-index layering:**\n- arcs z-index 0, ground-dust z-index 0, img z-index 1, sweeps z-index 2, sparks/embers z-index 3. So arcs and ground dust render behind the bull. That matches the reference image (arcs framing behind/around the bull). Good.\n- But wait — `.hero-visual` container has `mask-image: radial-gradient(ellipse at 52% 55%, black 48%, transparent 86%)` from the first block (line ~478). And the img has its own mask. The mask on `.hero-visual` applies to ALL children including the new arcs and ground-dust. That means the arcs/ground-dust will be masked too — they'll fade out toward the edges. Since arcs are at right:-16% top:-14%, they extend toward the edge, so the mask will partially fade them. This is actually consistent with the \"blend into background\" goal, but it may dim the arcs significantly. Worth noting — the mask is on the container, so arcs peeking outside the ellipse fade out. Could be intentional/desirable (soft blend) but worth flagging as a consideration.\n\n**3. SVG id=\"arcGold\" collision:**\n- The SVG has `<linearGradient id=\"arcGold\">`. If the site has multiple SVGs with the same id (e.g., other pages or repeated inline SVGs), ids could collide. Let me think — the hero appears only on index.html. Are there other SVGs with id=\"arcGold\"? I don't know from the context, but a quick search would confirm. There's a risk it's generic. Also, the gradient stops use rgba(229,193,88,0.95) → rgba(201,162,39,0.3) — the second stop has alpha 0.3 which is quite faint. Fine.\n\n**4. Ground dust positioning:**\n- `.ground-dust` bottom:-2%, left:50%, transform translateX(-50%), width 118%, height 40%. This is relative to `.hero-visual` container. But the bull img is `transform: translateY(5vh)` and max-height 95vh — the img is much bigger than the container? The img has max-width:176%, max-height:95vh, so it overflows the container. The ground-dust at bottom:-2% height:40% would sit at the bottom of the *container*, not necessarily at the bull's hooves. The bull is translated down by 5vh and is very tall (95vh max). Hmm, in the sandbox the visual was 520px tall with the img at y=253. The dust at y=322 height 208 — so dust occupies the bottom 40% of the container. That seems reasonable for \"kicking up dust at the base.\"\n- Potential issue: ground-dust width 118% + blur(12px) with `overflow:hidden`? The hero section has `overflow: hidden`, so the dust bleeding beyond is clipped — fine.\n- The dust is opacity 0 at 0%/100% and peaks at 0.85 at 28% — with a 12s cycle, it will be invisible ~half the time. Combined with the existing dust-clouds also having opacity 0 at start, this could make the effect feel intermittent. The reference image shows persistent dust. But the existing pattern already uses pulsing opacity, so it's consistent. It's a design choice. Note: on hover, animations speed up (7s/10s) — good.\n\n**5. `.ground-dust-2` animation-duration override on hover:**\n- `.hero-visual:hover .ground-dust, .hero-visual:hover .ground-dust-2 { animation-duration: 7s, 10s; }` — setting `animation-duration: 7s, 10s` with two values for a single animation `ground-billow`? If an element has ONE animation, `animation-duration: 7s, 10s` applies the first value to the first animation and the second to a second animation (which doesn't exist, so it's ignored or creates an implicit none). Actually per spec, if the animation shorthand list is longer than the animation-name list, extra values are ignored. So ground-dust gets 7s, ground-dust-2 gets... wait, this rule applies to both. For `.ground-dust` (one animation), duration becomes 7s. For `.ground-dust-2`, it also has one animation, so duration becomes 7s — the 10s is ignored. That's fine, just redundant. Minor.\n\nActually wait — each element matches the selector and gets `animation-duration: 7s, 10s`. Since each has one animation, the effective duration is 7s. The 10s is dead. Harmless but slightly sloppy. Minor point.\n\n**6. Mobile behavior:**\n- At ≤968px, `.hero-visual` gets `order: -1` and img max-width 60% max-height 40vh. The arcs/dust are %-positioned relative to the container so they scale down. But the SVG has fixed viewBox 0 0 400 400 with fixed circle coords — it scales with the container width. The arcs at cx=300 cy=170 r=205 max — the container on mobile is smaller, so arcs shrink. Should be OK.\n- However: on mobile the bull is smaller (60% width), but arcs are 88% width of the container — the arcs may appear proportionally larger relative to the shrunken bull, and the dust at 118% width could dominate. Worth a quick check but not critical.\n- The `.hero-visual` has a `mask-image` on the container which on mobile might clip more aggressively. Fine.\n\n**7. HTML well-formedness:**\n- The SVG has `<defs>` with a gradient, 3 circles, self-closed tags, aria-hidden. The ground-dust divs and grain spans are well-formed. The img tag before them — the img has a huge base64 src, closes properly. Looks fine.\n\n**8. `stroke-dasharray` values:**\n- arc-1: r=150, dasharray \"200 742\". Circumference = 2π×150 ≈ 942. 200+742 = 942. ✓ Good — dashes leave a gap that sums to the circumference.\n- arc-2: r=205, circumference ≈ 1288. dasharray \"120 1168\" = 1288. ✓\n- arc-3: r=100, circumference ≈ 628. dasharray \"110 518\" = 628. ✓\n- Nice — the math checks out, so each is a single clean arc segment.\n\n**9. Rotation direction/perceived motion:**\n- All three arcs spin (26s, 44s reverse, 19s reverse). With dashed arcs, spinning around center will make the arc segments orbit — creating the \"circular abstract arc lines\" effect from the reference. Good.\n\n**10. Hover on `.hero-arcs` opacity 1 (from 0.9)** — subtle. Fine.\n\n**11. One concern: `.hero-arcs` opacity 0.9 with drop-shadow on each arc.** The drop-shadow filter on SVG elements with fill-box transform — filter and transform both apply; drop-shadow is computed pre-transform. Fine.\n\n**12. z-index vs sweeps:** sweeps are z-index 2 with mix-blend overlay — they sweep across the whole hero, including over arcs (z 0) and dust (z 0). Fine.\n\n**13. Does the container mask clip arcs heavily?** The container mask: `radial-gradient(ellipse at 52% 55%, black 48%, transparent 86%)`. The arcs span top -14% to 74% height, right -16% to 72% width. Much of the arc area is outside the 48-86% ellipse radius — so arcs will be significantly faded near edges. That might actually be desired (soft blend). But the user asked to \"make it look like the reference\" with visible arc lines; if the mask dims them too much, they may barely show. Worth flagging: consider whether the arcs should escape the container mask — e.g., put the SVG outside .hero-visual or adjust. Actually since arcs are decorative framing, being masked is probably fine, but flag it.\n\n**14. Performance:** 7 grains + 6 embers + 5 sparks + 2 dust + 3 arcs + 2 sweeps = lots of animated elements, all with blur/drop-shadow filters and mix-blend-mode. On low-end devices this could be heavy, but it's the design's existing approach. Minor.\n\n**15. `transform-box: fill-box` + `transform-origin: center` on circle with stroke:** fill-box includes the stroke? Fill-box is the object bounding box of the element including stroke in some implementations? Actually objectBoundingBox includes stroke for CSS transform-box fill-box per SVG2? There can be minor offset. With 1-1.6px strokes it's negligible.\n\n**16. Missing `overflow: visible`:** The SVG doesn't clip by default (SVG root doesn't clip unless overflow hidden). Fine.\n\n**17. The `.hero-visual` second block re-declares position:relative etc. and the first block's mask applies (since second block doesn't reset mask). Confirmed earlier the mask remains. The arcs/dust inherit the mask — as discussed.\n\nAlso — one more: the ground-dust `bottom: -2%` extends below the container bottom; the hero section has overflow:hidden, so no page scroll issue. Good.\n\n**18. id collision check:** The site's other pages (careers, contact, etc.) also have SVGs (icons). Do any use id=\"arcGold\"? Only this new one defines it. But since each page is standalone HTML (no shared SVG sprite), no collision across pages. Within index.html, is \"arcGold\" unique? Only defined once. Fine, but to be safe could use a more specific id like \"arcGoldHero\". Minor robustness suggestion — if someone later copies the hero to another page, duplicate id would make the second SVG reference the first gradient (which is fine since identical). Low risk.\n\nLet me also double check: the arcs are positioned `top:-14%; right:-16%` — this puts the arc cluster's top-right region above/right of the container. The SVG is 88% of container. cx=300/400 = 75% of SVG width, cy=170/400 = 42.5% of height. So arc centers are at ~75%×0.88 + right offset... roughly the upper-right area. Good — matches \"upper right\" framing.\n\nAlso the arcs could extend off-screen right on wide hero? right:-16% means the SVG's right edge is 16% beyond the container's right edge. Combined with hero overflow hidden, the arcs clip at section edge but the section is 100% width minus nothing... the hero-grid container is max-width 1200px centered, but .hero-visual is inside the grid column, not full-width. So arcs extending 16% beyond the column still stay within the page. Fine.\n\nOverall: solid implementation, math checks out. Critical feedback points:\n1. Container mask dims arcs/dust — verify they're visible enough (may need arcs moved out of masked container or mask tweak).\n2. Hover rule `animation-duration: 7s, 10s` — the second value is dead; simplify to a single value (7s for both, or distinguish).\n3. Ground dust opacity pulses to 0 frequently — may look intermittent vs. the persistent dust in the reference; consider a floor opacity.\n4. SVG gradient id \"arcGold\" is generic — use a unique id to avoid any future collision if the hero is duplicated.\n5. Mobile: arcs/dust scale with container while bull shrinks to 60% — dust at 118% width may visually dominate; verify at ≤968px and consider toning down.\n\nThat's good, concise feedback.","open":false,"collapse":"preview"},{"kind":"text","text":"<think>I'll review the hero bull upgrade against the requirements and potential issues.</thinking>\n\nThe implementation is solid overall — the dash math checks out perfectly (arc-1: 200+742=942=2π×150 ✓, arc-2: 120+1168=1288=2π×205 ✓, arc-3: 110+518=628=2π×100 ✓), each circle is a single clean arc segment, and `transform-box: fill-box` makes each dashed arc orbit its own center correctly. Z-index layering is right (arcs/dust at 0 behind the bull at 1). A few things worth improving:\n\n1. **Container mask will dim the new effects.** `.hero-visual` (first block, ~line 478) still carries `mask-image: radial-gradient(ellipse at 52% 55%, black 48%, transparent 86%)`, and masks apply to *all* children. The arcs sit at `top:-14%; right:-16%` — largely outside the mask's 48–86% ellipse radius — so they'll be faded out where they should be most visible, and the ground dust is near the mask's bottom edge. The reference image shows crisp arc lines and a bold dust base; the mask may soften them into near-invisibility. Consider rendering the arcs/dust outside the masked element (e.g. siblings) or relaxing the mask, and verify actual visibility in a real browser.\n\n2. **Hover