btle haze is fine, but maybe position the ground layer's bottom at 0 with height ~140px — the scroll indicator would sit above it. Actually the scroll-indicator is centered at bottom of the hero section. If I add a dust haze full width at the bottom, it will pass behind the scroll indicator — could look nice (dust rolling under it) or could look busy. Keep the haze subtle enough that it's fine.\n\nAlternatively, I could keep the hero-ground haze subtle (opacity peaks ~0.25, blur high) and grains small. The scroll indicator has its own styles; let me check quickly what z-index/position it has. Let me grep.\n\nAlso I should consider the TradingView widget gap: earlier they wanted the hero pushed up close to the widget. Adding a ground layer at hero bottom doesn't affect that.\n\nLet me look at:\n- `.scroll-indicator` CSS\n- The `.hero` full block\n- Current `.hero-visual` ground-dust/ground-grain rules (I have them from earlier)\n\nThen implement:\n1. HTML: add `<div class=\"hero-ground\" aria-hidden=\"true\">` inside `<section class=\"hero\">`, after the hero-grid div (before or after scroll-indicator). Containing haze divs and ~10-14 sand grains positioned across full width (left column area + center + right).\n2. CSS: `.hero-ground` absolute inset-x 0 bottom 0 height ~40% (of hero), z-index 1, pointer-events none, overflow hidden (hero already has overflow hidden). Haze layers using radial-gradient gold ellipses with blur and billow animation (reuse ground-billow keyframes — they're defined already). Sand grains using a new class `.sand-grain` similar to `.ground-grain` but slightly larger (5px?) to read as the kicked sand, drifting up with a sway, staggered delays.\n\nWait — the user said \"mud/sand like particles that the bull has made with its legs from impact\" — i.e., the existing `.ground-grain`/`.ground-dust` at the bull's feet. They want that same look \"everywhere around the hero to blend it in.\" So best approach: extend that visual language across the fu