like the bull as focal point), unless... \"the gold glow you added\" — the wisps and haze are the ones I added. Good.\n\nWait — actually let me reconsider. The screenshot shows the page is quite dark and moody — the bull is glowing golden but the atmosphere looks heavy. The user says the gold glow is overwhelming. The main golden atmosphere contributors I added:\n1. `.hero-ground-haze` × 2 — big golden blurred ellipses across the bottom\n2. `.hero-wisp` × 7 — blurred gold clouds\n3. The particles' glow (currently subtle)\n\nThe bull-side original effects (dust-cloud ×3 inside hero-visual, ground-dust ×2, embers, sparks, ::before/::after glow) — those are the bull's look which they've approved through many iterations.\n\nSo plan: significantly cut haze + wisps, moderately boost particles. Also the haze on the bull side (`.hero-visual .ground-dust`) stays as the impact look.\n\nImplementation:\n\n1. `.hero-ground-haze`:\n   - background alphas: 0.17→0.08, 0.12→0.06, 0.17→0.08\n   - animation: change `hero-haze` peaks: 0.12/0.32/0.22 → 0.05/0.12/0.08\n   - blur 14px → 18px (softer, less defined glow)\n\n2. `.hero-wisp`:\n   - background: rgba(201,162,39,0.14) → 0.06, 0.04 → 0.02\n   - blur 10px → 14px\n   - switch to gentler `wisp-billow` keyframes with peak opacity ~0.3 → actually let me set 0%/100% opacity 0, 30% opacity 0.3, 55% opacity 0.18. Hmm, since gradient alphas are halved, dust-billow peak 0.75 would make effective ~0.75*0.06... The opacity multiplies the gradient. Let me just use dust-billow still but with the lower gradient alphas, the effective brightness is much lower. But the *opacity* animation at 0.75 with a 0.06 alpha gradient = very faint. Actually simpler to keep dust-billow and halve the gradient. Effective peak ≈ 0.75 × (0.06 alpha center) ≈ faint gold. That's the reduction. But maybe better to also reduce the opacity peak for consistency with \"decrease atmosphere\": create wisp-billow with peaks 0.4.\n\nLet me define:\n```css\n@keyframes wisp-billow {\n  0%, 100% { transform: scale(0.85) translateX(0); opacity: 0; }\n  30% { opacity: 0.4; }\n  55% { transform: scale(1.15) translateX(20px); opacity: 0.22; }\n}\n```\nAnd `.hero-wisp { background: radial-gradient(circle, rgba(201,162,39,0.07), rgba(201,162,39,0.02) 55%, transparent 75%); filter: blur(14px); animation: wisp-billow 13s ...; }`\n\n3. `.ambient-dust`: 5px, box-shadow 0 0 7px 2px rgba(201,162,39,0.35), keyframes dust-float: 0.7 peak, 0.35 mid, and duration 12s → 10s.\n\nWait, but last turn's complaint about glow being overwhelming came when specks had box-shadow 0 0 9px 2.5px @0.5. Now the overwhelming part is reportedly the whole atmosphere. They now explicitly want specks \"brought to life more\". So 5px with 7px/2px @0.35 glow + peak 0.7 opacity is lively but the glow per speck is moderate. I think that's the right call — the difference is the atmosphere (haze/wisps) is now minimal so the bright specks won't feel overwhelming.\n\n4. `.sand-grain`: 5px, box-shadow 0 0 7px 2px @0.35, sand-drift peak 0.7, mid 0.35, duration 10s → 8s.\n\n5. Maybe slightly more travel for aliveness: dust-float -110px → -120px; sand-drift -130px → -150px. And scale up a touch. Fine.\n\nLet me also double check there's no other \"glow atmosphere\" I added — the hero-ground haze is the big one. Good.\n\nEdits in CSS only (HTML unchanged). Let me write the str_replace:\n\nEdit 1 — `.hero-ground .hero-ground-haze` block + `hero-haze` keyframes:\nCurrent:\n```css\n.hero-ground .hero-ground-haze {\n  position: absolute;\n  left: -10%;\n  right: -10%;\n  bottom: -4%;\n  height: 62%;\n  border-radius: 50%;\n  background:\n    radial-gradient(ellipse at 18% 85%, rgba(201, 162, 39, 0.17), transparent 55%),\n    radial-gradient(ellipse at 50% 92%, rgba(201, 162, 39, 0.12), transparent 50%),\n    radial-gradient(ellipse at 82% 84%, rgba(201, 162, 39, 0.17), transparent 55%);\n  filter: blur(14px);\n  mix-blend-mode: screen;\n  opacity: 0;\n  animation: hero-haze 14s ease-in-out