"narrower\" than... the container? That doesn't add up unless the paragraphs have a max-width.\n\nHmm, unless the screenshot shows the paragraphs NOT spanning the container because they're shorter... no, both would span the full container width as block elements.\n\nYou know, I'm going in circles. Let me take the most practical path: measure precisely what the user might be seeing by rendering at desktop width. I can't resize the preview, but I can reason: at any width, `.container` centers. The inline text-align:center centers text. So the ONLY way the paragraph appears off-center is if the paragraph text is left-aligned or the block is wider on one side.\n\nGiven all this, I suspect the user's screenshot predates my centering fix (took it before reloading) OR they mean the letter should be a tighter centered column. \n\nActually — the MOST LIKELY explanation, thinking about it as a real user: They reloaded, saw the font change (cursive — acknowledged), but the screenshot they attached shows paragraphs 1-2 and the vision says \"center-aligned\". So they DID see centered text... and STILL say not centered?!\n\nHmm, unless the vision description is just wrong about centering (it inferred from narrow lines?). If the text were LEFT-aligned, a vision model might describe center-aligned if the paragraph is short... The quote is a long paragraph though, filling the width — left-aligned text in a full-width block looks clearly left-aligned.\n\nLet me just check: maybe my `text-align:center` inline on the wrapper got overridden by a more specific CSS rule for `.reveal p` or `.section p` with text-align:left? Inline styles beat stylesheets unless !important. Let me search the CSS for text-align rules on p or reveal.\n\nLet me grep for `text-align` in the CSS.","open":false,"collapse":"preview"},{"kind":"text","text":"The container measures centered in the preview, so something in CSS may be overriding the centering. Let me check for competing `text-align` rules."},{"kind":"t