is that the user is seeing the block left-of-center because of the **reveal transform or... the container's left position**. In the preview at 421px: section left 8, container left 8, center 202 vs page center 210.5. The ~8px offset comes from the scrollbar (page usable width = 421 - 15 = 406, so true center of content = 203, and container center 202 — that's within 1px). So genuinely centered.\n\nHmm, so what is the user seeing?! Let me reconsider — maybe the user's screenshot is of a DIFFERENT viewport and the description is unreliable. The user's message is authoritative: \"the whole paragraph is still not in the middle of the page.\"\n\nNew hypothesis: The user might be looking at the page on their actual desktop where the container is 800px wide centered — and the paragraphs ARE centered — but the user perceives \"not in the middle\" because... the paragraph text starts at different x positions each line (justified? no, centered). \n\nOR maybe the real issue: **the vision model's \"narrower text block\"** — maybe the paragraphs in their real browser are NOT spanning the container because there's a max-width on the paragraphs? No, I didn't set any.\n\nHmm hold on, let me reconsider the screenshot: it shows only paragraph 1 and 2 visible (cut off before paragraph 3). Both \"center-aligned... narrower text block\". If the text were truly centered in an 800px block, the vision model would likely not say \"narrower\". Unless the browser window in the screenshot is very wide (like 1440px), the 800px container is centered, and the text fills it. \"Narrower text block\" would then be wrong.\n\nI keep going back and forth. Let me try the most robust fix that satisfies \"put the whole paragraph in the middle of the page\" no matter the ambiguity:\n\n1. Make the letter a **narrower, definitively centered column**: reduce the container max-width to something like 680-720px AND ensure the paragraph block is centered with `margin: 0 auto; max-width: 640px` — creating an obvious centered column with equal whitespace on both sides. This makes it unmistakably \"in the middle of the page\" regardless of the previous subtlety.\n\nActually wait — maybe I should reconsider whether the user wants the paragraphs CENTER-ALIGNED TEXT (ragged both sides) or JUST centered block with LEFT-aligned text (like a real letter, which is usually left-aligned within a centered column). The user said \"center the whole paragraph to the center of the page, it's a bit to the left\" — they want the block in the middle, not necessarily justified center text. A centered column with left-aligned text is the classic \"letter\" look. But the first quote paragraph is centered-styled currently...\n\nHmm, actually in the FIRST request they said \"center the whole paragraph to the center of the page\" — I interpreted as center-align the text. They now say it's STILL not in the middle. The screenshot shows centered text though...\n\nOK let me actually reconsider from scratch: maybe the user's screenshot shows the paragraph NOT centered and the vision model got it wrong. If the vision model is right about \"center-aligned\", the user would have no complaint. Since they DO complain, maybe the screenshot shows LEFT-aligned text and the vision model inferred \"center\" from... unlikely.\n\nThe safest concrete action that directly addresses \"it's a bit to the left\": ensure the paragraph BLOCK is explicitly centered with max-width and margin auto, making the whole letter a centered column. I'll:\n- Keep the container max-width 800px centered (already).\n- Set the letter content wrapper to `max-width: 680px; margin: 0 auto;` so the block is a clear centered column narrower than 800px.\n- Keep text centered within (as the user liked the look? or left-align?). The user's first request: \"center the whole paragraph to the center of the page\". They want it centered. Keep text-align center.\n\nHmm, but if the block is already centered, adding max-width:680px + margin auto just narrows it — still centered. The user would still say \"not centered\" if the real issue is elsewhere!\n\nLet me think about what could genuinely make text appear left-shifted in their real browser despite text-align:center:\n1. **The `reveal` transform**: `transform: translateY(30px)` — vertical only.\n2. **Scrollbar** — minor.\n3. **A parent with padding-left** — `.section` has no padding. `.container` has `padding: 0 1rem` — symmetric.\n4. **The font itself**: Cormorant Garamond italic — no.\n\nHonestly, the layout IS centered. Given the screenshot, I now strongly suspect the user's screenshot was taken BEFORE my change was applied (browser cache) — they acknowledged the font change though...\n\nWAIT. Actually — re-read the user message: \"You changed the font well nicely done, but the whole paragraph is still not in the middle of the page\". \n\nAnd look at the screenshot description again: Paragraph 2 is described as \"regular (not italicized)\". But I italicized all paragraphs! So the screenshot shows paragraph 2 as REGULAR — meaning the screenshot is from BEFORE my font edit (paragraphs 2-4 were regular then). So the screenshot is STALE — it's the state before my last edit. That means the user is telling me: even after reloading (they saw the font change — \"changed the font well\"), the centering STILL isn't right.\n\nSo in their CURRENT view: paragraphs are cursive (font changed ✓) but still \"not in the middle\". The screenshot (stale) showed the paragraphs as centered text blocks... \n\nUgh, the screenshot is stale so I can't trust it for the current state. The user's live state: cursive font, but paragraph not centered per them.\n\nGiven the inline `text-align:center` I added IS on the wrapper... unless the user reloaded but the CSS/HTML cached... The font changed though (inline styles too!). Both my font + centering were inline styles. If the font change shows but centering doesn't, that's impossible — same edit.\n\nTherefore the centering IS applied, and