But on why-choose-us.html, the hero has a background — let me check. Line 51: `<section class=\"hero\" style=\"min-height:70vh;padding-top:120px;\">` — the hero section. Its background could be a dark gradient.\n\nThe \"grey triangle icon in top right corner\" — that's probably the scroll indicator or a decorative element, or the vision model misreading the hamburger.\n\nOK here's my strong hypothesis: **The nav content simply overflows at the user's viewport width.** The nav has: logo (1.5rem serif) + 7 links (0.8rem, uppercase, 0.1em letter-spacing, 2.5rem gap between each) + button. At 0.8rem with 2.5rem (40px) gaps, 7 links × ~90-110px each + 6 gaps × 40px = ~630-770px + logo ~120px + button ~160px = ~910-1050px total. So below ~1100px viewport (minus container padding), it cramps and wraps.\n\nThe container is 1200px max-width (line 101). At a 1200px+ viewport, everything fits. At ~1100px, tight. At ~1000px, the button text wraps.\n\nBut the user says \"it changed, it got bigger\" — implying regression. Did I change the nav font-size anywhere? Let me check git to see the actual diff. Let me check if there's a git repo.\n\nActually — hmm, wait. Let me reconsider. There's ANOTHER possibility: **the `hamburger` is NOT displaying at the right breakpoint** — i.e., at the user's width (maybe 900-968px), nav-links should be hidden (≤968px). But the screenshot shows nav-links VISIBLE. If the user's viewport is ≤968px, the nav-links should be `display:none`. The screenshot shows them visible → viewport >968px. So the cramming happens between 968px and ~1100px.\n\nHmm OK wait, but actually — let me look again at the screenshot: is the hamburger visible? The vision model says \"a small upward-pointing grey triangle icon is visible\" in the top right corner OUTSIDE the nav area. That might be the hamburger! If the hamburger is visible AND the nav-links are visible, then... the media query for hamburger might be broken — BOTH showing. The hamburger showing at >968px would be a regression!\n\nLet me check: `.hamburger { display: none }` base, `.hamburger { display: flex }` at ≤968px. And `.nav-links { display: flex }` base, `display: none` at ≤968px. Those look correct.\n\nBut wait — if the user's viewport is ≤968px and nav-links were hidden, the screenshot would show ONLY the logo + hamburger. The vision model clearly saw all 7 links + button. So viewport > 968px.\n\nHmm, unless the vision model described the MOBILE menu (mobile-menu div)? No, it described a horizontal bar.\n\nOK let me just check git diff to see what","open":false,"collapse":"preview"}],"ts":1786009258748},{"role":"user","parts":[{"kind":"text","text":"The text \"By The Numbers\" and the signature should be in alignment \nAnd the letter in cursive should be in alinement with them, A Letter From The Founder\nWhy I Started Reality FX is correct though don't touch that"}],"ts":1786010006776},{"role":"assistant","parts":[{"kind":"reasoning","id":"p1-5baae9e7","text":"Let me parse the user's new request carefully. They interrupted my header investigation and gave a new, authoritative request:\n\n\"The text 'By The Numbers' and the signature should be in alignment. And the letter in cursive should be in alignment with them. 'A Letter From The Founder', 'Why I Started Reality FX' is correct though don't touch that.\"\n\nSo the user wants:\n1. \"By The Numbers\" (the next section's eyebrow) and the signature \"— Founder, Reality FX\" to be in alignment (vertically aligned on the same center axis).\n2. The cursive letter text to be in alignment with them too.\n3. \"A Letter From The Founder\" / \"Why I Started Reality FX\" headings are correct — don't touch.\n\nHmm, interesting. The user is now describing a DIFFERENT alignment issue. The signature \"— Founder, Reality FX\" and the \"By The Numbers\" eyebrow should share the same center axis as the letter body. The headings are fine.\n\nWait — but \"By The Numbers\" is the NEXT section (\"The Metrics That Matter\" section, dar