the file look like? I can't know without git.\n\nLet me approach differently: let me look at ALL the nav-related rules and check the media queries for nav adjustments. And critically — let me check if there's a media query between 968px and ~1300px that adjusts nav-links gap/font-size (a common pattern: at 1024px, reduce gap to 1.5rem). If that query was lost, the nav would have the desktop 2.5rem gap at ~1024px viewport → cramming.\n\nFrom my earlier grep of @media lines:\n- 1599: @media (max-width: 640px)\n- 1891: /* ── Responsive ── */@media (max-width: 968px)\n- 1932: @media (max-width: 640px)\n- 2225: @media (max-width: 768px)\n- 2335: @media (max-width: 640px)\n\nNo intermediate breakpoint like 1100px. So between 968px and 1200px, the nav uses full desktop spacing: logo 1.5rem + 7 links at 0.8rem uppercase with 0.1em letter-spacing + 2.5rem gaps + button. Let me compute the width:\n\nLinks: HOME(4), WHY US(6), SERVICES(8), INDUSTRIES(10), PROGRAMS(8), CAREERS(7), CONTACT(7) = 50 chars. At 0.8rem (12.8px) uppercase with 0.1em letter-spacing: avg char ~8px + 1.28px spacing ≈ 9.3px → ~465px for all links. Plus 6 gaps × 40px = 240px. Plus logo ~120px. Plus button ~140px. Total ≈ 965px. Plus container padding (2 × ~24px = 48px) → ~1013px content width needed. The container max-width 1200px, so below ~1013px viewport, it cramps. At 1024px viewport: just barely fits or starts to cramp.\n\nHmm, so at ~1024px (a common laptop resolution), the nav is borderline. But that would have ALWAYS been the case — not \"changed\".\n\n\"it changed\" — something DID change. Let me look at the `.nav` CSS more carefully and compare against the OTHER pages' inline styles. Wait — maybe the issue is on the WHY US page specifically! Let me check: the nav-links on why-choose-us.html... the markup is identical to other pages (I saw it). Hmm.\n\nActually — WAIT A MINUTE. Let me re-read the screenshot description yet again: \"In the top right corner outside the navigation area, a small upwar