ree Evaluation button inline styles across pages — maybe only some pages have the small padding:","open":false,"collapse":"preview"},{"kind":"tool","id":"1n_FugPZcmM","toolName":"run_terminal_command","input":{"command":"for f in index.html why-choose-us.html our-services.html industries.html programs.html careers.html contact.html evaluation.html; do echo \"== $f ==\"; grep 'Free' \"REALITY-FOREX-TRADING-/$f\" | grep -i 'evaluation' | head -2; done","process_type":"SYNC","timeout_seconds":30}},{"kind":"reasoning","id":"p11-5baae9e7","text":"All pages are identical. So the nav markup is uniform and the CSS is intact. The cramming is a width issue: the nav content (~1013px needed) vs the container (90% of viewport, max 1200px). At viewports between ~968px (breakpoint) and ~1125px, the nav overflows and looks broken.\n\nThe user says \"it changed, it got bigger\" — but the code shows nothing that changed recently in the nav. It's possible:\n1. The user's browser window is narrower than before (maybe they resized, or their zoom level changed — a 110% zoom would effectively make the viewport ~90% and trigger cramming!)\n2. Or they're misremembering.\n\nActually — ZOOM. If the user pressed Ctrl+ or has browser zoom at 110-125%, everything renders bigger (\"it got bigger\"!) and the nav overflows → cramming → \"WHY US\" wraps → button wraps to 2 lines. That fits PERFECTLY: \"it changed, it got bigger and the why us section words is on top of each other.\"\n\nSo the fix that helps regardless of cause: make the nav resilient to tight widths:\n1. Add a mid-range media query (~1200px or 1100px) that tightens the nav: reduce gap 2.5rem → 1.8rem, reduce link font-size slightly, prevent the button from wrapping (white-space: nowrap).\n2. Add `white-space: nowrap` to the nav button so \"Free Evaluation\" never wraps.\n3. Ensure \"WHY US\" never wraps: `.nav-links a { white-space: nowrap }`.\n\nThese are safe, classy, and fix the cramming at any width between 968-1150px. Let me also reduce the base gap from 2.5rem → 2.25rem slightly for headroom.\n\nLet me implement:\n1. `.nav-links a { white-space: nowrap; }` — prevents link text wrapping (\"WHY\" over \"US\")\n2. `.nav-links .btn-primary { white-space: nowrap; }` — keeps \"Free Evaluation\" on one line\n3. New media query: `@media (max-width: 1200px)` to tighten gap to 1.5rem and letter-spacing to 0.08em. Actually — the container is max-width 1200, so at viewport 1200px, container = 1080px. Content needs ~1013px — close. At 1100px viewport, container = 990px < 1013 → overflow by 23px. A 1200px media query tightening the gap from 2.5rem (40px × 6 = 240px) to 1.5rem (24px × 6 = 144px) saves 96px → content needs ~917px → fits down to ~1020px viewport. \n\nAlso reduce nav-logo slightly? No, keep logo at 1.5rem.\n\nLet me write the CSS. I'll modify:\n- `.nav-links { gap: 2.5rem }` → keep for wide screens\n- Add `white-space: nowrap` to `.nav-links a` and `.nav-links .btn-primary`\n- Add a `@media (max-width: 1200px)` block with tighter nav spacing\n\nWhere to add the media query? Right after the `.nav-links .btn-primary::after { display: none }` rule (~line 326) or near the other responsive rules. I'll add it right after the nav block for cohesion. Let me find a good anchor — after `.nav-links .btn-primary::after { display: none; }`.\n\nLet me look at the exact text around line 320-335 to anchor the edit.","open":false,"collapse":"preview"},{"kind":"text","text":"All pages are uniform — the nav markup and CSS are intact. The real issue: the nav content (~1015px) barely fits the 90%-wide container, so at common window widths (or slight browser zoom — which explains \"it got bigger\"), the links and the \"Free Evaluation\" button wrap into broken stacks. The fix: keep links on one line and tighten spacing at mid widths. Let me find the anchor point:"},{"kind":"tool","id":"1n_9uKq3jU0","toolName":"run_terminal_command","input":{"command":"sed -n '315,340p' REALITY-FOREX-TRADING-/css/style.css","process_type":"SYNC","timeout_seconds":30}},{"kind":"reasoning","id":"p12-5baae9e7","text":"Now I'll make the nav resilient:\n\n1. Add `white-space: nowrap` to `.nav-links a` (prevents \"WHY US\" wrapping into two lines)\n2. Add `white-space: nowrap` to `.nav-links .btn-primary` (prevents \"Free Evaluation\" wrapping)\n3. Add a `@media (max-width: 1200px)` block after the nav section that tightens the gap and letter-spacing so everything fits down to the 968px hamburger breakpoint.\n\nThe current `.nav-links a` rule:\n```\n.nav-links a {\n  font-size: 0.8rem;\n  font-weight: 500;\n  letter-spacing: 0.1em;\n  text-transform: uppercase;\n  color: var(--text-body);\n  position: relative;\n}\n```\n\nI'll add `white-space: nowrap;` there.\n\nAnd after `.nav-links .btn-primary::after { display: none; }` add:\n```\n.nav-links .btn-primary {\n  white-space: nowrap;\n}\n\n/* Mid-range: keep the full nav on one line */\n@media (max-width: 1200px) {\n  .nav-links {\n    gap: 1.5rem;\n  }\n  .nav-links a {\n    letter-spacing: 0.08em;\n  }\n}\n```\n\nWait — is 1200px the right breakpoint? Container max-width is 1200px; at viewport 1200px, container = 1080px. With 2.5rem gaps the content needs ~1013px; at 1200px viewport it fits (1080 available). Tightening at ≤1200px: gap 1.5rem saves 96px → content ~917px → fits down to viewport ~1020px (container 90% = 918px). Below 968px, the hamburger takes over. So the tightened rule covers 968-1200px smoothly. \n\nAlso — the `.btn` base has `white-space`? The `.btn` base (line ~126): display:inline-flex, align-items:center, gap:0.5rem, padding, font-size... no white-space. The \"Free\\n            Evaluation\" in HTML collapses to \"Free Evaluation\" with a single space, and inline-flex will wrap text at the space. white-space:nowrap on the button prevents it.\n\nLet me make these edits.","open":false,"collapse":"preview"},{"kind":"text","text":"The fix: keep nav links and the button on one line, and tighten spacing at mid widths so nothing