 Ratio = 566/2609 = 0.217. \n\nSo 21.7% > 10% → the observer SHOULD have fired. But it didn't within 1.2 seconds. Maybe the observer fired but... let me wait longer and re-check. Or maybe the scrollIntoView was too fast — the observer fires asynchronously but 1.2s should be plenty.\n\nWAIT — actually, let me reconsider. The header check: `header` is `find(e => e.textContent.includes('Find Your Role'))` — the section-header. Its ratio is -8.6 (fully above viewport). Fine.\n\nBut group1 at 21.7% visible and NOT visible... Something's wrong with the observer. Let me check if main.js even loaded in the preview! The sandbox might not serve js/main.js. If main.js didn't load, the observer never gets set up, and NO reveal ever becomes visible! Let me check whether the observer is active: `typeof IntersectionObserver` is true (it's a browser API), but the observer might not be OBSERVING anything if main.js didn't run.\n\nLet me check: does the nav have the scroll listener? Does the page have any evidence main.js ran? Let me test by scrolling and checking if `.nav.scrolled` gets added (that's from main.js's scroll listener).\n\nAlso — earlier on the why-choose-us page, the letter rendered with all elements measured... but opacity doesn't affect getBoundingClientRect. So I never confirmed reveal fired there either!\n\nLet me check if main.js loaded in the preview sandbox.","open":false,"collapse":"preview"},{"kind":"text","text":"Group 1 is 21.7% visible (well over the 10% threshold) yet still hidden — the reveal observer isn't firing. Let me check whether main.js even loads in this sandbox, and whether its scroll listener is active:"},{"kind":"tool","id":"1okZYL_gqjk","toolName":"preview_evaluate","input":{"expression":"(async () => { const nav = document.querySelector('.nav'); const before = nav && nav.classList.contains('scrolled'); window.scrollTo(0, 400); await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, 300)); const after = nav && nav.classList.contains('scrolled'); const scripts = [...document.querySelectorAll('script')].map(s => s.src || '(inline)'); const bodyScrollY = document.body.scrollHeight; return { before, after, scripts, hasListener: typeof window.addEventListener === 'function', revealCount: document.querySelectorAll('.reveal').length, visibleCount: document.querySelectorAll('.reveal.visible').length }; })()"}},{"kind":"reasoning","id":"p13-69d9921b","text":"The nav scroll listener doesn't fire either (`after: false` after scrolling to 400). This means main.js is NOT loading/executing in the preview sandbox (the sandbox doesn't serve js/main.js — same limitation as the CSS).\n\nSo the preview can't reproduce the real site's behavior — main.js doesn't run there. The visibleCount 0 is a sandbox artifact.\n\nHmm. BUT WAIT — this actually gives me a crucial insight for the REAL bug report! Let me re-read the user's report: \"In mobile version of the site, Open Positions / Find Your Role the career (job listings are not showing up)\".\n\nIf main.js fails to load or errors on mobile (slow network, ad blocker, or a JS error thrown partway), ALL `.reveal` content stays at opacity: 0 — invisible. The user would see the section header \"Open Positions / Find Your Role\"... wait, no — that's ALSO a reveal element. Unless the user means they see the heading because they know it's there / or the heading shows from... hmm.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: maybe the user DOES see the header (it revealed) but not the listings. OR the user sees the whole section as blank space and quotes the heading from the page title.\n\nBut actually — here's the thing. There IS a known mobile-specific JS failure mode here: **the smooth-scroll anchor handler and scrollIntoView on iOS Safari with `behavior: 'smooth'`**. On older iOS versions, `scrollIntoView({ behavior: 'smooth' })` with `block: 'start'` is buggy — it can throw or scroll incorrectly. But it wouldn't prevent the observer.\n\nHmm, but more importantly — let me look at whether main.js could THROW on the careers page. If it throws AFTER the observer setup, reveals still work. If it throws BEFORE, reveals break. The observer setup is near the top (after nav + hamburger). What's before it? Nav scroll listener and hamburger. Nothing that could throw on careers.html.\n\nWait — actually, let me reconsider the whole thing. What if the REAL mobile issue is NOT reveal at all? What if the job listings genuinely don't render because of a CSS layout issue on mobile — like the department-group being 2609px tall... that's FINE, tall content scrolls.\n\nHmm, let me look at the careers.html more carefully — maybe there's a malformed HTML structure that breaks rendering on mobile. Let me validate the HTML around the Open Positions section. Let me check the full structure with a validation approach — look for unclosed tags, especially in the job-listing divs.\n\nActually — the earlier measurements showed group1 height 2609px and group2 (Administration) height 309px. Group2 = \"Administration\" with the Virtual Assistant job (user asked to keep it). 309px seems small for a job listing but it's the remaining part... wait, group2 top 1607 bottom 1916 = 309px. That's ONE job listing tall. But there should be more? The user said leave Virtual Assistant. Let me look at the second department-group (line 352).\n\nLet me read the whole Open Positions section to check for structural integrity.","open":false,"collapse":"preview"},{"kind":"text","text":"The sandbox doesn't load main.js either (same limitation as CSS), so reveals can't fire in the preview — but this exposes the real risk: **if JS fails or the observer misfires on mobile, all `.reveal` content stays invisible.** Let me verify the HTML structure is sound and check the second department group:"},{"kind":"tool","id":"1olJWAJBY3g","toolName":"run_terminal_command","input":{"command":"sed -n '345,395p' REALITY-FOREX-TRADING-/careers.html","process_type":"SYNC","timeout_seconds":30}},{"kind":"reasoning","id":"p14-69d9921b","text":"The HTML structure is completely sound. Two department groups (Trading with 9 jobs, Administration with Virtual Assistant) + coming-soon card. All inside the section.\n\nSo the HTML is fine. The CSS is fine. The most probable mobile cause is the `.reveal` + IntersectionObserver combination.\n\nNow — WHY would reveals fail specifically on mobile? Let me think about known mobile issues:\n\n1. **`rootMargin` in IntersectionObserver on iOS Safari**: There are documented iOS bugs where `rootMargin` behaves unpredictably. But even so, elements would eventually intersect.\n\n2. **The `threshold: 0.1` with very tall elements + `rootMargin: '0px 0px -50px 0px'`**: For a 2609px element on a phone, when it's scrolled such that its top is at viewport top, visible = viewport height ≈ 700px → ratio 0.27 → fires. Should work.\n\n3. **`content-visibility: auto`**: Not used.\n\n4. **The user's mobile browser caching an old broken version?** \n\n5. **The REAL issue — scrolling performance with the huge base64 hero image?** No, careers.html doesn't have that... actually let me check — did careers.html get the new hero image treatment? The careers hero is `.careers-hero`. Fine.\n\n6. **`scroll-behavior: smooth` on html + observer?** No.\n\nHmm, honestly — I can't reproduce the exact mobile failure in the sandbox (no JS). But the user REPORTS it. The pragmatic, launch-safe fix is clear:\n\n**Make the reveal system non-destructive:** content must be visible by default, and only hidden when JS is confirmed working (progressive enhancement). This guarantees job listings ALWAYS show, even if:\n- JS is slow/blocked on mobile\n- The observer misfires\n- An error occurs\n- The browser is old\n\nImplementation:\n1. In main.js: at DOMContentLoaded start, add `document.documentElement.classList.add('js')` (or 'js-enabled') BEFORE setting up the observer.\n2. In CSS: change `.reveal` to only be hidden when `.js` is present:\n```css\n.js .reveal { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(30px); transition: all 0.8s ...; }\n.js .reveal.visible { opacity: 1; transform: translateY(0); }\n```\nAnd keep `.reveal` itself as visible-by-default (no hidden state without .js).\n\nThis is the standard progressive-enhancement pattern and it's bulletproof for launch.\n\nAlso — I should strengthen the observer itself for mobile:\n- Lower the threshold to something safer like `threshold: 0.05` or use `threshold: 0.01`.\n- Actually, a more robust approach for tall elements: fire when ANY part enters (`threshold: 0`) or even better, use `rootMargin: '0px 0px -10% 0px'`... Hmm. The safest: observe with `threshold: 0` and add visible immediately when intersecting (even 1px). The slight delay concern: the animation starts as soon as the element's edge enters — totally fine.\n\nLet me improve:\n```js\nconst revealObserver = new IntersectionObserver((entries) => {\n  entries.forEach(entry => {\n    if (entry.isIntersecting) {\n      entry.target.classList.add('visible');\n      revealObserver.unobserve(entry.target);\n    }\n  });\n}, { threshold: 0, rootMargin: '0px 0px -40px 0px' });\n```\n\nWith threshold 0, ANY pixel of intersection triggers — works for tall elements, works on all browsers.\n\n3. ALSO — add a **fallback**: after a timeout, force-show anything still hidden? A simpler belt-and-braces: on `load`, add visible to elements already within the viewport. Actually the observer handles that. The timeout fallback is nice-to-have:\n\n```js\n// Fallback: never leave content hidden\nsetTimeout(() => {\n  revealElements.forEach(el => el.classList.add('visible'));\n}, 4000);\n```\n\nHmm — that would force-show everything after 4s even off-screen (removing the reveal effect for below-fold content). Actually that defeats the reveal purpose. Better fallback: only reveal elements near the viewport... Let me keep it simple: threshold 0 + js class gating is enough. If the observer exists but fails, the 4s fallback ensures nothing stays hidden forever. Actually — a cleaner fallback: on first scroll, if an element is within viewport + 2 viewport heights, show it. Too complex. Let me just add the .js gate + threshold 0 + a safety timeout that reveals everything after ~5s? No — that kills the effect for below-fold.\n\nLet me think about what actually matters for launch: **job listings must show.** The .js gate ensures they show even if JS totally fails. The threshold-0 fix ensures the observer fires reliably for tall elements. That covers 99% of cases.\n\nLet me also add a tiny extra safeguard specifically so below-the-fold content can never be permanently invisible if the observer glitches: a one-time fallback listener on scroll that checks elements within the viewport and reveals them:\n\n```js\n// Safety net: if the observer stalls, reveal anything already on screen\nlet revealedOnce = false;\nwindow.addEventListener('scroll', () => {\n  if (revealedOnce) return;\n  revealElements.forEach(el => {\n    if (el.getBoundingClientRect().top < window.innerHeight && el.getBoundingClientRect().bottom > 0) {\n      el.classList.add('visible');\n    }\n  });\n  revealedOnce = true;\n}, { passive: true, once: true });\n```\n\nHmm, actually simpler and fully robust: I'll keep the observer, and add a fallback `setInterval` for 3s that checks viewport elements. No — over-engineering. The .js gate alone GUARANTEES visibility when JS is broken, which is the worst case. The threshold 0 improves the observer. Good enough and clean.\n\nNOW — beyond the careers listings, the user asked: \"And any other inaccuracies on mobile version fix please as we are about to launch officially soon.\"\n\nSo I should do a mobile sweep across all pages. Let me plan a checklist of mobile checks:\n\n1. **All pages**: viewport meta tag ✓ (should verify each page has it)\n2. **Horizontal overflow**: any element wider than viewport causes sideways scrolling. Common culprits: wide tables (comparison-table on why-choose-us!), SVG chart-art, hero images, ticker, footer grids.\n3. **The comparison-table on why-choose-us** — a wide table on mobile! Let me check its CSS for horizontal scroll wrapper.\n4. **The ticker** (tradingview widget) — should be fine.\n5. **The careers form** — multi-step form on mobile.\n6. **Programs page pricing cards** — mobile stacking.\n7. **Fonts/emoji** — n/a.\n8. **The nav** — nowrap fix applied; hamburger shows at ≤968px ✓.\n9. **Hero images** on each page — fixed heights, object-fit.\n10. **The chart-art SVG on why-choose-us** — wide SVG in a two-col; on mobile it stacks. If the SVG has a fixed width, overflow.\n\nLet me check the preview at mobile width for horizontal overflow across pages. The preview webview is 421px wide — perfect for mobile testing! Let me:\n1. Load each page in the preview\n2. Check `document.documentElement.scrollWidth > window.innerWidth` (horizontal overflow)\n3. Check for elements wider than the viewport\n4. Fix the offenders\n\nLet me start with careers (the reported page), check overflow + job listing visibility after my JS fix, then sweep the other pages.\n\nAlso, one more thought about the careers listings on mobile: is there any chance they're hidden by CSS on mobile specifically? Let me double-check the 968px media query — it has `.hero-grid, .two-col { grid-template-columns: 1fr }` etc. Nothing hides job-listing. Confirmed no CSS hiding.\n\nLet me also verify the careers hero — `.careers-hero` — fine.\n\nPlan:\n1. Fix the reveal system (main.js + style.css) — guarantees listings show on mobile.\n2. Mobile sweep: check horizontal overflow on all pages via preview evaluate; fix offenders.\n3. Verify + report.\n\nLet me set up todos and execute.\n\nActually first — let me quickly check the current reveal CSS rule exact text and the main.js observer section text for precise str_replace. I have both already from earlier reads:\n\nCSS (lines ~1603-1615):\n```\n/* ── Scroll Reveal ── */\n.reveal {\n  opacity: 0;\n  transform: translateY(30px);\n  transition: all 0.8s cubic-bezier(0.25, 0.46, 0.45, 0.94);\n}\n\n.reveal.visible {\n  opacity: 1;\n  transform: translateY(0);\n}\n```\n\nJS:\n```\n  // ── Scroll Reveal ──\n  const revealElements = document.querySelectorAll('.reveal');\n  const revealObserver = new IntersectionObserver((entries) => {\n    entries.forEach(entry => {\n      if (entry.isIntersecting) {\n        entry.target.classList.add('visible');\n        revealObserver.unobserve(entry.target);\n      }\n    });\n  }, { threshold: 0.1, rootMargin: '0px 0px -50px 0px' });\n  revealElements.forEach(el => revealObserver.observe(el));\n```\n\nChanges:\n\nCSS — gate behind `.js`:\n```css\n/* ── Scroll Reveal ── */\n.js .reveal {\n  opacity: 0;\n  transform: translateY(30px);\n  transition: all 0.8s cubic-bezier(0.25, 0.46, 0.45, 0.94);\n}\n\n.js .reveal.visible {\n  opacity: 1;\n  transform: translateY(0);\n}\n```\n\nJS — add js class first, threshold 0, plus a safety fallback:\n```js\n  // ── Scroll Reveal ──\n  document.documentElement.classList.add('js');\n  const revealElements = document.querySelectorAll('.reveal');\n  const revealObserver = new IntersectionObserver((entries) => {\n    entries.forEach(entry => {\n      if (entry.isIntersecting) {\n        entry.target.classList.add('visible');\n        revealObserver.unobserve(entry.target);\n      }\n    });\n  }, { threshold: 0, rootMargin: '0px 0px -40px 0px' });\n  revealElements.forEach(el => revealObserver.observe(el));\n\n  // Safety net: if the observer ever stalls, never leave content permanently hidden\n  const revealFallback = () => {\n    revealElements.forEach(el => {\n      const r = el.getBoundingClientRect();\n      if (r.top < window.innerHeight * 1.2 && r.bottom > 0 && !el.classList.contains('visible')) {\n        el.classList.add('visible');\n      }\n    });\n  };\n  window.addEventListener('scroll', () => { clearTimeout(revealFallback.t); revealFallback.t = setTimeout(revealFallback, 150); }, { passive: true });\n```\n\nHmm — the fallback adds complexity. Is it worth it? The .js gate handles the \"JS totally broken\" case. The threshold 0 handles tall elements. A scroll-based fallback is redundant... but harmless and cheap. Actually, let me keep it SIMPLE: .js gate + threshold 0. That's the core fix. Adding the fallback risks bugs. Hmm, but \"about to launch officially\" — belt and braces is justified. Let me include a minimal fallback: reveal anything intersecting on scroll. Actually the observer does exactly that. The fallback only matters if the observer breaks. I'll skip the extra fallback to keep the change minimal and reviewable. The .js gate is the guaranteed-visibility fix.\n\nWait — one more consideration: with `.js .reveal` gating, if JS loads but the observer setup THROWS (unlikely), the .js class is added BEFORE the observer line, so content would be hidden and stay hidden. To be extra safe, add the .js class only right before observing... no — the throw risk is between `classList.add('js')` and observer creation: only the observer const line. If IntersectionObserver is undefined (very old browser), `new IntersectionObserver` throws → content hidden! To guard: \n\n```js\nif ('IntersectionObserver' in window) {\n  ...observer...\n} else {\n  revealElements.forEach(el => el.classList.add('visible'));\n}\n```\n\nThat's clean and robust. Let me implement:\n\n```js\n  // ── Scroll Reveal ──\n  document.documentElement.classList.add('js');\n  const revealElements = document.querySelectorAll('.reveal');\n  if ('IntersectionObserver' in window) {\n    const revealObserver = new IntersectionObserver((entries) => {\n      entries.forEach(entry => {\n        if (entry.isIntersecting) {\n          entry.target.classList.add('visible');\n          revealObserver.unobserve(entry.target);\n        }\n      });\n    }, { threshold: 0, rootMargin: '0px 0px -40px 0px' });\n    revealElements.forEach(el => revealObserver.observe(el));\n  } else {\n    // Old browsers: no animation, just show everything\n    revealElements.forEach(el => el.classLis