S via a data: URL? No.\n\nHmm — what about the `preview_evaluate` approach: inject CSS as a `<style>` tag by embedding the CSS text in the JS expression. The CSS is ~55KB. preview_evaluate expressions — can they handle 55KB? Earlier the agent base64'd the whole CSS (55KB → 74KB base64) and injected it, and the webview survived for a while (the crashes were from the full HTML inlining). The progress notes say: \"The CSS is 55KB — I'll inject it via base64 so the styled page renders in the preview\" and later \"The inlined file is too large for the webview\" (that was the FULL HTML with base64 image). Then \"Let me go back to the working preview and inject just the styles I need via JS\".\n\nSo CSS injection via base64 did work at some point. Let me try it: base64 the CSS, decode in the page, add a <style> tag, then measure the mobile layout properly. Also inject main.js similarly (to test the reveal fix!).\n\nActually — for the reveal fix test specifically, I need BOTH CSS and JS. Let me do:\n1. base64 CSS + JS via terminal\n2. In preview: decode both, append <style> and run the JS via eval or inject as script\n\nThe JS injection is trickier (running it as a script with the DOM). I can eval the JS content directly (it's a DOMContentLoaded listener — I'd need to trigger it or run it directly). Actually the JS is wrapped in `document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', ...)` — if I eval it after DOM is loaded, the listener never fires. I could strip the wrapper or just dispatch the event... Actually I can do: `eval(js)` then `document.dispatchEvent(new Event('DOMContentLoaded'))` — but the listener was registered inside eval which runs immediately... no wait, eval(js) REGISTERS the listener (the addEventListener call runs during eval). Then dispatching DOMContentLoaded triggers it. \n\nHmm, but the JS references things like IntersectionObserver, querySelectorAll — all available. It should work.\n\nLet me try this. Steps:\n1. Terminal: base64 the CSS → save to a variable... I can't persist between tool calls. I'll get the base64 string in the output, then embed it in the evaluate call. 55KB CSS → 74KB base64 in the terminal output. Then I paste it into the evaluate expression. That's a lot of output tokens but works. Actually the evaluate expression would then be ~74KB+ of base64 → decode → inject. The message might get truncated though.\n\nAlternative: chunk the CSS. `base64 -w 0` gives one line. The terminal output limit might cut it. Let me check the CSS size: earlier \"The CSS is 55KB\". The terminal tool output — I've seen it return large outputs before (sed outputs etc.). 74KB might be too big for one call output.\n\nHmm. Alternatively — I can inject the CSS in chunks: read specific RANGES of the CSS via sed (e.g., 10KB chunks), and build the style tag progressively across multiple evaluate calls. Tedious but reliable.\n\nOR — simpler and smarter: I don't need the FULL CSS to test mobile issues. I can inject a REDUCED version: just the rules that affect layout on mobile — actually no, too error-prone.\n\nLet me step back and think about what the highest-value verification is:\n1. **The reveal fix** — the actual reported bug. I can test this in the preview WITHOUT the real CSS: inject ONLY the reveal CSS (`.js .reveal { opacity: 0 }` etc.) + the main.js reveal section, then scroll and check `.visible` gets added to the tall department-groups. This verifies the JS logic works (threshold 0, safety net) without needing the full stylesheet.\n\n2. **Mobile overflow** — static analysis says clean. The preview unstyled rendering can still detect OVERFLOW from HTML attributes (e.g., wide SVGs with fixed widths, tables with many columns, images with fixed width attrs). Let me check each page's unstyled scrollWidth — elements wider than 421px in unstyled mode would ALSO overflow in real mode (HTML-level widths are independent of CSS). Plus static CSS checks (done — clean).\n\nSo the plan:\n1. Test the reveal JS logic in the preview with injected CSS+JS (verifying the fix for the reported bug).\n2. Check unstyled scrollWidth on all 8 pages (catches HTML-level overflowers).\n3. Static-check the remaining CSS concerns (table columns, hero, grids) — mostly done.\n4. Fix anything found.\n\nLet me first check the comparison-table column count and the index hero CSS, then do the preview tests.","open":false,"collapse":"preview"},{"kind":"text","text":"Static CSS looks clean so far. Let me check the comparison table structure and the index hero, then test the reveal fix's JS logic in the preview with injected styles:"},{"kind":"tool","id":"1osVezIGrAg","toolName":"run_terminal_command","input":{"command":"grep -c '<th>' REALITY-FOREX-TRADING-/why-choose-us.html; grep -n '<th>' REALITY-FOREX-TRADING-/why-choose-us.html | head -5; echo '===== index hero css:'; grep -n -A12 '^\\.hero {' REALITY-FOREX-TRADING-/css/style.css | head -20; echo '===== hero-bg:'; grep -n -A8 '^\\.hero-bg' REALITY-FOREX-TRADING-/css/style.css | head -12","process_type":"SYNC","timeout_seconds":30}},{"kind":"reasoning","id":"p21-69d9921b","text":"- Comparison table: 2 columns — fits on mobile (just narrow cells). Could add `overflow-x: auto` for safety but with 2 columns at width:100% it's fine.\n- Index hero: `min-height: calc(100vh - 40px)`, overflow hidden, bg image with object-fit cover — mobile-safe.\n\nNow let me test the reveal fix logic in the preview. I'll inject the reveal CSS + run the reveal JS and verify the tall department-group reveals on scroll. Let me write the injection:\n\n1. CSS: `.js .reveal { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(30px); transition: all 0.8s; } .js .reveal.visible { opacity: 1; transform: translateY(0); }`\n2. JS: the reveal block from main.js (extracted)\n3. Then scroll and check.\n\nActually, simpler: I'll inject the reveal JS logic inline and check `.visible` gets added to group1 when scrolled. Since the page is unstyled, the reveal hidden-state CSS doesn't matter for the LOGIC test — I just need to verify the observer fires with threshold 0 on the tall element, and the safety net works.\n\nLet me run the test.","open":false,"collapse":"preview"},{"kind":"text","text":"2-column table (fits on mobile) and the hero is mobile-safe. Now let me test the reveal fix logic in the preview — inject the reveal CSS + JS and verify the tall job-listing sections actually get revealed on scroll:"},{"kind":"tool","id":"1otCSqYsqZw","toolName":"preview_evaluate","input":{"expression":"(async () => {\n  // Inject reveal CSS\n  const st = document.createElement('style');\n  st.textContent = '.js .reveal{opacity:0;transform:translateY(30px);transition:all .8s}.js .reveal.visible{opacity:1;transform:translateY(0)}';\n  document.head.appendChild(st);\n  document.documentElement.classList.add('js');\n  // Run the reveal JS logic (same as main.js)\n  const revealElements = document.querySelectorAll('.reveal');\n  if ('IntersectionObserver' in window) {\n    const revealObserver = new IntersectionObserver((entries) => {\n      entries.forEach(entry => { if (entry.isIntersecting) { entry.target.classList.add('visible'); revealObserver.unobserve(entry.target); } });\n    }, { threshold: 0, rootMargin: '0px 0px -40px 0px' });\n    revealElements.forEach(el => revealObserver.observe(el));\n  }\n  // Scroll the tall Trading group into view\n  const g = document.querySelector('.department-group');\n  g.scrollIntoView({ block: 'start' });\n  await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, 800));\n  const visCount = document.querySelectorAll('.reveal.visible').length;\n  const g1vis = g.classList.contains('visible');\n  const g1r = g.getBoundingClientRect();\n  return { totalReveals: revealElements.length, visibleAfterScroll: visCount, tradingGroupVisible: g1vis, groupTop: Math.round(g1r.top), groupBottom: Math.round(g1r.bottom) };\n})()"}},{"kind":"reasoning","id":"p22-69d9921b","text":"The reveal logic now works correctly:\n- `tradingGroupVisible: true` — the tall 2609px department-group (the job listings!) reveals when scrolled into view, even at threshold 0\n- `visibleAfterScroll: 2` — the section header + the group revealed\n\nWith the OLD threshold 0.1 the same scroll left it invisible (we tested earlier: ratio 0.217 > 0.1 and it was STILL hidden — actually that was a sandbox quirk where main.js never ran... hmm, wait — in the earlier test, main.js didn't load so NO reveal could fire. The threshold change isn't what fixed it there — the JS wasn't running at all).\n\nBut the real-site failure was reported on mobile. My fixes:\n1. `.js` gating — content visible by default (CSS only hides when JS confirmed running). This GUARANTEES the listings show even if the observer never fires.\n2. threshold 0 — more reliable firing for tall elements.\n3. Safety net on scroll — reveals anything on screen if the observer stalls.\n4. No-IO fallback — old browsers show everything.\n\nThe logic test confirms the observer + threshold 0 works for the tall group.\n\nNow — one more important verification: the safety net. Let me also verify the `.js` gating doesn't hide content when JS hasn't run — i.e., WITHOUT the .js class, `.reveal` stays visible (opacity 1). In the preview, the CSS I injected has `.js .reveal { opacity: 0 }` — without .js on <html>, reveals are visible. Let me confirm by removing .js... actually, that's trivially true from the CSS selector. Skip.\n\nNow the mobile sweep for the OTHER pages. Since the preview can't serve CSS, real mobile layout can't be measured there. But I can still check:\n1. Unstyled horizontal overflow (HTML-level wide elements) on all 8 pages\n2. Static CSS analysis — done mostly\n\nLet me run the unstyled overflow check on all pages. I'll navigate through each page and check scrollWidth > innerWidth, and list the widest offenders. Even unstyled, this catches: fixed-width images/SVGs/tables/iframes in the HTML.\n\nPages: index, why-choose-us, our-services, industries, programs, careers, contact, evaluation.\n\nNote: in unstyled mode, default block elements span 100% of the body which is 100% of the viewport — no overflow. Inline elements like images with width attrs, SVGs, tables with many columns, iframes (tradingview widget!) would overflow. The tradingview widget on index is an iframe with a script embed — width might be fixed (100% usually). Let me check.\n\nLet me write a small loop: for each page, navigate and evaluate. Actually preview_navigate to a different page — the preview is registered at careers.html htmlPath. Can I navigate to index.html? The htmlPath preview serves the single registered file. Navigating to another path → 404/chrome-error. Hmm.\n\nActually — earlier when the preview was registered at why-choose-us.html, preview_navigate to \"http://127.0.0.1:49270/why-choose-us.html\" failed (chrome-error) — that was a DIFFERENT server though. For htmlPath mode, the app serves the file at a URL. Can I navigate to a different file? The registration URL was http://127.0.0.1:53039/careers.html. Let me try navigating to http://127.0.0.1:53039/index.html — if the server serves the whole project directory, it works; if only the single file, it 404s.\n\nLet me test with index.html.","open":false,"collapse":"preview"},{"kind":"text","text":"The reveal fix works — the 2609px-tall Trading listings section now reveals reliably on scroll. Now let me sweep the other pages for HTML-level mobile overflowers. Let me see if the preview server can serve other pages:"},{"kind":"tool","id":"1ot8YhZ4m_Y","toolName":"preview_navigate","input":{"to":"http://127.0.0.1:53039/index.html"}},{"kind":"reasoning","id":"p23-69d9921b","text":"The preview server DOES serve other pages (index.html loaded)! So the sandbox serves the whole project directory statically — but css/js/images return \"NOT served\" (the fetch test showed that). Weird — HTML files are served but assets aren't. Whatever — I can navigate to each HTML page and run the unstyled overflow check.\n\nLet me run the overflow check on index.html now.","open":false,"collapse":"preview"},{"kind":"text","text":"The server serves other HTML pages. Let me run the overflow sweep across all pages:"},{"kind":"tool","id":"1ouVls7Cw94","toolName":"preview_evaluate","input":{"expression":"(() => { const iw = window.innerWidth; const sw = document.documentElement.scrollWidth; const bad = []; document.querySelectorAll('*').forEach(el => { const r = el.getBoundingClientRect(); if (r.right > iw + 2 || r.left < -2) { if (r.width > iw + 2) bad.push({ tag: el.tagName, id: el.id, cls: String(el.className).slice(0,40), w: Math.round(r.width), right: Math.round(r.right) }); } }); bad.sort((a,b) => b.w - a.w); return { page: location.pathname.split('/').pop(), iw, sw, overflow: sw > iw, offenders: bad.slice(0, 6) }; })()"}},{"kind":"reasoning","id":"p24-69d9921b","text":"index.html: no HTML-level overflow. Let me check the remaining pages: why-choose-us, our-services, industries, programs, careers, contact, evaluation. Let me navigate through them.","open":false,"collapse":"preview"},{"kind":"tool","id":"1ouoklmluZQ","toolName":"preview_navigate","input":{"to":"http://127.0.0.1:53039/why-choose-us.html"}},{"kind":"tool","id":"1ovCK25PF1g","toolName":"preview_evaluate","input":{"expression":"(() => { const iw = window.innerWidth; const sw = document.documentElement.scrollWidth; const bad = []; document.querySelectorAll('*').forEach(el => { const r = el.getBoundingClientRect(); if (r.width > iw + 2 && (r.right > iw + 2 || r.left < -2)) bad.push({ tag: el.tagName, id: el.id, cls: String(el.className).slice(0,40), w: Math.round(r.width), right: Math.round(r.right) }); }); bad.sort((a,b) => b.w - a.w); return { page: location.pathname.split('/').pop(), iw, sw, overflow: sw > iw, offenders: bad.slice(0, 6) }; })()"}},{"kind":"tool","id":"1ovTNY7QjRc","toolName":"preview_navigate","input":{"to":"http://127.0.0.1:53039/our-services.html"}},{"kind":"tool","id":"1ovluIC-Fxk","toolName":"preview_evaluate","input":{"expression":"(() => { cons