LD broken version. But wait — in the OLD version (before my fix), ALL reveals including the Virtual Assistant group would be hidden... but the user says Virtual Assistant SHOWS. So something IS revealing some content.\n\nHmm, interesting. Let me reconsider. What shows vs what doesn't:\n- Virtual Assistant (Administration group, 2nd group, 309px) — SHOWS\n- Trading group (1st group, 2609px, 9 listings) — DOESN'T show\n\nWhat's different? The height. 2609px vs 309px.\n\nWith the OLD observer (`threshold: 0.1`): \n- Virtual Assistant group: 309px tall, needs 10% = 31px visible → fires easily ✓\n- Trading group: 2609px tall, needs 261px visible → also should fire... when its top is within viewport...\n\nHmm, but WAIT — there's a subtle IntersectionObserver behavior with `threshold: 0.1` and a TALL element. For a 2609px element in a ~700px viewport, the intersection ratio peaks at 700/2609 = 0.27 when fully covering the viewport. To reach 0.1 (261px visible), the element must cover 261px of viewport. During a smooth scroll... the ratio passes through 0.1 → fires. Should work.\n\nBUT — what if the user NEVER SCROLLS through it in a way that reaches 10%? On mobile with fast flick scrolling... no, it would still pass through.\n\nHmm, hold on — actually, wait. Let me reconsider whether the user is seeing the OLD version. The `.js` gate: if the user's mobile is showing the NEW style.css but OLD main.js (or vice versa), weird things happen:\n\nCase A: NEW CSS + OLD JS. The NEW CSS has `.js .reveal` gating — reveals visible by default (no .js class since old JS doesn't add it... wait, old JS also... no, the OLD main.js didn't add the .js class). So with NEW CSS + OLD JS: `.js` never added → all reveals VISIBLE → ALL listings show. That's not the reported behavior.\n\nCase B: OLD CSS + NEW JS. OLD CSS has `.reveal { opacity: 0 }` unconditional. NEW JS adds `.js` (no effect on old CSS). Observer threshold 0 + safety net. All reveals should reveal... The Virtual Assistant shows, Trading doesn't. Hmm.\n\nCase C: BOTH new (hard refresh) — should all work.\n\nCase D: BOTH old (no refresh) — all reveals hidden, nothing shows. But Virtual Assistant shows.\n\nHmm — Case B or a mixed state. OR — the real difference: the Trading group is TALL and there's something else going on.\n\nWAIT. Let me reconsider the structure. Let me re-examine: maybe the Trading department-group doesn't have `.reveal`... no, I verified: line 153 `<div class=\"department-group reveal\">` (Trading), line 352 (Administration). Both reveal.\n\nHmm, what else could hide the Trading group specifically? Let me think about the HTML structure of the Trading group — 9 job-listing divs. Is there something structurally wrong INSIDE the Trading group that breaks rendering on mobile? Like an unclosed tag that swallows content? Earlier I checked the structure around the listings — looked fine. But let me re-verify the FULL Trading group for unclosed divs, especially around the transitions between the 9 jobs.\n\nActually — wait. Let me reconsider the heights from my preview measurement: group1 (Trading) = 2609px, group2 (Administration) = 309px. If the Trading group contains 9 job listings, each ~250px unstyled + padding... 9 × ~280 = 2500px ✓ consistent. So the Trading group content IS in the DOM and renders (it has height).\n\nSo why would it not SHOW on the user's phone? opacity: 0. The `.visible` class isn't being added to it on their phone, OR the `.js` class is present (new CSS) and the reveal never fires for it.\n\nThe most likely scenario: **the user's phone is loading the NEW CSS (with `.js` gating) and NEW JS — but the observer STILL doesn't fire for the tall Trading group on their specific mobile browser.** Why would it fire for the short group but not the tall one?\n\nOH WAIT. I think I might have it!! **iOS Safari has a known IntersectionObserver bug where `rootMargin` on the observer is IGNORED/not applied** — no wait, that would make it MORE likely to fire.\n\nLet me think about the opposite: maybe the user's browser is an OLD Android WebView or an older Chrome where `threshold: 0` behaves... no.\n\nHmm, actually — let me reconsider from scratch. What if the user IS seeing the OLD version (cached CSS+JS), and in the OLD version:\n- OLD CSS: `.reveal { opacity: 0 }` unconditional\n- OLD JS: observer threshold 0.1\n\nAnd on their phone, the observer fires for the SHORT group (Administration — needs only 31px visible, fires immediately when scrolled) but NOT for the TALL Trading group. Why wouldn't it fire for the tall one with threshold 0.1?\n\n**AH — I KNOW WHY!!** iOS Safari (and some Android browsers) have a KNOWN IntersectionObserver bug with `rootMargin` and TALL elements: when an element is taller than the viewport AND you scroll such that its top is above the viewport, some browsers report intersectionRatio incorrectly, or — more importantly — **with `threshold: 0.1` and a tall element, if the browser's intersection ratio computation rounds or the element's visible portion never registers...** \n\nActually, here's a MUCH more likely and well-documented issue: **`rootMargin: '0px 0px -50px 0px'` on iOS Safari 15.x had a bug where the bottom rootMargin made elements at the bottom of the page never intersect.** There are reports of rootMargin being buggy in some iOS versions.\n\nBut honestly — I can't verify their exact browser. What I CAN do is make the reveal system even MORE bulletproof for tall elements:\n\n**Option 1: Fire reveal on ANY intersection, regardless of ratio — threshold 0.** Already done in the new JS. But if they're on the old cached JS, they don't have it.\n\n**Option 2: REMOVE the rootMargin entirely.** The rootMargin could be the culprit on some browsers. With `threshold: 0` and NO rootMargin, the observer fires as soon as 1px enters the viewport — the most compatible config possible.\n\n**Option 3: Add a belt-and-braces approach — CSS-only guarantee.** The nuclear option: make `.reveal` NEVER hide content that's critical... can't do per-element in CSS.\n\n**Option 4: Make the reveal not depend on the observer at all for tall sections — use a simpler scroll listener as the PRIMARY mechanism instead of (or in addition to) the observer.**\n\nActually — let me step back and think about what's DIFFERENT about the user's phone vs my preview test. In my preview test, I injected the reveal CSS + ran the JS manually — the group revealed with threshold 0. But that's me running it in a desktop Chromium webview.\n\nThe user's phone: real browser, real files. If they hard-refreshed, they have the new JS (threshold 0 + safety net). The safety net should have caught the Trading group on scroll... The safety net reveals anything with `r.top < innerHeight * 1.1 && r.bottom > -innerHeight * 0.2` on scroll. The Trading group's top is way up the page when you scroll past it... \n\nOH WAIT. I think I see a REAL bug in my safety net now!! Look at the condition:\n\n```js\nif (r.top < window.innerHeight * 1.1 && r.bottom > -window.innerHeight * 0.2) {\n```\n\nWhen you've SCROLLED PAST the Trading group (it's above the viewport): `r.top < 1.1*vh` is TRUE (top is negative), but `r.bottom > -0.2*vh` — bottom is also negative if scrolled past → FALSE → not revealed. But by then, the OBSERVER should have revealed it when it WAS in view...\n\nHmm, so the safety net only helps for elements BELOW the fold or partially in view. It doesn't retroactively reveal scrolled-past elements. But the observer should handle in-view ones.\n\nLet me think about whether the observer could genuinely fail on their phone for the Trading group with threshold 0... Actually, you know what — there's one more possibility I haven't considered: **the `.department-group` elements might not be the reveal wrapper the user sees as \"the listings\"** — maybe each JOB LISTING is what matters, and... no, they're inside the group.\n\nHmm — actually, WAIT. Let me reconsider the whole thing. What if the problem is completely different: **the Trading group's 9 listings render FINE but are visually missing because of the `.job-listing` cards themselves — e.g., on mobile the cards have a background that matches the page, or the padding makes them invisible...** No — the user said ONLY Virtual Assistant shows; the 9 Trading cards are all missing. If it were a card-styling issue, they'd show as unstyled blocks.\n\nThe most plausible technical explanation remains: on the user's phone, the Trading group's `.reveal` never gets `.visible` (or the group is opacity:0). And the Administration group does. The difference: height (2609 vs 309).\n\n**The height-based reveal failure is a REAL, documented IntersectionObserver gotcha**: with `threshold: 0.1`, tall elements are problematic in some browsers because of how the ratio is calculated with rootMargin. With threshold 0 it should be fine. But if the user is on cached OLD JS (threshold 0.1) — the tall group fails while the short one succeeds. That matches PERFECTLY: short group needs 31px (easy), tall group needs 261px (harder, and buggy in some browsers with rootMargin).\n\nSo the user is very likely on the **OLD cached main.js** with the OLD observer (threshold 0.1 + rootMargin -50px). And my new JS would fix it — but they haven't gotten it (no hard refresh, or their phone browser aggressively caches).\n\nBUT I can't just tell them \"clear your cache\" — they need the site to WORK. Two-pronged approach:\n\n1. **Make the code even more bulletproof** (so even if my current fix is subtly wrong, the next version definitely works):\n   - Remove `rootMargin` entirely from the observer (it's the most likely browser-compat culprit)\n   - Keep threshold 0\n   - Make the safety net MORE aggressive: run it on load AND on scroll, revealing anything whose bounds overlap the viewport (top < vh && bottom > 0), and ALSO reveal elements scrolled past whose bottom was previously in view... actually simpler: make the safety net reveal ANY element that has EVER been within the viewport — track on scroll.\n   - Actually — the simplest bulletproof mechanism: replace the observer dependency for the reveal with a plain scroll handler as the primary reveal mechanism? No — the observer is fine when configured simply.\n\n2. **The REAL most-robust fix — remove the risk entirely:** Make the reveal animation NON-BLOCKING for content visibility: instead of `opacity: 0` until JS reveals, use a CSS-only animation that doesn't depend on JS at all for showing content... \n\nHmm, what about this: **give `.reveal` a fallback animation that makes content visible even if `.visible` never comes?** Like an animation that ends at opacity 1:\n```css\n.js .reveal {\n  opacity: 0;\n  animation: reveal-fallback 1.2s ease forwards 0.8s; /* shows content even if JS never adds .visible */\n}\n```\nWait — that would animate everything visible after 0.8s regardless of scroll position, killing the scroll-triggered effect. Unless the delay is long... no, that defeats the purpose.\n\nBetter idea — the scroll safety net made comprehensive: on ANY scroll, reveal every element whose bounding rect intersects the viewport, using a rAF-throttled scroll listener that runs EVERY scroll event (not debounced 120ms — 120ms debounce could miss on fast flicks!). And run it once on load too. This is a full fallback that doesn't rely on the observer's ratio math:\n\n```js\nfunction revealAllInView() {\n  const vh = window.innerHeight;\n  revealElements.forEach(el => {\n    if (el.classList.contains('visible')) return;\n    const r = el.getBoundingClientRect();\n    if (r.top < vh && r.bottom > 0) el.classList.add('visible');\n  });\n}\nwindow.addEventListener('scroll', revealAllInView, { passive: true });\nrevealAllInView();\n```\n\nThis reveals anything intersecting the viewport on every scroll frame. Combined with the observer (threshold 0, no rootMargin) as the primary, this is belt-and-braces.\n\nBut hold on — what if the user's phone is on OLD cached JS entirely? Then NOTHING I change in the files helps until they refresh. I should:\n1. Make the files as robust as possible (above).\n2. Tell the user to hard-refresh / clear cache, and explain WHY the Virtual Assistant showed but not the others (tall vs short reveal trigger) — it's the strongest evidence they're on the cached version.\n\nHmm wait — actually, let me reconsider ONE more time whether there's something in the CURRENT files that would cause exactly this behavior on a real phone WITH a fresh load. Let me re-examine my current JS observer:\n\n```js\ndocument.documentElement.classList.add('js');\nconst revealElements = document.querySelectorAll('.reveal');\nif ('IntersectionObserver' in window) {\n  const revealObserver = new IntersectionObserver((entries) => {\n    entries.forEach(entry => {\n      if (entry.isIntersecting) {\n        entry.target.classList.add('visible');\n        revealObserver.unobserve(entry.target);\n      }\n    });\n  }, { threshold: 0, rootMargin: '0px 0px -40px 0px' });\n  revealElements.forEach(el => revealObserver.observe(el));\n} else { ... }\n```\n\nWith threshold 0 and rootMargin -40px: the effective trigger is: element's bounding box intersects the viewport shrunk by 40px at the bottom. For the tall Trading group: when its top edge crosses the viewport top (or the -40px bottom boundary), it fires. This is the MOST reliable config... in a spec-compliant browser.\n\nIs there a browser where threshold 0 + rootMargin fails for tall elements? There IS a known iOS Safari bug (fixed in 15.4+): IntersectionObserver with rootMargin where elements at the BOTTOM of the page or tall elements don't fire correctly. Some reports: \"IntersectionObserver not firing for elements taller than viewport on iOS\". If the user's iPhone is on an older iOS, the observer might genuinely fail for the 2609px group while firing for the 309px group. \n\nThe scroll-based safety net is the reliable cross-browser fallback. Let me make the safety net the PRIMARY and most aggressive it can be, keep the observer as enhancement, remove rootMargin (zero-risk), and also add a page-load + interval check.\n\nActually — let me go even further and make the reveal system work WITHOUT IntersectionObserver entirely as the primary mechanism? A scroll listener is 100% compatible with every browser ever. Honestly, for a static marketing site, a simple scroll listener IS the better choice:\n\n```js\n// Primary: simple scroll check (works everywhere)\nfunction revealInView() {\n  const vh = window.innerHeight;\n  revealElements.forEach(el => {\n    if (el.classList.contains('visible')) return;\n    const r = el.getBoundingClientRect();\n    if (r.top < vh * 0.95 && r.bottom > 0) el.classList.add('visible');\n  });\n}\nwindow.addEventListener('scroll', revealInView, { passive: true });\nrevealInView(); // reveal above-fold on load\n```\n\nBut — a naive scroll listener checks ALL elements on EVERY scroll event = 18 getBoundingClientRect calls per scroll = fine performance-wise (that's cheap, browsers handle thousands of such checks). For 18 elements it's negligible. But layout thrash on fast scrolls... it's fine for 18 elements, honestly.\n\nHmm, but using BOTH observer and scroll listener could double-reveal (harmless, idempotent). Let me simplify: \n\n**Decision: Replace the observer with a hybrid:**\n1. Observer with threshold 0, NO rootMargin (fast path, spec-compliant browsers) \n2. Scroll listener as a redundant fallback that checks bounds (covers browser bugs)\n3. Load-time check (covers above-fold)\n4. .js gating (covers no-JS)\n\nWait — but there's a subtle issue with running the scroll fallback on every scroll: elements that were revealed by the observer are skipped (visible check). Fine.\n\nLet me also reconsider — should I keep the observer at all if the scroll listener is the reliable one? Yes, keep both — the observer avoids layout reads on every scroll in good browsers, and the listener catches the broken cases. Both idempotent.\n\nActually, let me simplify my life and make it dead simple and bulletproof:\n\n```js\ndocument.documentElement.classList.add('js');\nconst revealElements = document.querySelectorAll('.reveal');\nconst revealInView = () => {\n  const vh = window.innerHeight;\n  revealElements.forEach(el => {\n    if (el.classList.contains('visible')) return;\n    const r = el.getBoundingClientRect();\n    if (r.top < vh && r.bottom > 0) el.classList.add('visible');\n  });\n};\nif ('IntersectionObserver' in window) {\n  const revealObserver = new IntersectionObserver((entries) => {\n    entries.forEach(entry => {\n      if (entry.isIntersecting) {\n        entry.target.classList.add('visible');\n        revealObserver.unobserve(entry.target);\n      }\n    });\n  }, { threshold: 0 });\n  revealElements.forEach(el => revealObserver.observe(el));\n}\n// Redundant cross-browser fallback: works even if the observer misbehaves on mobile\nwindow.addEventListener('scroll', revealInView, { passive: true });\nwindow.addEventListener('resize', revealInView);\nrevealInView();\n```\n\nThis is clean:\n- Observer: threshold 0, NO rootMargin (max compat)\n- Scroll listener fallback: reveals anything intersecting viewport on every scroll (no debounce — for 18 elements it's cheap; hmm, should I throttle with rAF? For correctness on fast flicks, no debounce is safer. 18 getBoundingClientRect per scroll event — browsers coalesce scroll events with rAF anyway. Fine.)\n- Initial revealInView() on load: reveals above-fold content immediately even if the observer never fires\n\nAnd I'll keep the `.js` gating CSS. Also — should I remove the `threshold: 0.1`-era behavior... done.\n\nWait, one concern about `revealInView` running on every scroll with `r.top < vh`: elements just below the fold with `r.top < vh` (i.e., any element whose top is within the viewport) — reveals as soon as the element's top enters the viewport. Same trigger as the observer. Good — consistent.\n\nBut hmm — with the scroll listener revealing on `r.top < vh