S Safari\" bug.\n2. **body { overflow-x: hidden }** on iOS Safari: known to cause content clipping and can create a new scrolling/containing context that breaks rendering of tall content. It's widely reported that `overflow-x: hidden` on body/html causes iOS Safari to NOT render content below the fold in some cases.\n\nBoth point to the same fix direction.\n\nGiven the symptom (giant section blank on iOS only, small section fine), the two most likely causes:\n1. The giant composited layer (reveal transform on the 4000px+ group)\n2. body overflow-x: hidden + tall content\n\nThe FIX (robust regardless of which specific mechanism):\n1. **Remove `.reveal` from the giant department-group elements** and put it on the individual job-listing cards instead (small elements, safe). \n2. **Consider removing `overflow-x: hidden` from body** and instead use `overflow-x: clip` (supported in modern Safari 16+) or apply overflow-x hidden only where needed. Actually — `overflow-x: clip` on html/body is the modern fix for horizontal scroll prevention WITHOUT creating a scroll container. On older iOS (Safari 15 and earlier), `clip` isn't supported... but the fallback would be no overflow rule, and horizontal overflow is prevented by the layout itself (I verified no elements overflow horizontally). Hmm — actually, removing body overflow-x hidden could reintroduce horizontal scrolling if anything overflows. My sweep showed no horizontal overflow on any page. But that was the UNSTYLED preview; the styled page... the CSS has width: 90% containers etc. — unlikely to overflow horizontally. Risky to remove without testing.\n\nLet me be strategic. The PRIMARY fix for the reported bug: per-card reveals (remove reveal from the giant groups). This is safe, definitely helps (no more giant composited layers), and looks better (staggered card reveals).\n\nFor body overflow-x: hidden — I could change it to `overflow-x: clip` which is the safe modern equivalent that does NOT create a scroll container (avoiding the iOS tall-content clipping bug class). Modern Safari supports `overflow: clip` (Safari 16+). For older iOS, `overflow-x: hidden` was the buggy one anyway; `clip` either works or falls back... if unsupported, the declaration is ignored → no horizontal clip → but no horizontal overflow exists anyway (verified). So `overflow-x: clip` is strictly safer on iOS and harmless elsewhere. Let me check what html has (line 32) — maybe html has overflow-x hidden too? The grep showed line 41 for body. Let me check line 32-45.\n\nLet me also verify the full body rule and check whether `.section` has any overflow.\n\nThen implement:\n1. careers.html: move `.reveal` from department-groups → individual job-listing cards (with staggered transition-delay for elegance? Keep simple — no delay, or small stagger via inline style).\n2. style.css: change body overflow-x: hidden → overflow-x: clip (keep hidden as fallback via @supports? Simpler: `overflow-x: clip;` — if unsupported, no rule → fine since no horizontal overflow).\n3. Check other pages for other giant reveal containers (card-grid, benefit-grid etc.) — those could be 3000-4000px on mobile stacked. Hmm — benefit-grid (8 items), value-grid (4 items), card-grid (4-6 items), stats-grid (5 items) — on mobile these stack to maybe 2500-4000px. If any hit the iOS limit, they'd blank too. To be safe, the reveal-per-card approach should apply there too... but that's a big refactor across all pages.\n\nWAIT — hold on. Let me reconsider. If the limit is ~4096px and grids on mobile are ~2500-3500px, they're borderline. The careers Trading group at ~5000px is way over. The user only reported the careers issue. Let me fix careers definitively, and for the other pages, check which reveal containers could exceed ~3500px on mobile and apply per-child reveals where risky.\n\nActually — let me reconsider the ROOT fix once more. There's an even simpler global solution: **remove the `transform` from the `.reveal` animation and keep only opacity.** Hmm — does opacity alone avoid the iOS layer-size bug? No — opacity < 1 also composites. BUT: after the transition ends, opacity: 1 → layer demoted. The bug is about layers that FAIL to paint. With opacity-only, the layer still exists during the transition. If the giant element fails to paint as a layer, it fails regardless of what promoted it.\n\nHmm, so the real fix must be: **never composite the giant element** → don't animate it → reveal its children instead. OR make the animation not use compositing at all (animate with a non-compositing property — e.g., just `opacity` on a very short transition... no, still composites).\n\nActually — wait, there's ANOTHER classic iOS fix: adding `transform: translateZ(0)` or `-webkit-transform: translateZ(0)` to FORCE layers... no, that's for flicker fixes, opposite direction.\n\nLet me also reconsider: is the giant-element blank really caused by the reveal transition, or does it happen even without any animation? The user said the section shows as blank on iOS. If it's the raw element height (no animation involved), then even removing reveal won't help — the 5000px group itself would fail. But the group is just a plain div with no styling (no transform/opacity without reveal) — plain divs that tall render fine on iOS (the whole page is thousands of px tall). The layer-size bug applies to COMPOSITED layers, not regular document flow content. So removing the reveal (→ no compositing) should fix it.\n\nLet me also double check: does `.job-listing` itself composite? `overflow: hidden` + `border-radius` + gradient background — YES, iOS composites elements with border-radius + overflow hidden (this is exactly what promotes them). Each card is ~500px → well under the limit → fine. The GROUP is what's over the limit. Removing reveal from the group and revealing cards individually: the cards composite individually (safe), the group doesn't composite (plain div, safe). \n\nAlso the `.department-title` — plain. Fine.\n\nLet me now also handle the possibility that it's NOT the layer limit but the `body overflow-x: hidden` bug. Fixing BOTH is the belt-and-braces move:\n1. Per-card reveals on careers (removes giant composited layer)\n2. body overflow-x: hidden → clip (removes the iOS scroll-container bug class)\n\nLet me look at the exact body rule first, then implement. Also let me get more research confirmation — the researcher result was empty. Let me read a StackOverflow article about the iOS tall layer issue to confirm. Actually, I'm confident enough in the mechanism (it's a well-known WebKit limitation), and the fix (smaller composited elements) is correct regardless. Let me verify the body rule and implement.","open":false,"collapse":"preview"},{"kind":"text","text":"`body { overflow-x: hidden }` at line 41 is a second known iOS Safari trigger (it creates a scroll container that can clip tall content). Let me see the exact rule and confirm the plan:"},{"kind":"tool","id":"1p9KFELrm0s","toolName":"run_terminal_command","input":{"command":"sed -n '28,48p' REALITY-FOREX-TRADING-/css/style.css","process_type":"SYNC","timeout_seconds":30}},{"kind":"ad","id":"p6-e21365ff","ad":{"adText":"Stop struggling with website layouts and launch a faster, simpler path with DropGenius AI-powered dropshipping.","title":"AI-Powered Shopify Stores: From Idea to Launch—Fast","cta":"Start Building","url":"https://www.dropgenius.com/","favicon":"https://icons.duckduckgo.com/ip3/www.dropgenius.com.ico","clickUrl":"https://api.trygravity.ai/track/click?p=gAAAAABqdGIin9xBXPkLfa9RwdRYpks8BL9NXIhcEJm1jtgUQzzplOvwBijfys0hNlXlZcOboGUv0qNQ_Zn69otmDuftp-UHMAGk03VydXuGGhwao7xtQZpflJcdJnRwkzfdEeieC2bV6OS3BPxSEMYfMHr-aJj9UVEwRE8p2bgskfRJY3gHrSyo9vrk64Vo8E0NjTPSlDP_u-2Dse0csSbPdvcZlYWwTWRsHgJE2oZfxNOMI82Y5jET-nC8xzCTSQ6ToroK1ysztHi6ZOPk5gn7DW_hS_fO_IC0I4EDAiKkIC4lkyqlBnz9vq2oRVT5cF3truqxBCRcEIGckzIbk69wJKxNz8TYdDmhLrJ9GDgSh4kN58rbWp3VcRqlgViwW0tzAHFuP6Pb-pzOISM3naeneMV5kcbsWnSEsv4fUB8_N_zqnInqu8Hk088ozVEcGUvNT4ozTDLh5I6