er Safari, the declaration is ignored — but there's no horizontal overflow anyway (verified), so nothing breaks.\n\nHmm — should I keep `hidden` as a fallback for older browsers? The cascade can't have both. `@supports (overflow: clip)` block:\n```css\nbody {\n  ...\n  overflow-x: hidden;\n}\n@supports (overflow: clip) {\n  body { overflow-x: clip; }\n}\n```\nHmm — on iOS Safari < 16, `@supports (overflow: clip)` → false → keeps `hidden` (the buggy one). On iOS 16+ → clip (fixed). The buggy versions are iOS < 16... so this doesn't help old iOS. But actually — `overflow-x: hidden` is only PART of the problem; the main fix is per-card reveals. And honestly, `overflow-x: hidden` on body isn't universally buggy — it's the COMBINATION with huge composited layers that's fatal. With per-card reveals, the giant layer is gone, so `overflow-x: hidden` is much less dangerous. But to be safe, let me use `clip` where supported and keep it simple:\n\n```css\nbody {\n  ...\n  overflow-x: clip; /* prevents horizontal scroll without the iOS Safari tall-content clipping bug of 'hidden' */\n}\n```\n\nSimple. If a browser doesn't support `clip`, no horizontal clipping — and there's no horizontal overflow to worry about. Clean.\n\n**Fix 3 — check OTHER pages for giant reveal containers.** Let me identify reveal targets that could exceed ~3500px on mobile:\n- index.html: `.card-grid reveal` (4 cards?), `.why-grid reveal` (6 cards — 6 × 400px = 2400px, borderline OK), testimonial-slider, stats, hero elements...\n- careers: benefits `.benefit-grid reveal` (8 items × ~300px = 2400px OK), `.timeline reveal` (6 steps × ~250px = 1500px OK)\n- programs: `.program-card reveal` — WAIT! Programs page: each `.program-card` is a reveal — cards are individual, ~600-800px each on mobile. Individual cards are fine (< 4096). But are the cards grouped in a giant wrapper with reveal? From the earlier grep: programs.html has `<div class=\"program-card reveal\">` ×7 — each card IS the reveal target. Individual cards = safe. ✓\n- services/industries: card-grids as reveal — check sizes.\n- evaluation/contact: forms — small.\n- why-choose-us: `two-col reveal` (chart art + text — maybe 800px), `comparison-table` section (not reveal? it's `reveal` on the table wrapper — the table on mobile is ~1500px, OK).\n\nLet me grep all `.reveal` containers with their likely mobile heights to find any other giant ones. The risky ones: `.card-grid.reveal`, `.benefit-grid.reveal`, `.value-grid.reveal`, `.stats-grid.reveal` etc. — grids with many stacked cards on mobile. 6+ cards × 400px = 2400px+ — under 4096, probably fine. The careers Trading group (9 × 550px = 5000px) was uniquely over.\n\nTo be thorough, let me find ALL grid containers with reveal and estimate. Actually — let me just check the grids with the most items. Let me grep the HTML for grid containers with reveal.\n\nActually — better: let me make the fix GLOBAL and SAFE: change the reveal animation itself to not promote giant layers? Can't — the layer size is the element's size.\n\nAlternative global safety: reduce the risk by NOT using `transform` in the reveal... doesn't help (opacity composites too).\n\nHmm — actually there IS one more global approach: **use `transition: opacity` only, no transform, AND cap the transition so compositing is brief**... still composites the giant element during the 0.8s. If the bug fires during composition, the layer stays broken.\n\nThe cleanest GLOBAL fix: ensure NO reveal element is gigantic. The department-groups were the worst. Let me sweep all pages for reveal targets and estimate sizes. Grids with many items that stack on mobile are the candidates. Let me check the item counts in each grid.\n\nLet me look at each page's reveal + grid structure quickly.","open":false,"collapse":"preview"},{"kind":"text","text":"`body { overflow-x: hidden }` confirmed. Now let me sweep every page for other reveal containers that could grow giant on mobile (the same iOS risk):"},{"kind":"tool","id":"1p-TPLacR2Y","toolName":"run_terminal_command","input":{"command":"grep -n 'grid reveal\\|group reveal\\|grid-container reveal\\|class=\".*grid.*reveal\\|class=\".*group.*reveal' REALITY-FOREX-TRADING-/*.html | head -30","process_type":"SYNC","timeout_seconds":30}},{"kind":"reasoning","id":"p8-e21365ff","text":"Giant reveal containers to evaluate:\n1. **careers.html:72** `.card-grid why-grid reveal` — \"Why Join Reality FX\" — how many cards? Earlier saw 6 cards (Career Growth, Remote, Continuous Learning, Rewards, Supportive Team, Work on Real Projects). 6 cards on mobile ≈ 6 × 450px = 2700px. Under 4096. Borderline-safe but let me check the card count.\n2. **careers.html:153** `.department-group reveal` — Trading, 9 jobs ≈ 5000px. **OVER THE LIMIT** ← the reported bug.\n3. **careers.html:352** `.department-group reveal` — Administration, 1 job ≈ 500px. Fine.\n4. **careers.html:453** `.benefit-grid reveal` — 8 benefits ≈ 8 × 300px = 2400px. Borderline-safe.\n5. **why-choose-us.html:234** `.stats-grid reveal` — 5 stats ≈ 5 × 250px = 1250px. Fine.\n\nHmm — also, what about other pages' grids that don't have \"reveal\" in the class string but are inside a `.reveal` wrapper? Like `<div class=\"reveal\"><div class=\"grid\">` — the wrapper is the reveal target. Let me also check for bare `class=\"reveal\"` wrappers around grids. Earlier the preview showed careers reveals: `.container two-col reveal`, `section-header reveal`, `reveal` (FAQ), `careers-form reveal`, `container reveal`. \n\nThe FAQ `reveal` div (careers): 6 accordion items ≈ 6 × 100px = 600px. Fine.\nThe `.container reveal` (CTA): small.\n\nWhat about other pages — index has `.card-grid`? Let me check index.html and the services/industries/programs pages for reveal targets around big content. Let me grep index.html reveals.\n\nActually — the biggest risk beyond careers is any grid with 6+ tall cards. Let me check index.html and our-services.html and industries.html reveals quickly.","open":false,"collapse":"preview"},{"kind":"tool","id":"1p-_WoMXsjQ","toolName":"run_terminal_command","input":{"command":"grep -n 'reveal' REALITY-FOREX-TRADING-/index.html | head -20; echo '===== services/industries/programs/eval/contact:'; grep -n 'reveal' REALITY-FOREX-TRADING-/our-services.html REALITY-FOREX-TRADING-/industries.html REALITY-FOREX-TRADING-/evaluation.html REALITY-FOREX-TRADING-/contact.html | grep -v 'section-header\\|class=\"reveal\"\\|two-col' | head -20","process_type":"SYNC","timeout_seconds":30}},{"kind":"reasoning","id":"p9-e21365ff","text":"Excellent news — most pages already use **per-card reveals** (each card has its own `reveal` class):\n- index.html: `.card reveal`, `.glass-card reveal`, `.meter-box reveal` — all individual small elements ✓\n- industries.html: `.industry-card reveal` — individual cards ✓\n- programs.html: `.program-card reveal` — individual cards ✓\n- The only GIANTS: careers' two `.department-group reveal` containers\n\nSo the fix is surgical:\n1. **careers.html**: Remove `reveal` from the two department-groups, add `reveal` to each of the 10 `.job-listing` cards.\n2. **style.css**: Change body `overflow-x: hidden` → `overflow-x: clip` (safer on iOS, avoids the scroll-container clipping bug class).\n3. The other grids on careers (why-grid 6 cards ≈ 2700px, benefit-grid 8 ≈ 2400px) are under the limit — but let me reconsider: are they safe? 6 cards on mobile... each \"why\" card has an emoji + title + paragraph ≈ 250-350px → 6 × 300 = 1800px. Fine. benefit-grid 8 × 250 = 2000px. Fine. Both under 4096. Leave them.\n\nWait — one more consideration about the why-grid: `.card-grid why-grid reveal` — the container is the reveal, and it contains 6 cards. On mobile 1 column → ~1800-2700px. Under the limit but not by a huge margin on a narrow phone with tall cards. Hmm — the iOS limit is ~4096px for the layer. Even 2700px is safe. OK.\n\nAlso — the FAQ `reveal` div on careers (6 accordions ≈ 600-800px) fine.\n\nNow let me also reconsider the `.js .reveal` gating + the new per-card reveal: each job-listing card gets reveal — 10 cards. revealInView iterates 28 reveals total on careers. Fine.\n\nLet me implement:\n\n**careers.html changes:**\n1. `<div class=\"department-group reveal\">` (Trading) → `<div class=\"department-group\">`\n2. Each of the 9 Trading `.job-listing` divs → `<div class=\"job-listing reveal\">`\n3. `<div class=\"department-group reveal\">` (Administration) → `<div class=\"department-group\">`\n4. Virtual Assistant `.job-listing` → `<div class=\"job-listing reveal\">`\n\nFor a nicer staggered feel, add small inline transition-delays to the cards (e.g., every other card 0.05s)? Keep it simple — no delays, the scroll reveal is enough. Actually, a subtle stagger looks premium: cards 1-9 get delay increasing by 0.04s. Hmm — but inline styles on 10 cards is verbose. The reveal transition is 0.8s; stagger makes it feel choreographed. The user loves classy animations. Let me add a CSS rule instead:\n\n```css\n.job-listing.reveal:nth-child(...) \n```\n— nth-child on job-listing within the group doesn't work simply because the title h3 is the first child. Could use `.department-group .job-listing:nth-of-type(n)`. Eh — let me keep it SIMPLE and not add stagger; the reveal already animates nicely. Don't over-engineer.\n\n**style.css change:**\n- body overflow-x: hidden → clip, with a comment.\n\nLet me apply these. First careers.html — I need to replace:\n1. Line 153: `<div class=\"department-group reveal\">` → `<div class=\"department-group\">`\n2. The 9 Trading job listings — each `<div class=\"job-listing\">` → `<div class=\"job-listing reveal\">`\n3. Line 352: `<div class=\"department-group reveal\">` → `<div class=\"department-group\">`\n4. The Virtual Assistant `<div class=\"job-listing\">` → `<div class=\"job-listing reveal\">`\n\nThe str_replace for `<div class=\"job-listing\">` with allowMultiple... there are 10 occurrences. If I use allowMultiple: true, all 10 get changed at once. But careful — are ALL `.job-listing` divs inside department-groups? Yes, per the structure. And the closing `</div>` tags don't need changes.\n\nSo:\n- Replace `<div class=\"department-group reveal\">` (×2) → `<div class=\"department-group\">` — allowMultiple true.\n- Replace `<div class=\"job-listing\">` (×10) → `<div class=\"job-listing reveal\">` — allowMultiple true.\n\nThat's clean and surgical.\n\nWait — but hold on. Let me reconsider whether removing the reveal from the group and putting it on the cards ACTUALLY fixes the iOS issue. The mechanism I hypothesized: the giant group gets composited (via reveal transform) → layer > 4096px → iOS fails to paint → blank. By removing the reveal from the group, the group is a plain div → not composited → renders. The cards get reveal → each card composited individually (~500px) → fine. ✓ This directly addresses the mechanism.\n\nALSO the body overflow-x: clip change addresses the other known iOS trigger. ✓\n\nLet me also think — should I double check the \".js\" gating will still work with cards? Yes — `.js .reveal` applies to any element with reveal class.\n\nOne more thing — with the reveal on each card, the `revealInView` + observer now monitor 10 more elements. revealInView checks `r.top < vh && r.bottom > 0` per card — each card is revealed as it enters view. ✓\n\nNow — let me ALSO reconsider whether the interval sweep + everything will still catch cards: yes, same mechanism.\n\nLet me apply the edits.","open":false,"collapse":"preview"},{"kind":"text","text":"Most pages already use per-card reveals — only the careers department groups are the giant composited layers. The fix is surgical: move the reveal from the giant groups onto each individual job card, and switch `body` to the iOS-safe `overflow-x: clip`:"},{"kind":"tool","id":"1p__8Y7AXPw","toolName":"str_replace","input":{"path":"REALITY-FOREX-TRADING-/careers.html","replacements":[{"oldString":"<div class=\"department-group reveal\">","newString":"<div class=\"department-g