n5. Register preview so they can watch it live\n6. Review\n\nGiven the size, let me be strategic. The slide extraction of ~740 images could be heavy but is doable. pdftoppm is the tool (renders PDF pages to PNG). pdfimages extracts embedded images but for Nearpod exports, pages are rendered designs — pdftoppm at reasonable DPI is right. Let me use pdftoppm -png -r 72 or 100.\n\nActually wait — the pages that are pure content (images) vs quiz pages. If I render ALL pages as images, the quiz pages will also render as images — but I want the quiz pages to be interactive HTML quizzes using their real questions. Hmm. The quiz slides in the PDF contain the questions as rendered images, but I already have the question TEXT extracted. So I should:\n- Extract all slides as images (for the lesson player to show content slides)\n- But quiz slides — I have text; I could either show the slide image + build interactive answer options, or skip slide images for quiz pages and show interactive questions.\n\nCleanest approach: render every page as an image (so nothing is lost — including quiz slide visuals), AND build the interactive quiz engine from the extracted text (so quizzes are clickable). In the lesson player, content slides show as images; at quiz positions, the interactive quiz appears (with the slide image optional).\n\nThat's a lot of engineering for one turn, but the user wants to see Phase 1. Let me structure it as a real build with todos. Given the scale, I'll:\n\n1. Extract images (background-ish, big command — do it first, it takes time)\n2. Build the OS app (this is the main work)\n3. Chapter 1 quiz data fully (their real questions), and as much quiz data as feasible for other chapters — actually, all 13 chapters' quiz questions I have extracted. Building all 300 questions into JSON by hand in one turn is a LOT of tokens. Let me do: full quiz data for all chapters but... realistically, I can encode the questions I extracted. That's a big data file. Maybe do Chapter 