ion, correlation, session risk, the 1% rule nuance, expectancy, etc.\n- Map enhancements to the OS: a Risk Calculator tool (position size, pip value, R-multiples), interactive scenarios, a \"Risk Audit\" checkpoint.\n\nAlso, I should probably read the Chapter 7 quiz text again — I have it from earlier extraction (in this conversation). Yes, I have the full Ch7 quiz text. Let me also quickly pull the other chapters' quiz data for QC... but that's 13 chapters; the user said \"where you think you need to add your input\" — they trust my judgment on scope. Best: deliver a strong Risk Management QC + enrichment doc now (their stated priority), and offer to do the same chapter-by-chapter (or a full sweep doc).\n\nShould I also build the Risk Calculator tool into the OS? That would be an amazing concrete addition — \"Risk Management is the most important aspect\" + \"we could create the most powerful academy\" + \"I'm ready!!!\" — a working risk calculator in the OS Laboratory section would demonstrate the \"Experience/Experiment\" stages. It's a meaningful but bounded build: a position-size calculator (the exact formula from their Ch7 quiz: Net liquidation × %Risk / (Stop loss distance × $value per pip)), a pip value calculator, and an R-multiple converter. I could add it as a new OS view (\"#/lab\" → Laboratory with the Risk Calculator), replacing the \"SOON\" placeholder for Laboratory with a real first tool.\n\nThat combo — (1) deep QC doc on Risk Management + (2) a live Risk Calculator tool in the OS — directly answers \"add your input to increase the quality\" and \"I'm ready!!!\" without over-scoping.\n\nLet me plan:\n1. Re-read Ch7 extracted text (I have it in context — the quiz questions are all there). Also skim Ch2/Ch7 formulas.\n2. Write the QC + enrichment doc: `REALITY-FOREX-TRADING-/OS-QUALITY-PASS.md` (or similar) — focused first on Risk Management:\n   - What's already excellent\n   - Corrections/flags on specific quiz questions (with exact quotes)\n   - Missing concepts worth adding (drawdown, risk of ruin, expectancy nuance, correlation, psychology loop, position sizing edge cases, broker realities: leverage changes, margin calls, negative balance protection)\n   - Hacks/tricks (practical pro tips)\n   - A \"Risk Pillar\" checklist\n   - Suggested OS enhancements mapped\n3. Build the Risk Calculator into the OS (new Laboratory view with position size + pip value + R-multiple tools), wire it into the nav (\"Laboratory\" becomes active, no more SOON for it... or keep Lab as the calculator's home with Journal/Mentor still SOON).\n4. Verify in preview, review, summarize.\n\nWait — scope check. The user's message is enthusiasm + quality control request. Building the calculator is a bonus that fits \"add your input... hacks, tricks\" and \"powerful\". I'll do both doc + calculator. But let me be careful about turn size — the doc can be very thorough; the calculator is a focused JS/CSS addition. I think it's achievable.\n\nActually — let me reconsider whether to also draft answer keys for Ch3-13 now. They said \"im ready\" to my follow-ups. My previous suggest included \"Draft the answer keys for Chapters 3–13\". That's a big data task (300+ questions). Combined with QC doc + calculator, the turn becomes huge. Better: focus this turn on Risk Management QC + the calculator (both directly tied to what they highlighted). Then offer keys chapter-by-chapter as next steps.\n\nLet me structure the QC doc well. I'll use my trading knowledge for the expert content. Let me be careful to be accurate:\n\nKey risk management knowledge to include:\n- Position sizing formula: Lots = (Account × Risk%) / (Stop distance in pips × $ per pip per lot). Their Ch7 question: (3000 × 1.4%) / (10 pips × $0.10 per pip) = 42 / 1 = 42... let me compute: 3000 × 0.014 = $42 risk. $42 / ($0.10 × 10 pips) = $42 / $1 = 42 contracts... wait the options were \"42 contracts × 1000 (micro lot) / 68 / 84\". Let me verify: risk per trade = $42. Stop loss distance = 10 pips. Dollar value per pip = $0.100 (EUR/USD micro lot). So per contract: 10 pips × $0.10 = $1.00 risk per contract. $42 / $1 = 42 contracts. Answer: 42 contracts × 1000 (micro lot) ✓. Good — the math checks out; I can validate their quiz question in the doc (nice QC signal).\n- Win rate + R:R: expectancy formula E = (W% × avgWin) − (L% × avgLoss). Their question: $1000 account, 65% win, 35% loss, avg win 1.5R, avg loss 1R. E = 0.65 × ($1000 × 1.5/100... hmm their framing \"1R is to 1:5R\" — the formula given: %Win × (Average win) − %loss × (Average loss). 0.65 × 15 − 0.35 × 10 = 9.75 − 3.5 = $6.25. Options: $8 / $6.25 / $5 → $6.25 ✓. Good — validates.\n- Win rate: 35/50 = 70% ✓.\n- Drawdown: recovery math — a 50% drawdown needs 100% gain to recover. Huge teaching point.\n- Risk of ruin formula.\n- Kelly criterion: fractional Kelly for traders.\n- 1% vs 2% rule, adjusting for drawdown (volatility-based sizing).\n- Correlation risk (they cover it — EURUSD/USDCAD option question; validate).\n- Session risk vs per-trade risk.\n- Margin call / maintenance margin / stop-out levels.\n- Leverage is a double-edged sword; the \"higher leverage = better\" false question.\n- Negative balance protection (regulator-specific).\n- News events and slippage — risk beyond stop loss (gap risk).\n- Journaling / process vs outcome (their philosophy).\n- The R-multiple mindset and why \"R\" thinking separates pros.\n- Practical hacks: risk 0.5-1% for beginners, half-risk after 3 losses in a day, never add to losers, scale in/out, take profits into strength, check spread before entering, use demo to test systems.\n\nQuiz QC flags in Ch7 (from the extracted text):\n1. \"I want to open a position worth $150 000, i have $5000 as my balance which leverage could i use\" — 150000/5000 = 30 → 1:30 ✓ correct.\n2. \"If your personal equity in your account falls below 25% of the current market value of the purchased asset you will be subject to a margin call\" — this is a simplified stateme