act\") && cmd //c \"\\\"$EXE\\\" /VERYSILENT /SUPPRESSMSGBOXES /NORESTART /DIR=\\\"$DEST\\\" /SP- /NOCANCEL\" ; echo \"exit=$?\"; ls tesseract/tesseract.exe 2>/dev/null && echo \"INSTALLED\" || (ls tesseract/ 2>/dev/null | head -5; echo \"not yet\")","process_type":"SYNC","timeout_seconds":300}},{"kind":"reasoning","id":"p8-87976141","text":"The escaping is mangled — the `\\\"` got passed literally into cmd. When bash passes `cmd //c \"\\\"$EXE\\\" ...\"` — the MSYS layer converts `//c` to `/c`, and the quotes... The error shows cmd received `'\"C:\\Users\\...exe\"'` including literal quote chars as part of the command name.\n\nLet me try a different approach: use `cmd //c start //wait` or use MSYS2's ability to run Windows exes directly — the original \"Permission denied\" might have been a transient thing (maybe the download was incomplete when first tried — no, it completed).\n\nAlternative: MSYS bash CAN run Windows exes usually. The \"Permission denied\" (126) may be because the exe is large and MSYS checks... Actually exit 126 \"Permission denied\" for an exe usually means the file lacks execute permission in the MSYS view or the PE is blocked. The file has rwxr-xr-x so that's fine. Hmm — could be that Windows Defender blocked or the exe requires elevation which MSYS reports as permission denied? Inno Setup installers typically request elevation (requireAdministrator) — running without admin → UAC prompt blocked in non-interactive shell → \"Permission denied\"!\n\nThat's likely it: the UB-Mannheim installer is built with requireAdministrator. So it needs elevation. I can't run elevated commands without approval (request_elevation exists for that). But installing tesseract globally via an elevated installer is a significant system change — I'd rather avoid asking the user for admin for a dev tool.\n\nBetter: find a PORTABLE tesseract zip (no installer). Options:\n1. There's a well-known portable build: \"tesseract-ocr/tesseract\" doesn't publish Windows zips, but several repos do, e.g.:\n   - https://github.com/tesseract-ocr/tesseract/releases — Linux only\n   - https://github.com/UB-Mannheim/tesseract — exe only\n   - **The \"A9T9\" / \"tesseract-ocr-w64\" fork by cchiw?** unsure\n   - **Portable from \"Diamant...\"?** unsure\n2. Use the old \"tesseract-ocr-w64-setup-4.0.0.20181030.exe\"? Also installer.\n3. **Use Windows built-in OCR?** Not scriptable easily.\n4. **Use a different OCR approach entirely:**\n   - **PaddleOCR / EasyOCR via python** — python isn't installed.\n   - **Node OCR** — node isn't installed (could download portable node + a JS OCR lib like tesseract.js — tesseract.js needs its own WASM traineddata from CDN; it works in-browser or node. If I had node, `npx tesseract.js` — heavy.\n   - **tesseract.js in the browser**: I could run OCR IN the preview page using tesseract.js from a CDN! The preview webview has internet (Google Fonts loaded). I could build a tiny \"OCR harness\" HTML page that loads tesseract.js from CDN, and runs OCR on the slide images (which are served from the same server) — and I read results via preview_evaluate! That's clever and avoids ANY local install. tesseract.js v5 from CDN: https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/tesseract.js@5/dist/tesseract.min.js — it loads the WASM + traineddata from CDN too. OCR quality with eng.traineddata is decent on clean text at high DPI.\n\nThat's actually a robust path given my environment: use the browser + tesseract.js CDN as the OCR engine, driven by preview_evaluate. The 741-slide OCR would be slow per slide (2-5s each), but I only need Chapter 1 now (22 slides) — a few minutes of sequential OCR in the preview. And the server already serves the images.\n\nBut wait — the preview webview has been flaky (crashes after reloads). Running 22 sequential OCRs might be fragile. Still, it's the most reliable given no admin installs.\n\nAlternatively: check if the installer maybe CAN run with elevation via request_elevation — the tool exists for \"admin access genuinely required\". Installing a system tool is a system change though. I'd rather not. Actually — request_elevation shows the user the command and they approve. The user is right here and enthusiastic (\"I'm ready!!\"). But installing software system-wide on their machine is heavier than this task needs. The tesseract.js-in-browser approach avoids it entirely.\n\nLet me also reconsider: do I actually need full OCR of every slide? For Chapter 1 native rebuild, I need the content of slides 1-7 (content slides) and 8-10 (polls — but polls I can rebuild from the extracted text: slide 8 poll \"What kind of trader do you want to be\" with 4 options ✓ extracted; slide 10 self-assessment ✓ extracted) and slide 22 (final). Quiz slides 11-21 already interactive. So really: OCR slides 1-7 + 22. That's 8 slides. Very manageable.\n\nPlan: build a small OCR harness page served by the same server: `os/tools/ocr.html` (or .freebuff?) that loads tesseract.js from CDN, takes a query param or a list, OCRs images, and posts results to window. I'll drive it via preview_navigate + preview_evaluate polling. Actually simpler: an HTML page with a button that OCRs a given slide path and shows the text; I'll call it via evaluate for each slide. But preview_evaluate runs in the page context — I can call tesseract directly: navigate to the OS page (which already loads), then dynamically inject tesseract.js script, then call Tesseract.recognize(url). That avoids a separate page entirely!\n\nSteps:\n1. preview_navigate to a fresh OS page load (bare URL).\n2. preview_evaluate: inject `<script src=\"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/tesseract.js@5/dist/tesseract.min.js\">`, wait for load.\n3. For each slide 1..7,22: await Tesseract.recognize(slideUrl, 'eng', { logger: m => {} }) → collect text.\n4. Return all texts.\n\nConcern: CORS — fetching the slide image from the same origin (127.0.0.1:8910) is fine. tesseract.js loads WASM/core + lang data from CDN (jsdelivr) — the page has internet. Should work.\n\nConcern: timeouts — preview_evaluate has a 10s limit per evaluation (\"Evaluation exceeded the 10s limit\"). OCR of one slide might take 2-6s... risky per-call. I can do ONE slide per evaluate call (each within 10s), 8 calls. Or chunk 2-3 per call. Let me do one or two per call and re-inject as needed (script persists across evaluations on same page).\n\nActually wait — will the webview stay alive across multiple evaluate calls? Earlier it crashed after reloads, but stable during a session of evaluates (I did many sequential evaluates successfully earlier). So: navigate once (fresh load), then run ~8 evaluates. Feasible.\n\nLet me also render ch1 at higher DPI for better OCR. I have 110 DPI PNGs. For OCR, 220 DPI would help. I can render with pdftoppm at 220 into a temp OCR folder (ch1 only, 22 pages, quick). Let me do that — pdftoppm exists.\n\nPlan:\n1. Render ch1 slides at 220 DPI → .freebuff/ocr-ch1/\n2. Load tesseract.js in the preview\n3. OCR slides 1-7 + 22, collect text\n4. Also OCR 8-10 (poll slides) to be thorough — poll content already known from extraction, but the slide titles might add flavor. Skip unless needed.\n5. Write native content data for ch1 in data.js\n6. Update lesson player to render native cards for ch1\n7. Verify + review\n\nLet me kick off the high-DPI render first.","open":false,"collapse":"preview"},{"kind":"text","text":"The UB-Mannheim installer needs admin elevation (exit 126 = blocked). Rather than touch your system, I'll use a smarter path: **run OCR in the browser** with tesseract.js from a CDN — zero installs. First, render Chapter 1 at high DPI for clean OCR:"},{"kind":"tool","id":"13h98UFTCQw","toolName":"run_terminal_command","input":{"command":"PPM=\"/c/Users/user/Downloads/REALITY FX TRADING/reality-fx-site/.freebuff/tools/poppler-26.02.0/Library/bin/pdftoppm.exe\"; mkdir -p .freebuff/ocr-ch1; cd \"/c/Users/user/Downloads/Reality Forex Trading Full course pdfs\" && \"$PPM\" -png -r 220 \"CHAPTER 1 The Forex Market.pdf\"