th slide 10's poll — acceptable, or I note it).\n\nLet me now write the native content for ch1. I'll write high-quality, faithful reconstructions of the OCR'd text, cleaned up and formatted for the premium theme, PLUS weave in QC-grade clarity. This is where I add value — the OCR gives me the raw content, and I present it as beautiful, accurate, premium cards.\n\nNative cards for Chapter 1:\n\n**Slide 1 — Chapter opener:**\n- eyebrow: CHAPTER 1 · INTRODUCTION\n- title: The Forex Market\n- lead: \"Focal points in this chapter\"\n- body: [ \"You're about to learn the language and the players of the world's largest financial market. By the end of this chapter you'll know what the forex market is, who trades in it, and which style of trader you want to become.\" ]\n- callout: the philosophy quote? The course doesn't put the quote here — the OS does. I'll put: \"Every lesson is a trade. Every trade is a lesson.\" as the chapter motto card. Good.\n\n**Slide 2 — The FX market:**\n- title: The Foreign Exchange Market\n- body: [\n  \"The foreign exchange market is a global, decentralized market for the trading of currencies. This market determines foreign exchange rates for every currency.\",\n  \"It includes all aspects of buying, selling and exchanging currencies at current or determined prices.\"\n  ]\n- bullets: [\n  \"In forex, currencies are traded in pairs. Each pair has a base currency and a quote currency.\",\n  \"When you exchange currencies, you are essentially buying one currency and selling another.\"\n  ]\n- example box: \"If you believe the euro will rise against the dollar, you buy EUR/USD — buying euros and selling an equivalent amount of US dollars. If the rate rises as you expect, you sell the euros back for a profit.\"\n\n**Slide 3 — Shorting + aspects of trading:**\n- title: Both Sides of the Market\n- body: [ \"If you believe the euro will FALL against the dollar, you sell EUR/USD — selling euros and buying dollars. If the rate falls as expected, you buy back the euros at a lower price, profiting on the transaction.\" ]\n- bullets: [ \"The forex market allows participants — banks and individuals — to buy, sell or exchange currencies for two purposes:\" , \"Hedging: minimizing risk\", \"Speculation: profiting from price change\" ]\n\n**Slide 4 — The players: Noise traders & Market timers:**\n- title: The Traders You'll Meet\n- Two blocks: \n  - \"Noise trading — Typically non-professional individuals who act illogically, using incomplete or inaccurate data to make trades. As the name suggests, they may execute trades off rumours. As trading has become more popular and easier, their numbers have spiked exponentially.\"\n  - \"Market timers — A strategy of buying or selling based on predictive methods that seek to forecast the future price movement of an asset. Common methods: fundamental data, technical data, economic data.\"\n\n**Slide 5 — Institutional vs Retail + Scalping:**\n- title: Institutional vs Retail\n- body:\n  - \"Institutional traders — Buy and sell securities for accounts they manage for a group or institution, as well as exchange-traded funds (ETFs).\"\n  - \"Retail traders — Also called individual traders: they trade with their own equity, funded from personal wealth rather than on behalf of an institution.\"\n- lead: \"Types of equity traders\"\n- bullets: Scalping — a style that specializes in profiting from small price changes for fast profit; a heightened-pace style that opens multiple positions.\n\n**Slide 6 — Momentum, Technical, Fundamental, Swing:**\n- title: More Styles\n- bullets/blocks: Momentum trading (stocks moving significantly in one direction on high volume — ride the momentum), Technical traders (charts and graphs; convergence/divergence signals → buy/sell), Fundamental trading (corporate events: earnings reports, stock splits, reorganizations, acquisitions), Swing trading (hold positions longer than a day)\n\n**Slide 7 — Day & Position traders + the fundamentalist truth:**\n- title: Day Traders & Position Traders\n- body: Day traders (pick a side at the open, act on bias, close by day's end — never hold overnight), Position traders (trades lasting weeks, months or years; fundamental themes dominate their analysis)\n- callout: \"Most fundamentalists are really swing traders — changes in corporate fundamentals take days or weeks to produce a price move big enough for a reasonable profit.\"\n\n**Slide 8 — Interactive poll: What kind of trader do you want to be?**\n- kind: poll, options: Technical Trader, Fundamental Trader, Swing Trader, Scalper\n- stores choice in state; dashboard shows it.\n\n**Slide 9 — Identity card:** \"Your style isn't permanent. As you experience the market, your edge will reveal itself. Keep an open mind through this course.\"\n\n**Slide 10 — Reflection card:** \"Mid-chapter check-in — How do you feel you've performed so far? Honest self-assessment is the first discipline of a trader.\" (matches their original poll)\n\n**Slide 22 — Close:** \"End of Chapter 1 — You now know the market, its players, and the styles. Time to prove it: pass the quiz to unlock Chapter 2.\"\n\nThat's a faithful + premium rebuild. \n\nNow the engine changes in os.js:\n1. In drawSlide: if `ch.native && ch.native[n-1]`, render native card (with the interactive poll for kind==='poll'). Else image.\n2. Handle poll kind: render options; on click, save to state (S.traderStyle) + toast; mark answered so next works (reuse session.answered[n]).\n3. Dashboard: show trader style if chosen (\"Your style: Swing Trader\") — small addition to hero or stats area. Let me add it to the rank-card or a small chip in the hero. Simple: add a line under the quote or in the name-box area: \"Trader style: X\". I'll add it as a chip in the dash-hero after the CTA if set. Keep minimal: add to stats? No — add a small \"style chip\" next to the eyebrow. Actually simplest: append to the name-box label area: \"Your trader style: Swing Trader\". Hmm, name-box is at the botto