ge-shade\"></div>\n        <img src=\"${slidePath(ch.id, n)}\" alt=\"Chapter ${ch.id} slide ${n}\" class=\"slide-img\" draggable=\"false\" oncontextmenu=\"return false;\">\n        <div class=\"stage-wm\">REALITY FX OS</div>\n        <div class=\"stage-count\">${fmt(n)} / ${ch.slides}</div>\n      </div>\n      ${quizQ ? quizCard(quizQ) : \"\"}\n      <div class=\"stage-nav\">\n        <button class=\"btn-ghost\" id=\"prev\" ${n === 1 ? \"disabled\" : \"\"}>← Back</button>\n        <button class=\"btn-gold\" id=\"next\">${n >= ch.slides ? \"Finish chapter\" : \"Next slide →\"}</button>\n      </div>`;","newString":"    const isQuizSlide = ch.quiz && ch.quizSlides.includes(n);\n    const quizQ = isQuizSlide ? ch.quiz[ch.quizSlides.indexOf(n)] : null;\n    const native = ch.native ? ch.native[n - 1] : null;\n    const isPollSlide = native && native.kind === \"poll\";\n\n    const frameHtml = native\n      ? nativeCard(native)\n      : `<div class=\"stage-frame\">\n          <div class=\"stage-shade\"></div>\n          <img src=\"${slidePath(ch.id, n)}\" alt=\"Chapter ${ch.id} slide ${n}\" class=\"slide-img\" draggable=\"false\" oncontextmenu=\"return false;\">\n          <div class=\"stage-wm\">REALITY FX OS</div>\n          <div class=\"stage-count\">${fmt(n)} / ${ch.slides}</div>\n        </div>`;\n\n    stage.innerHTML = `\n      ${frameHtml}\n      ${quizQ ? quizCard(quizQ) : \"\"}\n      <div class=\"stage-nav\">\n        <button class=\"btn-ghost\" id=\"prev\" ${n === 1 ? \"disabled\" : \"\"}>← Back</button>\n        <button class=\"btn-gold\" id=\"next\">${n >= ch.slides ? \"Finish chapter\" : \"Next slide →\"}</button>\n      </div>`;","allowMultiple":false},{"oldString":"    document.getElementById(\"next\").addEventListener(\"click\", () => {\n      if (isQuizSlide && ch.quiz) {\n        // must answer the question before moving on\n        if (session.answered[n] === undefined) { toast(\"Answer the question to continue\", \"warn\"); return; }\n      }","newString":"    document.getElementById(\"next\").addEventListener(\"click\", () => {\n      if ((isQuizSlide && ch.quiz) || isPollSlide) {\n        // must answer before moving on\n        if (session.answered[n] === undefined) { toast(\"Answer to continue\", \"warn\"); return; }\n      }","allowMultiple":false}]}},{"kind":"text","text":"\nNow the `nativeCard` renderer and the poll handler plus the dashboard style chip:"},{"kind":"tool","id":"130pKDBwQLI","toolName":"str_replace","input":{"path":"REALITY-FOREX-TRADING-/os/js/os.js","replacements":[{"oldString":"  function quizCard(q) {","newString":"  function nativeCard(native) {\n    const n = session.idx + 1;\n    const chosen = session.answered[n];\n    let extra = \"\";\n    if (native.kind === \"poll\") {\n      extra = `<div class=\"native-poll\">\n        ${native.options.map((o, i) =>\n          `<button class=\"native-poll-opt ${chosen === i ? \"sel\" : \"\"}\" data-i=\"${i}\" ${chosen !== undefined ? \"disabled\" : \"\"}>${esc(o)}</button>`\n        ).join(\"\")}\n        ${chosen !== undefined ? `<div class=\"quiz-fb good\">Identity saved — you can change it any time.</div>` : \"\"}\n      </div>`;\n    }\n    const isClose = native.kind === \"close\";\n    return `\n      <div class=\"native-card ${isClose ? \"native-close\" : \"\"}\">\n        ${isClose ? `<div class=\"native-close-ic\">${ICONS.trophy}</div>` : \"\"}\n        ${native.eyebrow ? `<p class=\"quiz-tag\">${esc(native.eyebrow)}</p>` : \"\"}\n        ${native.title ? `<h2 class=\"native-title gold-serif\">${esc(native.title)}</h2>` : \"\"}\n        ${native.lead ? `<p class=\"native-lead\">${esc(native.lead)}</p>` : \"\"}\n        ${(native.body || []).map(p => `<p class=\"native-body\">${esc(p)}</p>`).join(\"\")}\n        ${(native.bullets || []).map(b => `<div class=\"native-bullet\"><span class=\"nb-dot\"></span><p>${esc(b)}</p></div>`).join(\"\")}\n        ${native.example ? `<div class=\"native-example\">${esc(native.example)}</div>` : \"\"}\n        ${native.callout ? `<div class=\"native-callout\">“${esc(native.callout)}”</div>` : \"\"}\n        ${extra}\n      </div>`;\n  }\n\n  function quizCard(q) {","allowMultiple":false},{"oldString":"  // delegate quiz option clicks (stage is re-rendered each slide, so bind here)\n  document.addEventListener(\"click\", e => {\n    const opt = e.target.closest(\".q-opt\");","newString":"  // delegate native poll option clicks (trader identity)\n  document.addEventListener(\"click\", e => {\n    const opt = e.target.closest(\".native-poll-opt\");\n    if (!opt || opt.disabled || !session.ch) return;\n    const n = session.idx + 1;\n    if (session.answered[n] !== undefined) return;\n    session.answered[n] = parseInt(opt.dataset.i, 10);\n    S.traderStyle = session.ch.native[n - 1].options[session.answered[n]];\n    touch(); save();\n    toast(\"Identity saved — this can evolve\", \"rank\");\n    drawSlide(document.querySelector(\".stage\"));\n  });\n\n  // delegate quiz option clicks (stage is re-rendered each slide, so bind here)\n  document.addEventListener(\"click\", e => {\n    const opt = e.target.closest(\".q-opt\");","allowMultiple":false},{"oldString":"          <div class=\"dash-cta\">\n            ${cont ? `<button class=\"btn-gold\" data-go=\"${cont.id}\">Continue — ${esc(cont.title)}</button>` : `<button class=\"btn-gold\" data-go=\"cert\">Claim your certificate</button>`}\n          </div>\n        </div>","newString":"          <div class=\"dash-cta\">\n            ${cont ? `<button class=\"btn-gold\" data-go=\"${cont.id}\">Continue — ${esc(cont.title)}</button>` : `<button class=\"btn-gold\" data-go=\"cert\">Claim your certificate</button>`}\n          </div>\n          ${S.traderStyle ? `<div class=\"style-chip\">${ICONS.check} Trader style: <b>${esc(S.traderStyle)}</b></div>` : \"\"}\n        </div>","allowMultiple":false}]}},{"kind":"text","text":"\nNow the native card styles:"},{"kind":"tool","id":"131WNb9xN6U","toolName":"str_replace","input":{"path":"REALITY-FOREX-TRADING-/os/css/os.css","replacements":[{"oldString":"/* Quiz */","newString":"/* Native lesson cards */\n.native-card {\n  background: linear-gradient(165deg, #16130c 0%, #101010 55%, #0c0b08 100%);\n  border: 1px solid var(--border-subtle);\n  border-radius: 16px; padding: 40px 44px;\n  box-shadow: 0 22px 55px rgba(0,0,0,.5);\n  position: relative; overflow: hidden;\n}\n.native-card::before {\n  content: \"\"; position: absolute; width: 340px; height: 340px; border-radius: 50%;\n  right: -140px; top: -160px;\n  background: radial-gradient(circle, rgba(201,162,39,.14), transparent 65%);\n  pointer-events: none;\n}\n.native-title { font-size: clamp(24px, 3vw, 32px); margin-bottom: 14px; }\n.native-lead { font-size: 12px; letter-spacing: 2.5px; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--accent-gold); font-weight: 600; margin-bottom: 14px; }\n.native-body { font-size: 15.5px; line-height: 1.75; color: var(--text-body); margin-bottom: 14px; max-width: 62ch; }\n.native-body:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }\n.native-bullet { display: flex; gap: 12px; align-items: flex-start; margin-bottom: 10px; }\n.native-bullet p { font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65; color: var(--text-body); max-width: 60ch; }\n.nb-dot { width: 7px; height: 7px; border-radius: 50%; background: var(--grad-gold); margin-top: 9px; flex-shrink: 0; box-shadow: 0 0 8px rgba(201,162,39,.6); }\n.native-example {\n  margin-top: 18px; padding: 16px 20px; border-radius: 10px;\n  border: 1px solid var(--border-subtle); background: rgba(201,162,39,.06);\n  font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.7; color: var(--accent-gold-light); max-width: 64ch;\n}\n.native-callout {\n  margin-top: 20px; padding: 18px 22px; text-align: center;\n  font-family: var(--font-quote); font-style: italic; font-size: 19px;\n  color: var(--accent-gold-light);\n  border-top: 1px solid var(--glass-border); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--glass-border);\n}\n.native-poll { margin-top: 20px; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 10px; max-width: 460px; }\n.native-poll-opt {\n  background: #171717; border: 1px solid var(--glass-border); color: var(--text-body);\n  padding: 14px 18px; border-radius: 10px; cursor: pointer; text-align: left;\n  font-family: var(--font-body); font-size: 14.5px; transition: var(--transition);\n}\n.native-poll-opt:hover:not(:disabled) { border-color: var(--accent-gold); background: var(--accent-gold-dim); transform: translateX(4px); }\n.native-poll-opt.sel { border-color: var(--accent-gold); background: var(--accent-gold-dim); }\n.native-close { text-align: center; }\n.native-close .native-body { max-width: 46ch; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; }\n.native-close-ic { font-size: 40px; color: var(--accent-gold); margin-bottom: 14px; display: flex; justify-content: center; }\n.native-close-ic svg { width: 44px; height: 44px; }\n\n.style-chip {\n  display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px;\n  margin-top: 6px; padding: 8px 16px; border-radius: 30px;\n  border: 1px solid var(--border-subtle); background: rgba(201,162,39,.07);\n  font-size: 12.5px; color: var(--text-muted);\n}\n.style-chip svg { width: 14px; height: 14px; color: var(--accent-gold); }\n.style-chip b { color: var(--accent-gold-light); font-weight: 600; }\n\n/* Quiz */","allowMultiple":false}]}},{"kind":"text","text":"\n"},{"kind":"tool","id":"131vtgnvAWc","toolName":"run_terminal_command","input":{"command":"cd REALITY-FOREX-TRADING-/os && for f in js/os.js js/data.js; do awk '{o+=gsub(/{/,\"{\"); c+=gsub(/}/,\"}\")} END{print FILENAME\": open=\"o\" close=\"c}' $f; done","process_type":"SYNC","timeout_seconds":30}},{"kind":"reasoning","id":"p19-87976141","text":"Balanced. Now verify in the preview — full reload, enter Chapter 1 lesson, check native cards render (slide 1 opener), slide 8 poll, slide 22 close. The saved state has ch1 complete from earlier testing — entering lesson 1 will resume... all slides viewed, quiz passed → resume at slide 1 (idx 0). Good — I'll see the opener card.\n\nLet me navigate to the bare URL (fresh load of new JS), then to lesson 1.","open":false,"collapse":"preview"},{"kind":"text","text":"Balanced. Now the full verification — fresh load, then Chapter 1 lesson:"},{"kind":"tool","id":"132N-WDLzrQ","toolName":"preview_navigate","input":{"to":"http://127.0.0.1:8910/os/index.html"}},{"kind":"tool","id":"132oq6xsosg","toolName":"preview_evaluate","input":{"expression":"(async () => {\n  const wait = ms => new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, ms));\n  await wait(500);\n  location.hash = '#/lesson/1';\n  await wait(250);\n  const native = document.querySelector('.native-card');\n  const img = document.querySelector('.stage-frame');\n  return {\n    hasNative: !!native,\n    hasImage: !!img,\n    title: native ? native.querySelector('.native-title').textContent : null,\n    callout: native ? native.querySelector('.native-callout') ? native.querySelector('.native-callout').textContent : null : null,\n    count: document.querySelector('.stage-count') ? document.querySelector('.stage-count').textContent : 'no-count'\n  };\n})()"}},{"kind":"reasoning","id":"p20-87976141","text":"The native card renders for slide 1: title \"The Forex Market\", callout with the philosophy quote, no image frame (correct — native replaces image). Now let me test:\n1. Slide 8 poll (jump to #/lesson/1/8)\n2. Slide 22 close card (jump to #/lesson/1/22)\n3. The dashboard style chip after answering the poll\n\nLet me test slide 8 poll.","open":false,"collapse":"preview"},{"kind":"text","text":"Native opener card renders with the philosophy callout. Testing the trader-identity poll on slide 8:"},{"kind":"tool","id":"133KpapgDUk","toolName":"preview_evaluate","input":{"expression":"(async () => {\n  const wait = ms => new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, ms));\n  location.hash = '#/lesson/1/8';\n  await wait(250);\n  const pollOpts = [...document.querySelectorAll('.native-poll-opt')].map(o => o.textContent.trim());\n  const title = document.querySelector('.native-title') ? document.querySelector('.native-title').textContent : null;\n  if (pollOpts.length) {\n    pollOpts.length === 4 && (document.querySelectorAll('.native-poll-opt')[2].click());\n    await wait(80);\n  }\n  const st = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem('rfx_os_v1') || '{}');\n  return { title, pollOpts, traderStyle: st.traderStyle || null };\n})()"}},{"kind":"reasoning","id":"p21-87976141","text":"The poll works: 4 options from the original course, clicking \"Swing Trader\" saved `traderStyle: \"Swing Trader\"` to state. Now test:\n1. Slide 22 close card\n2. Dashboard style chip\n\nLet me test both.","open":false,"collapse":"preview"},{"kind":"text","text":"Poll works — identity saved (\"Swing Trader\"). Now the closing card and the dashboard style chip:"},{"kind":"tool","id":"133pjVGhj-0","toolName":"preview_evaluate","input":{"expression":"(async () => {\n  const wait = ms => new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, ms));\n  location.hash = '#/lesson/1/22';\n  await wait(200);\n  const close = document.querySelector('.native-close');\n  const closeTitle = close ? close.querySelector('.native-title').textContent : null;\n  const hasCloseIcon = close ? !!close.querySelector('.native-close-ic svg') : false;\n  location.hash = '#/dashboard';\n  await wait(200);\n  const chip = document.querySelector('.style-chip');\n  return { closeTitle, hasCloseIcon, chip: chip ? chip.textContent.replace(/\\s+/g, ' ').trim() : null };\n})()"}},{"kind":"reasoning","id":"p22-87976141","text":"Everything works:\n- Slide 22 close card: \"You Know the Market\" with gold trophy SVG icon\n- Dashboard: \"Trader style: Swing Trader\" chip shows after the poll answer\n\nLet me take a screenshot of a native card to see how it looks visually, then run the review pass, clean up the OCR temp assets, and summarize.","open":false,"collapse":"preview"},{"kind":"text","text":"Everything works. Let me see the native card visually:"},{"kind":"tool","id":"134EMRgdtPU","toolName":"preview_evaluate","input":{"expression":"(() => { location.hash = '#/lesson/1/2'; return 'nav'; })()"}},{"kind":"tool","id":"134YWVuUCw8","toolName":"preview_screenshot","input":{}},{"kind":"reasoning","id":"p23-87976141","text":"The native card renders beautifully — slide 2 \"The Foreign Exchange Market\" with:\n- Body paragraphs in clean white text\n- Gold dot bullets\n- The gold-bordered example box (EUR/USD example)\n- Back/Next buttons\n\nThis is exactly the premium gold/dark theme the user wanted — no more Nearpod branding.\n\nNow let me:\n1. Clean up the OCR temp assets (os/assets/ocr-hi/) — they were temporary for OCR. Actually, they could be useful if the user wants to re-OCR other chapters later... but they're 220 DPI duplicates (~bigger). Let me keep .freebuff/ocr-ch1 for reference but remove the served copy? Actually — the served copy could be useful for future OCR of other chapters via the browser. But 22 images at 220 DPI is ~10-20MB extra in the site folder. For cleanliness, I'll remove the served ocr-hi folder (the OCR work is done; if we OCR ch2 next, I'll re-copy). Hmm — but keeping it means the next OCR session doesn't need re-rendering... but the tool renders are in .freebuff/ocr-ch1 anyway (per-chapter). Let me remove the served copy to keep the deployable site clean, keep .freebuff/ocr-ch1.\n\nWait — actually, should I keep ocr-hi/ch1 served? The site folder gets deployed; extra 220 DPI images of ch1 slides aren't needed (ch1 now native). Remove it.\n\n2. Run the code review pass.\n3. Update todos + summarize.\n\nLet me clean up and run the review in parallel-ish (cleanup command first, then review agent).","open":false,"collapse":"preview"},{"kind":"text","text":"The native card looks stunning — clean white text, gold-dot bullets, the gold EUR/USD example box, zero Nearpod branding. Let me clean up the temporary OCR assets and run the review:"},{"kind":"tool","id":"135bTkDyyu8","toolName":"run_terminal_command","input":{"command":"rm -rf \"REALITY-FOREX-TRADING-/os/assets/ocr-hi\" && echo \"cleaned served ocr temp\"; du -sh \"REALITY-FOREX-TRADING-/os/assets/slides\" .freebuff/ocr-ch1 2>/dev/null","process_type":"SYNC","timeout_seconds":30}},{"kind":"agent","id":"136HyJfcOQA","agentType":"code-reviewer-deepseek-flash","displayName":"Nit Pick Nick","prompt":"Review the native lesson-card feature added to the Reality FX OS app (REALITY-FOREX-TRADING-/os/): (1) data.js — Chapter 1 now has a 'native' array of 22 entries (slides 1-10 as content/poll cards, 11 nulls for quiz slides 11-21, a 'close' card at index 21 for slide 22); (2) os.js drawSlide now renders nativeCard() when ch.native[n-1] exists, else the slide image; the next-button guard now also requires answers on kind:'poll' slides; a delegated .native-poll-opt click handler stores S.traderStyle and marks session.answered[n]; the dashboard shows a style-chip when S.traderStyle is set; (3) os.css native-card/native-poll/style-chip styles. Check for: array-index alignment between native entries and slide numbers (does slide N map to native[N-1] correctly for all 22?); the poll re-render loop (clicking a poll opt re-renders via drawSlide — does the delegated handler's session.idx stay correct?); the finish flow still working when native cards are shown on slide 22 (close card) with the quiz guard; default-state missing traderStyle field; any rendering issue with null native entries; XSS (all native strings escaped?). Report only concrete defects.","status":"done","blocks":[{"kind":"reasoning","id":"p24-87976141","text":"Let me review the native lesson-card feature carefully by thinking through the code.\n\nKey areas to check:\n\n**1. Array-index alignment (native[N-1] ↔ slide N):**\nThe native array has 22 entries: indices 0-9 (slides 1-10), indices 10-20 (11 nulls, slides 11-21), index 21 (close card, slide 22). `native[n-1]` for n=22 → native[21] → close card ✓. For n=11 → native[10] → null → image ✓. Correct alignment. But wait — I should double-check the actual edit: the original array ended with the close card; I replaced that block with \"null × 11 + close card\". So the array is [10 cards, 11 nulls, close] = 22 entries. Correct.\n\nOne subtle thing: the `native` array has `null` entries — does anything call methods on native without a null check? In drawSlide: `const native = ch.native ? ch.native[n - 1] : null;` then `const isPollSlide = native && native.kind === \"poll\";` then `const frameHtml = native ? nativeCard(native) : ...image...`. Null-safe ✓.\n\n**2. Poll re-render loop:**\nThe delegated handler: `const n = session.idx + 1;` — at click time, session.idx is the current slide (0-based). It sets session.answered[n] and calls drawSlide(stage) which re-renders with the poll options disabled + feedback. The re-render calls drawSlide which reads session.idx (unchanged) → same slide n. Correct. No stale idx issue.\n\nBut there's a subtle issue: the delegated handler reads `session.ch.native[n - 1].options[...]` — if a poll-opt is somehow clicked when native is null... but poll-opts only render when native.kind === 'poll', so native[n-1] is the poll object. Safe.\n\n**3. Finish flow with native close card on slide 22:**\nSlide 22 is NOT a quiz slide (quizSlides = 12-21), so isQuizSlide false. isPollSlide false (close kind). Next button → n >= ch.slides (22 >= 22) → finishChapter. ✓ The finish card shows score + poll + claim. Works.\n\nBut wait — one thing: the next-button guard change: `if ((isQuizSlide && ch.quiz) || isPollSlide)`. On slide 22 (close), neither → next → finishChapter directly. Good.\n\n**4. Default state missing traderStyle:**\n`defaultState()` returns `{ name: \"\", xp: 0, streak: 0, lastActive: \"\", chapters: {} }` — no `traderStyle` field. The dashboard checks `S.traderStyle ? ... : \"\"` — undefined is falsy → no chip. Safe. But there's a subtle issue: `load()` does `Object.assign(defaultState(), parsed)` — if a user's stored state has chapters but no traderStyle, S.traderStyle is undefined → falsy → fine. No bug, but adding traderStyle to defaultState would be cleaner for consistency (minor).\n\n**5. XSS:**\nnativeCard uses esc() on eyebrow, title, lead, body[], bullets[], example, callout, options. All escaped ✓. The `isClose` trophy icon is ICONS.trophy (fixed SVG) ✓.\n\n**6. Poll slide \"must answer before next\" — does the quiz slide logic interact?**\nPoll slides are slides 8 (not in quizSlides). isQuizSlide false for slide 8. isPollSlide true → guard requires session.answered[8]. ✓. But — the ORIGINAL quiz guard was `if (isQuizSlide && ch.quiz)`. For chapters with quiz but where a slide is BOTH a quiz slide AND... no overlap.\n\n**7. Potential issue — the `session.answered[n]` for poll vs quiz reuse:**\nsession.answered keys are slide numbers; no collision.\n\n**8. The resume logic + poll:** If a student views slide 8, doesn't answer the poll, and navigates away and back — resume at first unviewed slide. If slide 8 was viewed but unanswered, the resume goes to the next unviewed slide (9+), skipping the unanswered poll. That's fine (poll is optional identity).\n\nBut here's a REAL issue: the chapter-complete logic. `isComplete(ch)` for ch1 = allViewed && passed. Slide 8 poll doesn't block completion (not required). Fine.\n\n**9. Real potential defect — `drawSlide` re-renders and the poll's `.quiz-fb` good message:**\nOn re-render after poll answer, the options show `sel` class + disabled + feedback. ✓\n\n**10. XP double-award on poll:** Poll answers don't award XP — fine.\n\n**11. `nativeCard` uses `session.idx + 1` to read session.answered — but nativeCard is called from drawSlide with session.idx already correct. ✓\n\n**12. One more: in drawSlide, the keyboard handler and nav still reference `#prev`/`#next` — present in all renders. ✓\n\n**13. The `close` card has no callout/example — fine.\n\n**14. Possible issue — `finishChapter` for ch1 references `ch.quiz` (present) — the finish flow for quiz chapters requires passing the quiz. But the student might finish slides WITHOUT passing (retake). The finish card's \"Back to the quiz\" sets `session.idx = ch.quizSlides[0] - 1` = 11 → slide 12 → quiz restart. With native cards, slides 1-10 are native, quiz at 12-21, close at 22. Fine.\n\n**15. Styling dead code:** `.native-lead` used only in slide 5 (lead field). Fine.\n\n**16. Data content check — slide 22 close card references \"pass the quiz to unlock Chapter 2\" — accurate.\n\n**17. One genuine concern: `S.traderStyle` persists but if user changes identity (re-answer), the delegated handler overwrites S.traderStyle — but session.answered[n] !== undefined blocks re-answer on the same visit. To change identity, the student would need to re-enter the chapter... actually the poll option is disabled after answering (`${chosen !== undefined ? \"disabled\" : \"\"}`) so they can't re-answer on the same slide visit. Re-entering the chapter resets session.answered → poll is answerable again. OK, acceptable (\"you can change it any time\").\n\n**18. Minor: `defaultState()` missing `traderStyle: null` — cosmetic; also `Object.assign` means older saved states without the field are fine.\n\n**19. The `esc()` for the close card body — yes escaped.\n\n**20. Check: does any code do `ch.native[n - 1]` with ch.native being null for other chapters? drawSlide: `const native = ch.native ? ch.native[n - 1] : null;` — guarded for chapters without native ✓.\n\n**21. Edge: `nativeCard` for a slide where native is an empty object `{}` (no fields) — renders empty card. Not the case here.\n\n**22. Real bug candidate — the guard change:**\n```js\nif ((isQuizSlide && ch.quiz) || isPollSlide) {\n  if (session.answered[n] === undefined) { toast(...); return; }\n}\n```\nFor quiz slides where quiz EXISTS — same as before ✓. For poll slides — requires answer ✓. But what about chapters where `ch.quiz` is null (3-13) and a slide is in quizSlides? Previously: `isQuizSlide && ch.quiz` → false → no guard → next works (slide image shown, no interactive quiz). Same now. ✓\n\n**23. XSS via `native.options` in the poll render — escaped ✓.\n\n**24. The `style-chip` uses ICONS.check — defined ✓.\n\n**25. One thing to verify: data.js syntax — the native array with `null, null...` — commas: the array is `[...cards, null, null,..., {close}]` — I wrote the nulls as `null, null, ...` followed by a comma before `{kind:\"close\"}`? Looking at my edit: \n```\n      null, null, null, null, null, null, null, null, null, null, null,\n      {\n        kind: \"close\",\n```\nYes — trailing comma after the last null, then the close object. Valid JS (trailing commas in arrays fine). The braces check passed (79/79) but that doesn't validate syntax fully. The preview worked (close card rendered), so data.js is syntactically valid.\n\nSo overall: the implementation looks solid. The only real (minor) feedback:\n1. `traderStyle` should be added to `defaultState()` for consistency (so saved state always has the key — avoids `S.traderStyle` being undefined; harmless but cleaner).\n2. Consider a null/undefined guard in the delegated poll handler for `session.ch.native[n-1]` — currently if a `.native-poll-opt` were ever rendered without session.ch, it'd throw. But poll opts only render from native cards, so session.ch is always set. Minor defensive.\n3. The \"you can change it any time\" message slightly conflicts with the disabled state on re-render — cosmetic, not a defect.\n4. Also — a genuine UX note: the poll on slide 8 awards no XP and isn't required, but the next-button guard REQUIRES answering it to proceed. That's intentional (mirrors quiz slides). Fine.\n\nLet me also double check one potential real bug: in `nativeCard`, the poll's `chosen === i` uses `session.answered[n]` which is a number index — but what if a quiz answer was recorded on the same slide number from a previous chapter session? session.answered is reset per renderLesson, so no.\n\nAlso — `drawSlide` marks the slide viewed + awards XP for poll slides too. Fine.\n\nOne more real check: **the finish flow when a student skips slides via hash navigation** — not related to native.\n\nActually, let me reconsider one thing the reviewer prompt asked: \"the finish flow still working when native cards are shown on slide 22 (close card) with the quiz guard\". The next button on slide 22: guard evaluates `(isQuizSlide && ch.quiz) || isPollSlide` — slide 22: isQuizSlide = ch.quiz && quizSlides.includes(22) = false; isPollSlide = false. So no guard → finishChapter. ✓ Confirmed by live testing earlier (the finish card appeared in prior tests with native content... actually those tests were before native existed. But the logic is sound.)\n\nWait — there's one thing I should double check about `finishChapter` and XP_CHAPTER: it uses `session.firstCorrect / total` for score. Native slides don't affect that. ✓\n\nAlso the reviewer asked about \"rendering issue with null native entries\" — confirmed handled via ternary.\n\nFinal review: implementation is correct. Minor suggestions:\n1. Add `traderStyle: null` to defaultState() for consistency.\n2. Defensive guard in the poll delegated handler: `if (!opt || opt.disabled || !session.ch || !session.ch.native) return;` — currently it checks `!session.ch` but if session.ch.native were null it would throw at `session.ch.native[n-1]`. In practice native exists whenever poll-opts render, but the guard is cheap.\n3. Cosmetic: the poll feedback says \"you can change it any time\" while options are disabled on re-render — slightly misleading; could say \"saved\".\n\nKeep it brief and focused on real defects. Most things check out; give the concise feedback.","open":false,"collapse":"preview"},{"kind":"text","text":"<think>Let me trace through the native lesson-card implementation carefully.\n\n**Array-index alignment:** The native array has 22 entries: indices 0–9 = slides 1–10, indices 10–20 = 11 `null`s (slides 11–21), index 21 = close card (slide 22). `drawSlide` uses `ch.native[n - 1]`, so slide 22 → native[21] → close ✓, slide 11 → native[10] → null → image fallback ✓. Alignment is correct. The `native ? nativeCard(native) : image` ternary is null-safe for chapters without native and for the null entries ✓.\n\n**Poll re-render loop:** Delegated handler reads `const n = session.idx + 1` at click time — session.idx is unchanged by the re-render (drawSlide re-renders the same slide), so `session.answered[n]` and `native[n-1].options[...]` stay correct. No stale-index bug.\n\n**Finish flow on slide 22:** isQuizSlide = false (22 ∉ quizSlides 12–21), isPollSlide = false (kind \"close\") → guard skipped → `n >= ch.slides` → finishChapter ✓. Guard change `(isQuizSlide && ch.quiz) || isPollSlide` preserves the old behavior for quiz chapters and correctly adds poll requirement.\n\n**XSS:** eyebrow/title/lead/body/bullets/example/callout/options all pass through `esc()` ✓. The close icon is a fixed SVG string ✓.\n\n**defaultState:** `{ name, xp, streak, lastActive, chapters }` — no `traderStyle` key. Dashboard checks `S.traderStyle ? ... : \"\"` so undefined is fine at runtime, but the field should be in defaultState for consistency with the rest of the schema (cosmetic, not a bug).\n\n**Delegated poll handler robustness:** `if (!opt || opt.disabled || !session.ch) return;` — then `session.ch.native[n - 1].options[...]`. If `session.ch.native` were null/undefined it would throw — but poll options only render from native cards, so `session.ch.native[n-1]` is guaranteed non-null whenever a `.native-poll-opt` exists. Defensive-only.\n\n**One real (minor) behavior note:** the poll feedback says \"Identity saved — you can change it any time,\" but the options are disabled on the re-render (`${chosen !== undefined ? \"disabled\" : \"\"}`), so the student cannot actually change it on the same slide visit — only by re-entering the chapter. Slightly misleading copy, cosmetic.\n\nNo dead code, no missing imports, no deleted sections. The feature is functionally correct and was verified live (opener card, poll saving traderStyle, close card, dashboard chip all confirmed).</think>\n\nThe native lesson-card feature is functionally correct — array alignment (native[N-1] ↔ slide N, 11 nulls for quiz slides, close card at slide 22), the poll re-render loop, the next-button guard, and the finish flow all check out, and XSS is handled via esc() everywhere.\n\nMinor feedback only:\n\n1. **Add `traderStyle: null` to `defaultState()`** in os.js for schema consistency — the other persisted fields are all declared there, and this one isn't. Harmless today (undefined is falsy), but a saved state without the key is only a `Object.assign` away from being inconsistent.\n\n2. **Defensive guard in the delegated poll handler:** `if (!opt || opt.disabled || !session.ch) return;` — consider `|| !session.ch.native` since the next line indexes `session.ch.native[n - 1].options`. It can't fire today (poll options only render from native cards), but it's a one-token hardening.\n\n3. **Poll feedback copy vs. disabled options:** \"Identity saved — you can change it any time\" appears alongside disabled options on the re-render, which reads as contradictory. Either say \"saved\" plainly, or leave the options enabled for immediate re-picking (and drop the `disabled` attribute)."}]},{"kind":"reasoning","id":"p25-87976141","text":"The review confirms the feature is correct with 3 minor suggestions. Let me apply them quickly:\n1. Add `traderStyle: null` to defaultState()\n2. Defensive guard in poll handler (`|| !session.ch.native`)\n3. Poll feedback copy — change to something accurate. I'll keep options disabled (avoids accidental clicks) but make the message honest: \"Identity saved — it will appear on your dashboard.\" Actually the reviewer's point: \"you can change it any time\" vs disabled. Let me change the copy to \"Saved — this identity now shows on your dashboard.\" That's accurate and clean.\n\nLet me apply all three, verify braces, and wrap up with the summary.","open":false,"collapse":"preview"},{"kind":"text","text":"All three minor fixes confirmed by the review. Applying them:"},{"kind":"tool","id":"139Nr_4kKkE","toolName":"str_replace","input":{"path":"REALITY-FOREX-TRADING-/os/js/os.js","replacements":[{"oldString":"  function defaultState() {\n    return { name: \"\", xp: 0, streak: 0, lastActive: \"\", chapters: {} };\n  }","newString":"  function defaultState() {\n    return { name: \"\", xp: 0, streak: 0, lastActive: \"\", traderStyle: null, chapters: {} };\n  }","allowMultiple":false},{"oldString":"    const opt = e.target.closest(\".native-poll-opt\");\n    if (!opt || opt.disabled || !session.ch) return;","newString":"    const opt = e.target.closest(\".native-poll-opt\");\n    if (!opt || opt.disabled || !session.ch || !session.ch.native) return;","allowMultiple":false},{"oldString":"        ${chosen !== undefined ? `<div class=\"quiz-fb good\">Identity saved — you can change it any time.</div>` : \"\"}","newString":"        ${chosen !== undefined ? `<div class=\"quiz-fb good\">Saved — this identity now shows on your dashboard.</div>` : \"\"}","allowMultiple":false}]}},{"kind":"text","text":"\n"},{"kind":"tool","id":"139qWEQg4WQ","toolName":"run_terminal_command","input":{"command":"cd REALITY-FOREX-TRADING-/os && awk '{o+=gsub(/{/,\"{\"); c+=gsub(/}/,\"}\")} END{print \"os.js: open=\"o\" close=\"c}' js/os.js && curl -s -m 5 -o /dev/null -w \"site check: %{http_code}\\n\" http://127.0.0.1:8910/os/index.html","process_type":"SYNC","timeout_seconds":30}},{"kind":"tool","id":"13-Fdq_oafw","toolName":"write_todos","input":{"todos":[{"task":"Download portable Tesseract OCR into the project tools folder","completed":true},{"task":"Render Chapter 1 slides at high DPI and OCR the content slides to recover their text","completed":true},{"task":"Rebuild Chapter 1 as native gold/dark lesson cards in the OS (replacing slide images for ch1)","completed":true},{"task":"Verify the native lesson renders correctly in the live preview","completed":true},{"task":"Code review pass on the native lesson engine","completed":true}]}},{"kind":"text","text":"**Chapter 1 is now a native Reality FX experience.** 🐂 The Nearpod era of that chapter is over — here's what just happened:\n\n## How it works (the smart way)\n\nYour slides are images, and I can't see images — so instead of installing anything on your system (the OCR installer wanted admin rights), I **ran OCR in the browser itself** using a CDN library. It read your Chapter 1 slides with excellent accuracy — I recovered the *actual teaching text*: the FX market definition, base vs quote currency, the EUR/USD worked example, noise traders, market timers, institutional vs retail, all 7 trader styles, and the fundamentalist-truth callout.\n\n## What Chapter 1 looks like now\n\nEvery content slide is a **custom gold/dark card** in the exact site theme:\n- Clean white body text, gold-dot bullets, a gold-bordered **example box** for the EUR/USD walkthrough\n- A **cursive gold callout** for the philosophy line (\"Every lesson is a trade...\")\n- **No Nearpod branding anywhere** — it's pure Reality FX\n- A bonus delight: the \"What kind of trader do you want to be?\" poll (your real question!) is now **interactive** — students pick their trader style and it appears on their dashboard (\"Trader style: Swing Trader\" — verified live)\n- The quiz slides stay interactive as before; the chapter closes with a trophy card\n\nA review pass confirmed the feature is clean — 3 tiny hardening fixes applied (schema consistency, a defensive guard, honest feedback copy).\n\n## The pipeline is proven\n\nThe full recipe works: **render high-DPI → OCR → rebuild as native cards**. Chapter 1 was the proof — and it looks *powerful*, as you said. The next chapter (Chapter 2, 49 slides) can follow the same pipeline whenever you're ready, and we keep going until all 13 chapters are native. Reload the Preview and walk Chapter 1 — you'll feel the difference immediately.\n\nWant me to roll the pipeline into Chapter 2 next, or would you rather verify Chapter 1's rebuilt content first (the OCR text is faithful, but you're the author — your eyes on it is the final QC)?"},{"kind":"tool","id":"13-42Pr0ytQ","toolName":"suggest_prompts","input":{"prompts":[{"prompt":"Roll the OCR → native rebuild pipeline into Chapter 2 (Fx Terminology & Concepts, 49 slides) so the whole course progressively sheds the Nearpod branding","label":"Rebuild Chapter 2 natively"},{"prompt":"I reviewed the rebuilt Chapter 1 cards — here are the corrections to the content wording, update the native data","label":"Review Chapter 1's native content"},{"prompt":"Build the Catalog Mode showcase next — free visitors see the full journey map with gold locks and a 'Purchase to unlock' CTA, plus a 3-slide free preview of Chapter 1","label":"Build the free-trial Catalog Mode"},{"prompt":"Run Quality Pass #2 on Trading Psychology (Chapter 6) — verify the quiz and add the missing psychology-of-risk concepts","label":"Quality Pass #2: Psychology"}]}},{"kind":"changes","id":"p26-87976141","files":[{"path":"REALITY-FOREX-TRADING-\\os\\css\\os.css","status":"modified","lockfile":false,"binary":false,"adds":54,"dels":0,"patch":"Index: REALITY-FOREX-TRADING-\\os\\css\\os.css\n===================================================================\n--- REALITY-FOREX-TRADING-\\os\\css\\os.css\tbefore\n+++ REALITY-FOREX-TRADING-\\os\\css\\os.css\tafter\n@@ -295,6 +295,60 @@\n \n .stage-nav { display: flex; justify-content: space-between; gap: 14px; margin-top: 20px; }\n \n+/* Native lesson cards */\n+.native-card {\n+  background: linear-gradient(165deg, #16130c 0%, #101010 55%, #0c0b08 100%);\n+  border: 1px solid var(--border-subtle);\n+  border-radius: 16px; padding: 40px 44px;\n+  box-shadow: 0 22px 55px rgba(0,0,0,.5);\n+  position: relative; overflow: hidden;\n+}\n+.native-card::before {\n+  content: \"\"; position: absolute; width: 340px; height: 340px; border-radius: 50%;\n+  right: -140px; top: -160px;\n+  background: radial-gradient(circle, rgba(201,162,39,.14), transparent 65%);\n+  pointer-events: none;\n+}\n+.native-title { font-size: clamp(24px, 3vw, 32px); margin-bottom: 14px; }\n+.native-lead { font-size: 12px; letter-spacing: 2.5px; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--accent-gold); font-weight: 600; margin-bottom: 14px; }\n+.native-body { font-size: 15.5px; line-height: 1.75; color: var(--text-body); margin-bottom: 14px; max-width: 62ch; }\n+.native-body:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }\n+.native-bullet { display: flex; gap: 12px; align-items: flex-start; margin-bottom: 10px; }\n+.native-bullet p { font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65; color: var(--text-body); max-width: 60ch; }\n+.nb-dot { width: 7px; height: 7px; border-radius: 50%; background: var(--grad-gold); margin-top: 9px; flex-shrink: 0; box-shadow: 0 0 8px rgba(201,162,39,.6); }\n+.native-example {\n+  margin-top: 18px; padding: 16px 20px; border-radius: 10px;\n+  border: 1px solid var(--border-subtle); background: rgba(201,162,39,.06);\n+  font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.7; color: var(--accent-gold-light); max-width: 64ch;\n+}\n+.native-callout {\n+  margin-top: 20px; padding: 18px 22px; text-align: center;\n+  font-family: var(--font-quote); font-style: italic; font-size: 19px;\n+  color: var(--accent-gold-light);\n+  border-top: 1px solid var(--glass-border); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--glass-border);\n+}\n+.native-poll { margin-top: 20px; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 10px; max-width: 460px; }\n+.native-poll-opt {\n+  background: #171717; border: 1px solid var(--glass-border); color: var(--text-body);\n+  padding: 14px 18px; border-radius: 10px; cursor: pointer; text-align: left;\n+  font-family: var(--font-body); font-size: 14.5px; transition: var(--transition);\n+}\n+.native-poll-opt:hover:not(:disabled) { border-color: var(--accent-gold); background: var(--accent-gold-dim); transform: translateX(4px); }\n+.native-poll-opt.sel { border-color: var(--accent-gold); background: var(--accent-gold-dim); }\n+.native-close { text-align: center; }\n+.native-close .native-body { max-width: 46ch; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; }\n+.native-close-ic { font-size: 40px; color: var(--accent-gold); margin-bottom: 14px; display: flex; justify-content: center; }\n+.native-close-ic svg { width: 44px; height: 44px; }\n+\n+.style-chip {\n+  display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px;\n+  margin-top: 6px; padding: 8px 16px; border-radius: 30px;\n+  border: 1px solid var(--border-subtle); background: rgba(201,162,39,.07);\n+  font-size: 12.5px; color: var(--text-muted);\n+}\n+.style-chip svg { width: 14px; height: 14px; color: var(--accent-gold); }\n+.style-chip b { color: var(--accent-gold-light); font-weight: 600; }\n+\n /* Quiz */\n .quiz-card {\n   margin-top: 22px; background: var(--bg-card);\n","tooLarge":false},{"path":"REALITY-FOREX-TRADING-\\os\\js\\data.js","status":"modified","lockfile":false,"binary":false,"adds":107,"dels":0,"patch":"Index: REALITY-FOREX-TRADING-\\os\\js\\data.js\n===================================================================\n--- REALITY-FOREX-TRADING-\\os\\js\\data.js\tbefore\n+++ REALITY-FOREX-TRADING-\\os\\js\\data.js\tafter\n@@ -67,6 +67,113 @@\n       { q: \"What resonates with scalping?\",\n         options: [\"Slow-paced trading\", \"Fast-paced trading\"], answer: 1,\n         explain: \"Scalping is the fastest style — seconds to minutes per trade.\" }\n+    ],\n+    native: [\n+      {\n+        eyebrow: \"Chapter 1 · Introduction\",\n+        title: \"The Forex Market\",\n+        lead: \"Focal points in this chapter\",\n+        body: [\n+          \"You are about to learn the language and the players of the world's largest financial market. By the end of this chapter you will know what the forex market is, who trades in it, and which style of trader you want to become.\"\n+        ],\n+        callout: \"Every lesson is a trade. Every trade is a lesson.\"\n+      },\n+      {\n+        eyebrow: \"The market\",\n+        title: \"The Foreign Exchange Market\",\n+        body: [\n+          \"The foreign exchange market is a global, decentralized market for the trading of currencies. This market determines foreign exchange rates for every currency.\",\n+          \"It includes all aspects of buying, selling and exchanging currencies at current or determined prices.\"\n+        ],\n+        bullets: [\n+          \"In forex, currencies are traded in pairs. Each pair has a base currency and a quote currency.\",\n+          \"When you exchange currencies, you are essentially buying one currency and selling another.\"\n+        ],\n+        example: \"If you believe the euro will rise against the US dollar, you buy EUR/USD — buying euros and selling an equivalent amount of dollars. If the rate rises as you expect, you sell the euros back for a profit.\"\n+      },\n+      {\n+        eyebrow: \"The market\",\n+        title: \"Both Sides of the Market\",\n+        body: [\n+          \"If you believe the euro will FALL against the dollar, you sell EUR/USD — selling euros and buying an equivalent amount of US dollars. If the rate falls as you expect, you buy the euros back at a lower price, profiting on the transaction.\"\n+        ],\n+        bullets: [\n+          \"The forex market allows participants — banks and individuals — to buy, sell or exchange currencies for two purposes:\",\n+          \"Hedging — buying and selling to minimize risk\",\n+          \"Speculation — profiting from price change\"\n+        ]\n+      },\n+      {\n+        eyebrow: \"The players\",\n+        title: \"The Traders You'll Meet\",\n+        body: [\n+          \"Noise trading — typically non-professional individuals who act illogically, using incomplete or inaccurate data to make trades. As the name suggests, they may execute trades off rumours. As trading has become more popular and easier, their numbers have spiked exponentially.\",\n+          \"Market timers — a strategy of buying or selling based on predictive methods that seek to forecast the future price movement of an underlying asset. Common methods include fundamental data, technical data, and economic data.\"\n+        ]\n+      },\n+      {\n+        eyebrow: \"The players\",\n+        title: \"Institutional vs Retail\",\n+        body: [\n+          \"Institutional traders buy and sell securities for the accounts they manage for a group or institution, as well as exchange-traded funds (ETFs).\",\n+          \"Retail traders — often called individual traders — trade with their own equity, funded from personal wealth rather than on behalf of an institution.\"\n+        ],\n+        lead: \"Types of equity traders\",\n+        bullets: [\n+          \"Scalping — a style that specializes in profiting off small price changes for a fast profit. It is a heightened-pace style in which the trader opens multiple positions.\"\n+        ]\n+      },\n+      {\n+        eyebrow: \"The styles\",\n+        title: \"Momentum, Technical, Fundamental, Swing\",\n+        bullets: [\n+          \"Momentum trading — traders seek stocks moving significantly in one direction on high volume (a trend) and ride that momentum to the desired profit.\",\n+          \"Technical traders — they focus on charts and graphs, analysing lines for signs of convergence or divergence that might indicate buy or sell signals.\",\n+          \"Fundamental trading — traders base decisions on corporate events, particularly actual or anticipated earnings reports, stock splits, reorganizations, or acquisitions.\",\n+          \"Swing trading — holding positions longer than a day.\"\n+        ]\n+      },\n+      {\n+        eyebrow: \"The styles\",\n+        title: \"Day Traders & Position Traders\",\n+        body: [\n+          \"Day traders pick a side at the beginning of the day, acting on their bias, and finish the day with either a profit or a loss. They never hold trades overnight.\",\n+          \"Position traders hold trades for weeks, months, or even years. They know fundamental themes will be the predominant factor when analysing the markets, and they base their decisions on them.\"\n+        ],\n+        callout: \"Most fundamentalists are really swing traders — changes in corporate fundamentals typically take days or even weeks to produce a price movement sufficient for a reasonable profit.\"\n+      },\n+      {\n+        kind: \"poll\",\n+        eyebrow: \"Your identity\",\n+        title: \"What kind of trader do you want to be?\",\n+        sub: \"Choose what resonates today. This is your starting identity — it will evolve with experience.\",\n+        options: [\"Technical Trader\", \"Fundamental Trader\", \"Swing Trader\", \"Scalper\"]\n+      },\n+      {\n+        eyebrow: \"Your identity\",\n+        title: \"Keep an Open Mind\",\n+        body: [\n+          \"Your style is not permanent. As you experience the market, your edge will reveal itself — and many great traders combine styles over time.\",\n+          \"For now, hold your choice lightly and stay curious through this course.\"\n+        ]\n+      },\n+      {\n+        eyebrow: \"Check-in\",\n+        title: \"Mid-Chapter Reflection\",\n+        body: [\n+          \"How do you feel you've performed so far?\",\n+          \"Honest self-assessment is the first discipline of a trader. Name how you feel — then keep going.\"\n+        ]\n+      },\n+      null, null, null, null, null, null, null, null, null, null, null,\n+      {\n+        kind: \"close\",\n+        eyebrow: \"Chapter complete\",\n+        title: \"You Know the Market\",\n+        body: [\n+          \"You now know the market, its players, and the trading styles. Time to prove it — pass the quiz to unlock Chapter 2.\"\n+        ]\n+      }\n     ]\n   },\n   {\n","tooLarge":false},{"path":"REALITY-FOREX-TRADING-\\os\\js\\os.js","status":"modified","lockfile":false,"binary":false,"adds":57,"dels":10,"patch":"Index: REALITY-FOREX-TRADING-\\os\\js\\os.js\n===================================================================\n--- REALITY-FOREX-TRADING-\\os\\js\\os.js\tbefore\n+++ REALITY-FOREX-TRADING-\\os\\js\\os.js\tafter\n@@ -11,7 +11,7 @@\n \n   /* ---------- State ---------- */\n   function defaultState() {\n-    return { name: \"\", xp: 0, streak: 0, lastActive: \"\", chapters: {} };\n+    return { name: \"\", xp: 0, streak: 0, lastActive: \"\", traderStyle: null, chapters: {} };\n   }\n   function load() {\n     try { return Object.assign(defaultState(), JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem(KEY) || \"{}\")); }\n@@ -161,6 +161,7 @@\n           <div class=\"dash-cta\">\n             ${cont ? `<button class=\"btn-gold\" data-go=\"${cont.id}\">Continue — ${esc(cont.title)}</button>` : `<button class=\"btn-gold\" data-go=\"cert\">Claim your certificate</button>`}\n           </div>\n+          ${S.traderStyle ? `<div class=\"style-chip\">${ICONS.check} Trader style: <b>${esc(S.traderStyle)}</b></div>` : \"\"}\n         </div>\n         <div class=\"ring-wrap\">\n           <svg class=\"ring\" viewBox=\"0 0 120 120\">\n@@ -319,14 +320,20 @@\n \n     const isQuizSlide = ch.quiz && ch.quizSlides.includes(n);\n     const quizQ = isQuizSlide ? ch.quiz[ch.quizSlides.indexOf(n)] : null;\n+    const native = ch.native ? ch.native[n - 1] : null;\n+    const isPollSlide = native && native.kind === \"poll\";\n \n+    const frameHtml = native\n+      ? nativeCard(native)\n+      : `<div class=\"stage-frame\">\n+          <div class=\"stage-shade\"></div>\n+          <img src=\"${slidePath(ch.id, n)}\" alt=\"Chapter ${ch.id} slide ${n}\" class=\"slide-img\" draggable=\"false\" oncontextmenu=\"return false;\">\n+          <div class=\"stage-wm\">REALITY FX OS</div>\n+          <div class=\"stage-count\">${fmt(n)} / ${ch.slides}</div>\n+        </div>`;\n+\n     stage.innerHTML = `\n-      <div class=\"stage-frame\">\n-        <div class=\"stage-shade\"></div>\n-        <img src=\"${slidePath(ch.id, n)}\" alt=\"Chapter ${ch.id} slide ${n}\" class=\"slide-img\" draggable=\"false\" oncontextmenu=\"return false;\">\n-        <div class=\"stage-wm\">REALITY FX OS</div>\n-        <div class=\"stage-count\">${fmt(n)} / ${ch.slides}</div>\n-      </div>\n+      ${frameHtml}\n       ${quizQ ? quizCard(quizQ) : \"\"}\n       <div class=\"stage-nav\">\n         <button class=\"btn-ghost\" id=\"prev\" ${n === 1 ? \"disabled\" : \"\"}>← Back</button>\n@@ -336,9 +343,9 @@\n     // allow prev even if quiz present\n     document.getElementById(\"prev\").addEventListener(\"click\", () => { session.idx = Math.max(0, session.idx - 1); drawSlide(stage); });\n     document.getElementById(\"next\").addEventListener(\"click\", () => {\n-      if (isQuizSlide && ch.quiz) {\n-        // must answer the question before moving on\n-        if (session.answered[n] === undefined) { toast(\"Answer the question to continue\", \"warn\"); return; }\n+      if ((isQuizSlide && ch.quiz) || isPollSlide) {\n+        // must answer before moving on\n+        if (session.answered[n] === undefined) { toast(\"Answer to continue\", \"warn\"); return; }\n       }\n       if (n >= ch.slides) finishChapter(stage);\n       else { session.idx++; drawSlide(stage); }\n@@ -350,6 +357,33 @@\n     };\n   }\n \n+  function nativeCard(native) {\n+    const n = session.idx + 1;\n+    const chosen = session.answered[n];\n+    let extra = \"\";\n+    if (native.kind === \"poll\") {\n+      extra = `<div class=\"native-poll\">\n+        ${native.options.map((o, i) =>\n+          `<button class=\"native-poll-opt ${chosen === i ? \"sel\" : \"\"}\" data-i=\"${i}\" ${chosen !== undefined ? \"disabled\" : \"\"}>${esc(o)}</button>`\n+        ).join(\"\")}\n+        ${chosen !== undefined ? `<div class=\"quiz-fb good\">Saved — this identity now shows on your dashboard.</div>` : \"\"}\n+      </div>`;\n+    }\n+    const isClose = native.kind === \"close\";\n+    return `\n+      <div class=\"native-card ${isClose ? \"native-close\" : \"\"}\">\n+        ${isClose ? `<div class=\"native-close-ic\">${ICONS.trophy}</div>` : \"\"}\n+        ${native.eyebrow ? `<p class=\"quiz-tag\">${esc(native.eyebrow)}</p>` : \"\"}\n+        ${native.title ? `<h2 class=\"native-title gold-serif\">${esc(native.title)}</h2>` : \"\"}\n+        ${native.lead ? `<p class=\"native-lead\">${esc(native.lead)}</p>` : \"\"}\n+        ${(native.body || []).map(p => `<p class=\"native-body\">${esc(p)}</p>`).join(\"\")}\n+        ${(native.bullets || []).map(b => `<div class=\"native-bullet\"><span class=\"nb-dot\"></span><p>${esc(b)}</p></div>`).join(\"\")}\n+        ${native.example ? `<div class=\"native-example\">${esc(native.example)}</div>` : \"\"}\n+        ${native.callout ? `<div class=\"native-callout\">“${esc(native.callout)}”</div>` : \"\"}\n+        ${extra}\n+      </div>`;\n+  }\n+\n   function quizCard(q) {\n     const n = session.idx + 1;\n     const chosen = session.answered[n];\n@@ -372,6 +406,19 @@\n       </div>`;\n   }\n \n+  // delegate native poll option clicks (trader identity)\n+  document.addEventListener(\"click\", e => {\n+    const opt = e.target.closest(\".native-poll-opt\");\n+    if (!opt || opt.disabled || !session.ch || !session.ch.native) return;\n+    const n = session.idx + 1;\n+    if (session.answered[n] !== undefined) return;\n+    session.answered[n] = parseInt(opt.dataset.i, 10);\n+    S.traderStyle = session.ch.native[n - 1].options[session.answered[n]];\n+    touch(); save();\n+    toast(\"Identity saved — this can evolve\", \"rank\");\n+    drawSlide(document.querySelector(\".stage\"));\n+  });\n+\n   // delegate quiz option clicks (stage is re-rendered each slide, so bind here)\n   document.addEventListener(\"click\", e => {\n     const opt = e.target.closest(\".q-opt\");\n","tooLarge":false}]}],"ts":1786026901054},{"role":"user","parts":[{"kind":"text","text":"When you ask how do they feel like they peformed in that chapter (hide their score momentarily to suprise them and after they click a box you reveal their score! sounds cool)\nI love the trader style badge you added it's so cool. Is there a way we can add a more smart system that tracks student data when they are responding to the quizzes (a automated process) im thinking something that is sowing them they're current grades, they're average thinking them per slide, they're average responsne time, and if a student is failing you could be providing insights for them gems) to help them get better and visa versa if they are passing to aid them with insight that will keep them peforming at a high level, showing that we are inclusive to both processes. \nLet me know...\nOverall im impresed i love the gold bulletins you're adding in every slide and your small insights on the bottom of the slides they are powerful, in fact we defineteley need more of that in the other chapters for sure"}],"ts":1786027625789},{"role":"assistant","parts":[{"kind":"reasoning","id":"p1-d28334da","text":"The user wants several things:\n\n1. **Score surprise mechanic**: When the end-of-chapter self-assessment poll asks \"How do you feel you performed?\", hide their actual score momentarily — after they click a box (their self-assessment), REVEAL their real score. Sounds cool — let's build it.\n\n2. **Smart student analytics system**: An automated system that tracks student data while they respond to quizzes:\n   - Current grades\n   - Average per slide\n   - Average response time\n   - If failing → provide insights/gems to help them improve\n   - If passing → provide insights to keep them performing at a high level\n   - Inclusive to both processes\n\n3. **More gold bullets + insights on slides** — they love the gold callouts and small insights at the bottom of slides; want more in other chapters. (This is the QC/enrichment continuation — mostly relevant when rebuilding chapters. I can acknowledge and note we'll do this as each chapter gets rebuilt. But I could also add \"insight\" fields to more slides... the native content is only ch1 right now. The plan: as we rebuild chapters 2-13, weave in insights. I'll confirm this.)\n\nLet me focus on building #1 and #2 now — they're concrete OS features.\n\n**Feature 1: Score reveal mechanic**\n\nCurrent finishChapter flow for quiz chapters:\n```js\nconst score = Math.round(session.firstCorrect / total * 100);\nconst passed = score >= PASS_PCT;\n...\nhtml += pollBlock(st);\nhtml += passed ? `<button ...>Claim XP & continue</button>` : `<button ... data-n=\"0\">Back to the quiz</button>`;\n```\n\nThe user wants: the score display (currently shown in .finish-score) should be HIDDEN until the student answers the self-assessment poll. After they click a poll box → reveal their actual score (with animation — surprise!).\n\nRedesign the finish card for quiz chapters:\n- Show \"Chapter complete — how do you feel you performed?\" poll FIRST\n- Score hidden: show a \"?\" or a locked/mystery element (\"Your score is hidden — reflect first, then reveal\")\n- After clicking a poll option → reveal score with a nice animation (flip/reveal), show pass/fail, then the Claim/Retake button appears.\n\nAlso compare their self-assessment vs actual score — that's a POWERFUL insight: \"You rated yourself Good (60-79%) — your actual score was 40%.\" Calibration awareness is a real trader skill (self-awareness). That fits the philosophy perfectly! Their POLL_OPTIONS have pct ranges. I can compare the selected poll range to the actual score and give a calibration insight: \"Your self-assessment and reality are aligned\" / \"You're being harder on yourself than reality (imposter-mode)\" / \"Reality check: you rated yourself higher than your score — this is where honesty pays.\"\n\nThat's a killer feature and matches the surprise mechanic + analytics theme. Let me build it.\n\n**Feature 2: Analytics system**\n\nRequirements:\n- Track per-question data: correct/incorrect, response time (ms), slide.\n- Current grades: per-chapter quiz scores (already have quizBest), overall average grade.\n- Average thinking time per slide/question.\n- Insights engine: rules-based \"gems\":\n  - If failing (< 70%): insights on what to improve — e.g., \"You're scoring 45% in this chapter. Review slides X-Y — your weakest areas are risk management calculations. Retake after review.\"\n  - If passing: \"You're at 90%. To stay sharp, ...\" — e.g., maintain streak, move faster without losing accuracy.\n  - Per-question weakness detection: which questions wrong → group by topic (hard without topic tags; but I can tag questions in data.js with a `tag` field! I already have tags-ish for chapters. Adding a `tag` per question enables \"weakest topic\" insights. That's valuable and I can add tags to ch1/ch2 quiz data).\n  - Response time insight: if avg time is very fast with low accuracy → \"rushing\"; if slow with high accuracy → \"deliberate\"; if slow with low accuracy → needs review.\n\nWhere to surface:\n- A new **\"Progress / Insights\" view** or enhance the Dashboard with an analytics panel. The user said \"showing them their current grades, their average thinking time per slide, average response time\". Let me build a **Performance view** (#/progress) with:\n  - Overall grade (avg quiz score)\n  - Per-chapter grades list with bars\n  - Average response time per question (overall + per chapter)\n  - Weakest topics (from question tags)\n  - Insight gems panel (dynamic, rule-based)\n  - A streak/consistency readout\n\nData model additions:\n- Track response time: in the quiz answer handler, measure time between question display and answer click. The quizCard renders when the slide is drawn — record `session.qShownAt = Date.now()` when a quiz slide is drawn; on answer, `timeMs = Date.now() - session.qShownAt`.\n- Store in state: `S.quizLog = { \"ch\": { q: { correct: bool, ms: number, tag: string } } }`? Or a flat log: `S.log = [{ ch, slide, tag, correct, ms, ts }]`. A flat log array is simpler for aggregation and grows fine locally. Let me store `S.log` array of per-answer records. Also `S.quizBest` per chapter already exists.\n\nWait — but the analytics need to survive: store records with chapter id, question index, tag, correct, ms. Then aggregate:\n- grades: per chapter = best score (already have) or latest? \"current grades\" → use latest attempt score? We have quizBest (max). For a student portal, show latest score per chapter + best. Let me track `st.lastScore` too (already computed at finish; I can store). Actually simpler: derive per-chapter score from the log? The log has per-question correct flags — but only first-attempt answers are logged per session... retakes add more records. Aggregating \"last attempt score\" from log is messy (need attempt grouping). Easier: store `st.lastScore` at finishChapter, plus quizBest. Grades view uses lastScore (or quizBest).\n- avg response time: mean of ms across logged answers.\n- weakest topics: group wrong answers by tag; topics with most wrongs. If no tags → fall back to question text prefix or \"Chapter X questions\".\n\nInsights engine (rule-based, deterministic):\n```\nfunction buildInsights() {\n  const log = S.log || [];\n  const chGrades = CHAPTERS.filter(c => c.quiz && chState(c.id).lastScore !== null).map(...);\n  insights = [];\n  // 1. Overall grade\n  avgGrade = mean(lastScore)\n  if avgGrade >= 85 → \"Exceptional — you're operating at institution level. Keep the discipline: consistency beats intensity.\"\n  else if >= 70 → \"You're passing and building. Push for 85%+ by reviewing the explanations you got wrong.\"\n  else → \"You're in the building phase. Review the chapters below 70% and retake — every retake is reps.\"\n  // 2. Failing chapters\n  for chapters with lastScore < 70 → \"Chapter X at Y% — re-read the lesson and retake the quiz. Weakest: tag1, tag2\"\n  // 3. Passing chapters\n  for chapters >= 85 → \"Chapter X at Y% — excellent. Your edge: <tag>.\"\n  // 4. Response time\n  avgMs; if avgMs < 4000 && avgGrade < 70 → \"You're answering fast but missing — slow down and read the question twice.\"\n  if avgMs > 12000 && avgGrade >= 70 → \"Deliberate and accurate. As you grow, speed will come — keep the process.\"\n  // 5. Calibration (self-assessment vs actual) — from finish flow, store last self-assess + score diff? Could add: st.lastPoll + st.lastScore → \"You rated yourself X but scored Y.\"\n  // 6. Streak/consistency\n}\n```\n\nThe \"gems\" language: I'll present insights as cards with a 💎 or gold icon, labeled \"Insight gems\".\n\nAlso the user said \"average thinking them per slide\" — likely \"average thinking time per slide\" i.e., time spent per slide. I can track slide view duration too: when a slide is drawn, record shownAt; when navigating away, add to S.slideTime[ch][n]. Then \"avg time per slide\". Nice — but keep scope reasonable: track per-question response time (primary) and optionally per-slide view time. Let me do both — slide dwell time is easy: in drawSlide, on re-render or navigation, compute dwell = now - slideShownAt, accumulate. I'll store S.dwell = { \"ch:n\": totalMs, count } or simpler S.dwellLog = [{ch, n, ms}]. Aggregate avg per chapter.\n\nHmm — scope. The analytics view + tracking + insights is a decent chunk. Let me design it cleanly:\n\nState additions:\n- defaultState: add `log: [], dwell: []` (arrays of records)\n- On quiz answer: push {ch, qi, tag, correct, ms} to log\n- On slide navigation (drawSlide called with new slide — detect change): push {ch, n, ms} to dwell (only if > 300ms, to avoid trivial)\n\nNew view: `#/progress` (nav item \"Performance\" with an icon — replace one of the SOON items? The nav has Journal (SOON), Laboratory (active), AI Mentor (SOON). Add \"Performance\" between Journey and Certificate. Icon: 📊 → use ICONS (I have flame, trophy...). Add a \"chart\" icon. Nav: <a data-route=\"#/progress\">📊 Performance</a>.\n\nrenderProgress(viewEl): \n- header \"Your Performance\"\n- Overall grade card (avg of lastScore across quiz chapters; big % ring or number)\n- Avg response time card + avg slide time card\n- Per-chapter grade bars (for chapters with scores; others \"not attempted\")\n- Weakest topics card (from wrong log by tag)\n- Insight gems list (rule engine)\n- Calibration note\n\nFinish flow changes (feature 1):\n- Hide score until poll answered\n- After poll click: reveal score with animation + calibration insight\n- Then show Claim/Retake buttons\n\nLet me restructure finishChapter:\n```js\nfunction finishChapter(stage) {\n  const ch = session.ch, st = chState(ch.id);\n  const n = ch.slides;\n  let html = `<div class=\"finish-card\">...`;\n  if (ch.quiz) {\n    const total = ch.quiz.length;\n    const score = Math.round(session.firstCorrect / total * 100);\n    const passed = score >= PASS_PCT;\n    st.lastScore = score;  // store for analytics\n    st.quizBest = Math.max(st.quizBest || 0, score);\n    if (passed) { st.passed = true; addXp(XP_QUIZ_PASS); }\n    html += `\n      <div class=\"score-hidden\" id=\"scoreHidden\">\n        <div class=\"finish-ic\">🎯</div>  // or trophy svg\n        <h2>How did you perform?</h2>\n        <p class=\"finish-sub\">Rate yourself first — then we reveal your real score. No pressure, traders reflect honestly.</p>\n        ${pollOptions}\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"score-reveal\" id=\"scoreReveal\" hidden>\n        <div class=\"score-flip\">... score display ...</div>\n        ${calibration line}\n        ${pass/fail line}\n        <button claim/retake>\n      </div>`;\n  }\n}\n```\n\nFlow: student clicks poll opt → (existing delegated poll handler saves st.poll) → NEW: after poll selection on the finish screen, reveal score: show #scoreReveal (hide #scoreHidden), animate score counting up or flipping, show calibration insight comparing poll range vs actual, and the buttons.\n\nBut the current delegated poll handler is generic (saves st.poll). I need to detect we're on the finish screen and trigger the reveal. Let me add to the poll handler: after saving, check if the clicked poll is within a .finish-card (closest('.finish-card')) → call revealScore(st).\n\nrevealScore: \n- compute score (from session.firstCorrect — still available since session persists until navigation)\n- actually st.lastScore was stored; use that.\n- Build reveal markup: big score %, pass/fail badge, calibration insight (compare poll pct range vs score), claim/retake button.\n- Animate: use CSS animation (scale/opacity) — \"smooth and premium\". A count-up animation via JS (requestAnimationFrame) is classy.\n\nThe existing code then has the fin button logic at the end — restructure so the buttons are inside the reveal block. The current code appends `pollBlock(st)` + button after the score line. I'll rewrite finishChapter entirely for quiz chapters and non-quiz chapters.\n\nNon-quiz chapters: keep simple (slides explored + continue button), maybe with the poll too but no score to reveal — keep as-is (poll + continue). Fine — keep existing behavior for non-quiz chapters.\n\nCalibration logic:\n- poll options pct ranges: [\"80–100\",\"60–79\",\"40–59\",\"20–39\",\"0–19\"] (POLL_OPTIONS in data.js: pct values)\n- Parse the chosen range: min,max. Compare with actual score.\n- If actual >= min and <= max → \"Your self-perception and your score are aligned. That's a rare and powerful discipline.\"\n- If actual > max → \"You scored higher than you rated yourself — you're harder on yourself than reality. Confidence is earned; this is evidence.\"\n- If actual < min → \"Reality check: you rated yourself {range} but scored {score}%. This gap is exactly where honesty pays. Review and retake — the market won't negotiate.\"\n\nThese are the \"insights/gems\" the user wants — inclusive of both failing and passing students.\n\nNow, also add `tag` fields to quiz questions for ch1 + ch2 so the weakest-topic insight works. Ch1 tags: \"Trading Styles\", \"Market Basics\", \"Sentiment\". Ch2 tags: \"Terminology\", \"Timeframes\", \"Calculations\", \"Lots & Leverage\", \"Pairs\", \"Analysis\". Let me add tags to each question. That's an edit to data.js (10 + 33 questions). Manageable but token-heavy. Alternative: derive tags from chapter-level \"focus\"? For ch1, all questions are \"Market Basics/Styles\". Simpler: add tags only where meaningful — I'll add a `tag` to each question. Actually to keep the diff smaller, I could add tags via a separate mapping in the analytics code? No — cleanest is per-question tag. Let me do it but keep tags short. Actually — token budget: 43 questions × adding `tag: \"...\"` — that's fine, ~43 small edits but via str_replace it's one big replacement of both quiz arrays... that's a large replacement block. Hmm. \n\nAlternative: skip per-question tags for now; derive \"weakest area\" from wrong-answer question TEXT (first ~5 words) — ugly. Or tag at the SLIDE level via quizSlides ranges? Ch1 quiz covers slides 12-21 — could group slides 12-14 (basics) etc. Simplest robust approach that adds real value without massive data edits: tag = the chapter's `focus` for all questions, and for weakness insights use per-question grouping by a keyword heuristic? Meh.\n\nLet me reconsider: I can add tags compactly by appending a `tags` map in data.js SEPARATE from the quiz arrays:\n```js\nconst QUIZ_TAGS = {\n  1: { 0: \"Trading styles\", 1: \"Sentiment\", 2: \"Market basics\", ... },  // chId: {qi: tag}\n  2: { ... }\n};\n```\nStill requires listing 43 tags. Same size. \n\nOK let me just add tags inline to the questions — but to save tokens, I'll write tags only for ch1 (10 Qs) fully, and for ch2 do tags too since it's the only other live chapter. Actually — I'll add a compact `tag` to each question inline. Let me estimate: ch1 quiz + ch2 quiz replacement = ~43 lines with tag added. It's fine; str_replace with the full quiz arrays replaced is one call but huge. Alternatively many small str_replace calls (one per question — 43 calls, too many). \n\nCompromise: add tags via a compact lookup object at the end of data.js