y keep the existing options (they're the user's real quiz content) but map all of them to a style profile. Actually — the cleanest approach that respects the existing content AND gives a rich adaptive system:\n\n1. Create `STYLES` in data.js keyed by normalized style names. Map the 4 poll options → 4 style profiles:\n   - \"Technical Trader\" → tech profile\n   - \"Fundamental Trader\" → fundamental profile\n   - \"Swing Trader\" → swing profile\n   - \"Scalper\" → scalp profile\n\nHmm, but the user's vision is around scalp vs day vs swing vs position. The poll options are the user's real content though. I could update the poll options to the four timeframe styles the user described. But the poll is part of the user's real quiz/course content (their Chapter 1). Changing \"Technical Trader\"/\"Fundamental Trader\" to \"Day Trader\"/\"Position Trader\" — the user explicitly said \"swing, scalp, day and position trading i didn't know which one worked well for me\". The user's own words show they think in terms of those four styles. And the slides cover Day Traders & Position Traders as separate. So updating the poll options to: Scalper, Day Trader, Swing Trader, Position Trader aligns perfectly with the course content AND the user's vision. Actually wait — should I change their real quiz content? The slide 7 card covers \"Day Traders & Position Traders\" and slide 6 covers \"Swing\". The poll currently asks \"What kind of trader do you want to be?\" with 4 options mixing approaches. The user said the badge tells them what style they chose. The four timeframe styles are cleaner for tailoring: each has distinct lesson implications.\n\nI'll update the poll options to the four timeframe styles: \"Scalper\", \"Day Trader\", \"Swing Trader\", \"Position Trader\". This matches the user's mental model, matches the course content, and gives us clean adaptive buckets. But wait — the stored traderStyle from existing users (e.g., \"Swing Trader\" from my test) — the dashboard chip