Swing, Scalper). Changing two (Technical→Day, Fundamental→Position) — actually Day and Position are covered in the course content (slide 7). This is a reasonable, defensible improvement aligned with their vision. I'll do it.\n\nNow the feature set:\n\n**1. STYLES data (data.js)** — 4 profiles, each with:\n- `key` (scalper, day, swing, position)\n- `name` (\"Scalper\", ...)\n- `icon` (SVG path for the style)\n- `tagline` — one-liner\n- `profile` — 2-3 sentences on who this trader is\n- `edge` — what makes this style work (strengths)\n- `watch` — the traps/pitfalls to watch for this style\n- `chapters` — per-style chapter emphasis: e.g., scalper → { 4: \"Candlesticks\", 5: \"Market Movement\", 9: \"Market Orders\", 10: \"Indicators\" } with reasons. This powers the \"Your path\" recommendations.\n- `timeframe` — the TF they trade\n\n**2. Style-aware lesson cards** — native cards get optional `styles: { scalper: \"…\", day: \"…\", swing: \"…\", position: \"…\" }` (or a `styleNote` object). When the student has a style and the card defines a matching entry, render a gold \"Tailored for you\" panel: `${ICONS.sparkle} For {style}s — {text}`. Also `insight` can be per-style: if `styles` present, show style insight instead of generic.\n\nAdd `styles` to ch1 cards where meaningful:\n- Slide 4 \"The Traders You'll Meet\" (noise traders/market timers): style-specific — scalpers battle noise intraday; position traders ignore noise.\n- Slide 6 \"Momentum, Technical, Fundamental, Swing\": per-style note.\n- Slide 7 \"Day Traders & Position Traders\": per-style note.\n- Slide 8 poll.\n- Slide 9 \"Keep an Open Mind\": per-style guidance.\n- Slide 10 \"Mid-Chapter Reflection\": per-style question.\n\n**3. Dashboard style upgrade** — the style-chip becomes a full style card: style name + tagline + \"View your path\" button + \"Change style\" link. If no style chosen, a card prompting \"What kind of trader are you? Find out in Chapter 1 →\".\n\n**4. New \"Your Path\" view (#/path)** — the adaptive hub:\n- Profile: \"You chose: Swing Trader\" + profile text\n- Edge & Watch columns (strengths/traps)\n- \"Your personalized focus\" — recommended chapters with reasons (chapterGuide)\n- \"Why the OS tailors for you\" explanation\n- \"Change your style\" — a selector with the 4 styles, updates S.traderStyle and re-renders (this is how students can change their path as they evolve)\n- Maybe note that lessons adapt: \"Slides marked with the gold 'Tailored for you' panel show your style's lens.\"\n\n**5. Nav item** — add \"Your Path\" to sidebar (data-route=\"#/path\").\n\n**6. Insight gem tie-in** — buildInsights adds a style-aware gem when traderStyle set: e.g., \"As a Swing Trader: your patience is your edge — Chapter 5's market movement matters more than minute-by-minute noise.\"\n\n**7. Mobile-friendly** — reuse existing responsive styles.\n\nCSS: add `.style-card`, `.style-panel` (lesson card panel), `.path-*` styles, `.style-picker` etc. Keep the gold/dark language.\n\nAlso the poll on slide 8 — after choosing, feedback text says \"Saved — this identity now shows on your dashboard.\" Fine. Maybe add \"and tailors your lessons.\"\n\nAlso the slide 8 poll options change → the stored S.traderStyle strings change. STYLES keys: scalper, day, swing, position. Normalization: lowercased, strip \" trader\".\n\nEdge: `S.traderStyle` could be \"Swing Trader\" → key \"swing\". \"Scalper\" → \"scalper\". \"Day Trader\" → \"day\". \"Position Trader\" → \"position\". Old stored \"Technical Trader\"/\"Fundamental Trader\" → no profile → fallback: a generic STYLES.general? I'll add a `STYLES.general` fallback profile used when the stored style has no profile, so nothing breaks. Also handle in styleCard with graceful \"—\".\n\nLet me also think: should the style panels on lesson cards be a different mechanism? The `nativeCard` function renders from `native` object. I'll add `styles` (object keyed by style key with `{ note: \"...\", insight: \"...\" }` or just strings). Simplest: `styles: { swing: \"Swing edge: …\", scalper: \"…\" }` where the value replaces/enhances. I'll render: if student has style and `native.styles?.[key]` exists → render `<div class=\"native-style\"><span class=\"ns-badge\">${ICONS.sparkle} Tailored for you</span><p>${text}</p></div>`. If `native.stylesInsight?.[key]` — overkill. Keep it: `styles` replaces the insight when present? No — show both: the tailored panel is distinct from the generic insight. Actually to reduce clutter: if a per-style note exists, show the tailored panel INSTEAD of generic insight? The user wants tailored content. I'll show the tailored panel in addition, and ALSO support per-style insight via `styles` object with `{ note, insight }`. Let me define:\n\n```js\nstyles: {\n  swing: { note: \"...\", insight: \"...\" },\n  scalper: { note: \"...\" }\n}\n```\n\nRender: tailored panel with note; if insight present, show insight gem inside the panel. Clean.\n\nLet me now write the STYLES content. This is the \"crazy intelligence\" part — write genuinely expert, per-style content:\n\n**Scalper:**\n- profile: \"Scalping is sprinting: trades measured in seconds to minutes, dozens of entries a day, each one fighting for a few pips. You live on the smallest timeframes — 1m to 5m — where liquidity, spread and execution speed matter more than any indicator.\"\n- edge: [\"Reads order flow and tape faster than anyone\", \"Compounds small wins without waiting for 'the big move'\", \"Learns discipline in volume — 20 decisions a day is 20 reps a day\"]\n- watch: [\"Spread + commission are your first costs — they can silently eat 30% of a scalp edge\", \"Overtrading when the market is quiet — no volatility, no scalp\", \"Emotional fatigue: the 10th trade of the day is rarely as clean as the 1st\"]\n- chapters (priority): 4 (Candlesticks — instant reads), 5 (Market Movement — structure on small TF), 9 (Market Orders — execution precision), 10 (Technical Indicators — momentum/volatility tools)\n- timeframe: \"1m – 5m charts, seconds-to-minutes holds\"\n- tagline: \"Seconds to minutes. Speed is the edge.\"\n\n**Day Trader:**\n- profile: \"Day trading is a daily campaign: you form a bias at the open, trade it through the session, and close everything flat before the close. No overnight risk, no gaps — your P&L is decided inside one day.\"\n- edge: [\"Takes advantage of daily volatility without overnight risk\", \"Trades with a clear time-box — every day is a fresh start\", \"Leverages intraday liquidity at the open and close\"]\n- watch: [\"The open is emotional — a pre-market bias without a plan becomes a gamble\", \"Chasing midday chop: quiet hours produce the worst fills\", \"Closing losers 'just one more hour' — the overnight rule exists for a reason\"]\n- chapters: 4, 5, 9, 7 (risk is your daily survival), 6\n- tagline: \"One day, one plan. Flat before the close.\"\n\n**Swing Trader:**\n- profile: \"Swing trading is chess, not sprinting: positions held for days to weeks, riding the structure of higher timeframes — 1H, 4H, daily. You let the market come to your levels, and your patience is your edge.\"\n- edge: [\"Trades with the daily trend instead of fighting intraday noise\", \"Needs only a few good setups a month — quality over frequency\", \"Fills are forgiving: you don't pay for the exact tick\"]\n- watch: [\"Overnight and weekend gaps can punish a perfect setup\", \"Ignoring the daily trend to catch a 'cheap' retracement\", \"Position size creeping up on 'sure things' — the monthly loss is the one that matters\"]\n- chapters: 3 (Fundamental Analysis — swing's fuel), 5 (Market Movement), 11 (Market Cycle), 8 (Pairs), 7\n- tagline: \"Days to weeks. Let the market come to you.\"\n\n**Position Trader:**\n- profile: \"Position trading is the long game: positions held for weeks, months, even years, built on fundamental themes rather than chart noise. You think in quarters, not candles — and your account compounds on conviction.\"\n- edge: [\"Profits from the